No. Okay, thank you very much.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.290
The next agenda item is to hear

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.410
from different board committees.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.243
We're going to start

00:02:29.243 --> 00:02:31.290
with the People and
Remuneration Committee,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.770
and, Jane, would you like to
give us a high-level summary

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.590
recognising that we've got
the papers here already.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.080
- I mean very little to
add, chair, to the summary.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.810
I mean two good meetings recently.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:44.430
I mean continued focus

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.970
on increasing the non-executive

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.800
and chair pipeline,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.050
recognising the importance
of good leadership

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.090
throughout the service.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.070
So there continues to be
good work going on that.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.210
We were obviously looking

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:02.210
at some of the work being
done on performance,

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.030
recognising it's a time of
some disruption obviously

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.490
with the changes going ahead,

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.190
but the team is looking at adapting

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.720
some of that performance training

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.100
to be more suitable

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.260
for the kind of circumstances
we're going into.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:23.040
And so no let up on
trying to continued focus

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.475
on management excellence there

00:03:26.475 --> 00:03:29.310
and, in particular, sort of establishing

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:34.020
minimum expected standards
around all our line managers.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.530
The rest I think, chair, is in the notes.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.903
- [Penny] Okay. Any questions for Jane?

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.500
Thanks. No, okay.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.580
Thank you very much.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.320
Jeremy, Audit and Risk.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.850
- Thanks, again. I'll be brief.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.610
We had a good discussion
about the risk registers.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:51.443
After the meeting actually,

00:03:51.443 --> 00:03:53.040
we also had a conversation with Penny,

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.010
getting her input into the risk registers.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.110
They're on the agenda for
later on in the meeting,

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.230
so I won't go into more detail on that.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:05.490
I think, alongside the risks
that we've been covering

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:09.600
over the last few years
within the NHS England,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.670
I think additionally we're now looking

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.920
to look around the risks of transition,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.880
and as we transform to the
new organisational structures,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.020
we need to keep an eye on that,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.250
but we also need to keep an eye,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.020
from an audit and risk point of view,

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:26.100
on the risk that that may
have a negative impact

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.730
on control and the way we
manage the organisation.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.370
So that's particularly high on
the agenda for the committee,

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.560
and I think, with Elizabeth,
we'll be working with Deloitte

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.360
to look at how we try and manage that

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.193
through the transition process

00:04:37.193 --> 00:04:39.960
so that we both manage the
risk around the transition

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.060
but we manage the impact that
the transition might have

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:42.990
on risk and control.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.790
So that's high on the agenda.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.710
We're also talking very
closely to the department

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.570
about how we can work
closely together on that

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:49.920
to make sure we don't duplicate,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.618
and move towards the new
structures over time.

00:04:52.618 --> 00:04:54.723
- Okay, great. Any questions?

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.323
Okay, thank you. Robert.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.100
- Yeah, so we had a
Quality Committee meeting,

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.030
I think almost the day
before the chair's review

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:10.110
on safety and quality
rationalisation came out.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:12.870
So I think the backdrop to this

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.400
is that people will be aware

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.200
that there has been
something for some years now

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.000
called the National Quality Board

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.310
as well as the NHSE Quality Committee,

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.250
and Penny and I have been in discussion

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.790
about how best to manage
things moving forward,

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.020
and I think the likelihood is

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.760
that one will integrate into the other.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.040
So that's ongoing work.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.380
At the same time, there's
other pieces of alignment

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.180
which are crucial.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.833
Obviously the 10 Year Plan.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.790
There's a recognition of
risk around the migration,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.410
all the migration that's necessary

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:44.550
to deliver the 10 Year Plan,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.190
and so the next Quality
Committee which will go ahead,

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.170
will focus on that.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.870
The Quality strategy is under development,

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.150
and, again, that will be
co-owned at the moment

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.060
by the National Quality Board
and the Quality Committee.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.000
And then there's reference to
the Integrated Quality Report

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.400
which is a sort of high-level dashboard

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.440
which, again, we're seeking to align

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.960
with the National Performance
Assessment Framework.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.460
So, work going on,

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.440
but I think the future is
beginning to take shape

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.350
in terms of how this
will best be structured.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:17.970
One final comment is that we
have three very good people

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.400
as patient voices on the Quality Committee

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.050
who contribute very constructively,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:25.440
and we're thinking about how
best to get the patient voice

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:26.910
onto the National Quality Board,

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.560
and that's an ongoing discussion.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.760
- Thank you. Any questions?

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.353
Nope. Okay, Mark.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.520
- I suppose I'll take the report as read.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.470
There are a couple of
themes that come out of it,

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:44.760
one across the app,

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.450
and the review of
electronic patient records,

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:52.980
which is the price of fragmentation

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.200
and the need for a consistent approach

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.300
to really get best practise.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.360
There are clearly a lot
of lessons to be learned

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.570
in a way of really accelerating
technology into the system

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:04.870
and making the most of it.

00:07:06.594 --> 00:07:09.480
There's an ongoing huge
amount of work on cyber risk.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:10.920
This isn't an NHS issue.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.460
It's across the entire economy

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.900
that we are going to have to face into now

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.193
and going forward.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.730
And then a long discussion

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.400
about what the best operating model is

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.280
because clearly we need local delivery

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:27.903
where the knowledge is highest.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.240
We need consistent standards

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.590
and therefore what is both
the structure and the approach

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.393
to really deliver business change.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:38.603
- Okay.

00:07:39.994 --> 00:07:40.827
Be good just to add something

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:44.250
about how you're working
with DHSC colleagues

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.490
to think through how we
have a safe transition

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.590
of our digital and data systems.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:53.943
- Yeah, so we're meeting the
week after next with the DHSC.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:59.880
Clearly we run a large number
of critical national systems

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.730
as well as providing guidance
for the system itself

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.650
that I'm working very
closely with Vin and Ming,

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:08.913
the interim CDIO.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.450
I think we're getting to...

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:15.690
The options started off
a wide range of options.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.140
We're getting narrowing
down to a sensible group.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:22.140
I would hope, in the
next three to four weeks,

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.990
we'll have a clear view
of which way we go.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.253
- Okay, thank you. Okay, good.

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:32.876
Anything else anyone wants
to raise at this stage?

00:08:32.876 --> 00:08:33.923
Okay, thank you.

00:08:33.923 --> 00:08:36.240
So, Jim, over to you for your update.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.073
- Thanks, Penny.

00:08:37.073 --> 00:08:38.160
So quick canter through

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:39.930
since, well last time we met,

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.790
we talked a bit about the 100-day plan

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:45.666
that's governing our exec
activities as agreed with DHSC,

00:08:45.666 --> 00:08:48.390
and other colleagues would,
you know, pleased to say,

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:51.720
we've managed to deliver pretty
much all of what was set out

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:52.560
in that plan.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.460
So stuff in there about the
ICB changes, the model ICB,

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:01.239
the Oversight Framework,
segmentation, transparency,

00:09:01.239 --> 00:09:05.070
the UEC delivery plan, SR contribution,

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:06.420
and also the 10 Year Plan.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.970
So a huge amount of
work done by colleagues

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:10.350
but with support of the service.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:12.240
I'll come back to the model ICB

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.830
because I think that's a
really material thing for us.

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.090
We clearly had the 10 Year Plan published.

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.610
It seems to have had a
very, very good response

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.410
in pretty much every quarter.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.933
Certainly in the
services, huge enthusiasm.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.170
With the public, with
stakeholders as well,

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.360
very, very strong response.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.360
We had a chief exec session last Thursday

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.580
aimed at delivery of the plan.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.670
So now really very much
in a delivery phase

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:42.150
using summer to get all
the mechanics in place,

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.730
all the hard work that's
going to be required

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:47.790
about shape and IHOs, the tariff reform,

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:49.560
the allocation processes to lead

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.020
in a medium term planning in the autumn

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.750
which is still very committed to kickoff

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:58.470
as quickly as we can, late
September, early October.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.380
Given the model, ICB
work was very successful,

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:03.480
and we're trying to change how we work

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:04.740
with colleagues in the NHS.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.810
We're going to build that out.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:09.150
And we've had a lot of
volunteers from last week

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:11.820
to work with us on the
development of IHOs,

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:15.570
the new FT pipeline, models
for neighbourhood care,

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:16.560
you know, et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.680
So real enthusiasm there,

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.050
and I think we've shown the
benefit and feel the benefit

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:26.160
of doing that jointly with
colleagues in the service.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:26.993
As we go through the agenda,

00:10:26.993 --> 00:10:29.700
you can see probably still
a bit of a mixed picture

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:31.593
on performance overall,

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.600
but it is worth noting

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.660
what happened to the elective waiting list

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.970
with two months of reduction

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:43.970
against seasonal cycles for
the first time in 17 years.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.050
And Mark and the team
are going really hard

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:47.310
at just trying to keep that going.

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.500
It's always hard in summer months.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.510
The autumn is much more productive,

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.310
so is key for us.

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:55.200
I'll come back to that
because it's material

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.927
when we talk about industrial action.

00:10:57.927 --> 00:10:59.130
And colleagues will have seen,

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.710
we're starting to again
build our transparency,

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:05.130
more data being published
and made available.

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:06.690
There's another step a
couple of weeks' time

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.900
is to start sharing more
routine performance data

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.310
with NHS colleagues.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:13.080
So currently that's quite hard to find.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.510
You've got to have people who
will dig around for it all.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.251
So we'll be dropping this
in to colleagues inboxes

00:11:18.251 --> 00:11:20.670
in the next couple of weeks.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:24.180
We are, sadly though, in an awkward place,

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.870
a regrettable place,
around industrial action.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:31.590
And I think, first of all,
let's accept and appreciate

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:33.420
everybody has the right to strike

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.610
and take industrial action,

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:40.200
but this comes at a time
when we're all cognizant

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.400
of where society is with us,
British Social Attitude Survey.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.050
What the service is trying to do

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:47.910
in terms of step up for the public

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.110
and improving performance,

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:51.930
improving satisfaction and outcomes,

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.780
and also the need to
keep everybody together

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.720
and avoid divisiveness

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.350
that industrial action inevitably causes.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:05.070
We are very hopeful still
that it can be avoided,

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.290
but, in the circumstance
where it can't be,

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:09.090
we've really stepped
up, through Sarah-Jane,

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:10.797
our preparation for that.

00:12:10.797 --> 00:12:14.220
And as part of that we
should be clear that,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:17.760
given the learning from the
last period of industrial action

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.830
and papers that came here in the autumn

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.050
and various other reports that we've seen

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:24.720
around harm and the impact,

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.660
that we can't take a tight definition

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:32.660
around service provision
that was taken the last time.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.760
So we'll be taking a
much different approach

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.423
in stepping up our activities
with NHS leadership tomorrow,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:44.580
encouraging them to be
clearer about safety,

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:48.000
and that those responsibilities
and choices sit with them

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.070
and their management team.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:53.370
I would, you know, completely
reinforce or really hope

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:54.930
that this is avoidable

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.620
and that the relevant
people can have talks,

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:02.253
make progress over the next
few weeks, and avoid it.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.910
And then, just finally, to just draw out,

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.200
as we go through the report,

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.360
picking up on the kind of mixed picture,

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.814
there are some really
strong positives, you know,

00:13:11.814 --> 00:13:12.810
really ahead of the game

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:15.180
in terms of winter planning
relative to previous years.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:18.420
Sarah-Jane will guide us
through this later on.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:19.880
Financial performance so far

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.960
is so much better than it could have been,

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:23.850
led by Elizabeth and colleagues.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.710
Then also we've had a series of events

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:26.820
through professional groups,

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:31.320
chief execs a few times,
CFOs a few weeks ago.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.510
Duncan had chief nurses in a room

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.180
on Tuesday this week as well.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:39.930
We're trying to step up that
engagement, enlist support,

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:41.400
keep the community with us,

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:44.700
which I'm glad to say has
been really, really impressive

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.430
and very, very much appreciated.

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:48.270
So I'll stop there Penny,

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:50.490
and happy to take any
questions if there are any.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.417
- Okay, thank you, Jim. Andrew?

00:13:53.993 --> 00:13:57.780
- So, Jim, just reflecting
on previous years,

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.947
what was the cost of
industrial action for the NHS

00:14:00.947 --> 00:14:05.130
'cause clearly that's not
going to be funded centrally?

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:08.400
It'll come from our
resource space, won't it?

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:09.840
- Yeah.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.265
Elizabeth, do you want to answer that one?

00:14:11.265 --> 00:14:12.750
- Yeah, yeah. So happy to.

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:14.520
So we've looked back,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:15.990
and over the period,

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.965
'cause obviously it
was more than one year,

00:14:17.965 --> 00:14:20.070
it was about 36 days of industrial action

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.350
for just the juniors.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.140
It was estimated the direct cost alone

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.123
was 1 1/2 billion pounds.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:31.170
That's not included in
the lost productivity,

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:33.420
the fact that people will
be profile annual leave

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.123
and then et cetera, so
those indirect costs.

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:38.193
It's not including that.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:41.280
Obviously, when we've looked back as well,

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:44.910
I've looked at the
variability of the estimates

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:46.680
and some of those costs
that were coming through

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.340
by provider system and region,

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:55.260
and, for me, I think in the event

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:57.630
that we end up with
industrial action again,

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.663
we'd have to take quite a
different approach actually.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.000
There's been a lot of learning,

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.650
and I think there was
a lot of cost incurred

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:08.220
that we wouldn't
anticipate to happen again

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.140
in the way that it had originally.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:11.430
- Okay, and just reflecting

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.893
on the kind of safety issues, Jim,

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:18.630
again, the way we set it up last time,

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:20.673
it was the kind of national framework

00:15:20.673 --> 00:15:22.230
that people had to comply with,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:26.310
and I don't know how many kind
of incidents were reported

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:28.353
as a consequence of the strikes,

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:32.970
but I assume that we'll be
asking trust to do that again

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:36.210
where they believe that
safety has been compromised.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:37.047
- Absolutely, Andrew,

00:15:37.047 --> 00:15:39.000
and this is, I mean Sarah-Jane and others

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.950
will chip in as well,

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:42.150
it's really complicated

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:45.450
to be able to identify the harm and impact

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.580
directly to the industrial action,

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:50.310
and that whole Christmas Day service,

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.500
that was part of agreed process last time,

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:55.860
but we need to take a
different position this time.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:59.820
I personally met patients
and families in my trust life

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:02.460
who'd had late cancer diagnosis,

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:04.320
late Parkinson's diagnosis,

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.970
came to harm later on,

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:07.860
that were not caught

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:10.650
in that initial assessment and reporting,

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:13.320
and the amount of disruption
that ripples through,

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.120
it actually takes months for these things

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.320
to actually settle.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:17.640
So there is a danger.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.970
You look at very discrete periods of time

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:22.860
with very discrete range of services

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:25.530
but then miss the broader picture,

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:30.450
and it's impossible to
measure the avoided access

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:32.240
where there was a
hesitancy in the population

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:33.870
to come forward because they were worried

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.730
about what could actually be provided

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:38.970
or not wanting to put unnecessary
strain on the population.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:40.350
We'll never quantify that,

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:41.803
which is why I think we just need to take

00:16:41.803 --> 00:16:43.020
a very different approach.

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:48.020
We all tolerated levels
of harm and risk last time

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:51.240
that I really just don't
think we should anymore,

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.490
and it's going to be very,
very tricky to navigate.

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.920
But hopefully we'll avoid it.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:57.420
Hopefully it will be averted,

00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:58.530
and, if not, we'll have to work

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:00.810
in partnership with
colleagues to address it.

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:02.484
- [Andrew] Thank you.

00:17:02.484 --> 00:17:04.290
- So I've got questions
from Jane and from Robert.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:05.613
Let's take both questions.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:07.493
- Thanks, Penny.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:10.770
Obviously large numbers
of resident doctors

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:13.050
didn't vote to strike, if
you look at the numbers,

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:14.700
but I think, for many of them,

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.350
whether or not they did,

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:20.520
some of the non-financial
issues and sort of concerns

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:22.830
will be still for most in their mind,

00:17:22.830 --> 00:17:25.380
and perhaps can you just
give a kind of update

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:28.440
as to where we are with
following up on some of those

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:30.510
'cause I think a lot of
those are very material

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:32.550
to people's sort of working lives.

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:33.930
- That's a really good point actually.

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:36.930
So there are a range of things
that we've all known about

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:38.160
for quite a long time

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:40.980
that have caused unhappiness
with resident doctors

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:43.440
and were part of the package last time

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:46.800
around food and, you know, exam costs

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:48.450
and these sorts of things.

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:49.440
Hand on heart,

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:52.980
I don't think we've systematically
addressed those issues

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:54.690
with the urgency required

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.970
and as systematically as required,

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:58.427
and we need to pick up pace on that

00:17:58.427 --> 00:18:00.600
'cause it's a significant contributor

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:04.350
to their understandable dissatisfaction.

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.780
From my discussions with
the resident doctors,

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.640
and again, colleagues, chip in on this,

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:11.519
there is also massive
dissatisfaction with the system

00:18:11.519 --> 00:18:12.450
that they have to go through

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:15.370
to be appointed to find out

00:18:16.351 --> 00:18:17.400
where their rotations are,

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:21.030
being sent to places that are
inconvenient and difficult

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:23.340
for them and their families,
you know, et cetera.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.410
And they were understandable systems

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.979
built in the early 2000s.

00:18:27.979 --> 00:18:29.697
I really think we should
be challenging that

00:18:29.697 --> 00:18:32.100
and working with them
to reconstruct a system

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.920
that gives more local
ownership and belonging

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:36.990
and more sensitivity to their needs

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:39.990
but actually also trying
to reconnect colleagues

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:43.590
to the local community which they work in.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:46.123
So, for me, I would
extend the non-pay actions

00:18:46.123 --> 00:18:48.903
into that as well.

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:52.530
And then the financial
side's been well covered

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:53.863
through the media and other colleagues.

00:18:53.863 --> 00:18:56.010
I don't want to inflame that further,

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.980
but everybody knows there's no money,

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:00.750
there's no money in the economy.

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:03.090
We're not going to get any extra money.

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:04.260
So it's a zero-sum game.

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.510
Whatever goes into that deal

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:08.580
has to come from somewhere else,

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:11.310
which I think we're all wanting to avoid.

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:12.143
- Robert?

00:19:12.143 --> 00:19:14.313
- Yeah, I mean, so Jim's
just made the point

00:19:14.313 --> 00:19:15.146
I was going to make.

00:19:15.146 --> 00:19:18.180
I think getting the allocation
of training posts right

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:20.070
and the structure of training

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:22.380
so that the juniors or the residents

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.600
have a sense of belonging,
continuity of patient care,

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.667
being valued, and all that
stuff is so important,

00:19:26.667 --> 00:19:30.720
and I think it's underlying
a lot of the disgruntlement.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.160
And it's not only important

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:34.530
because it's important
for the existing trainees,

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:35.640
but if we don't get it right,

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:38.370
then we're breeding a bunch
of disgruntled juniors

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:40.440
that will become disgruntled
consultants and so and so on.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:42.600
So I think it's difficult to exaggerate

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:43.433
how important that is,

00:19:43.433 --> 00:19:45.990
and I just totally support
the work that's going on.

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:50.430
- So we'll pick up on metrics in a minute,

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:52.710
but it may be given we all recognise

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.180
this is an important area

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.890
'cause it's not just for resident docs,

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:56.723
it's for all staff.

00:19:56.723 --> 00:19:58.110
How do we look at wellbeing

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:00.900
and having a positive working environment?

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:03.450
I'm very conscious we don't
have that in our metrics.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:04.560
- No.

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:06.950
- And maybe we should look
at how could we capture that

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:08.250
on the basis of,

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:09.083
- Absolutely.

00:20:09.083 --> 00:20:11.853
- what gets measured gets
done sort of principle.

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:15.240
So I think just one thing
on industrial action.

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:16.073
So I know, Jim,

00:20:16.073 --> 00:20:21.073
you have very deliberately
cut short your holiday

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:26.220
to be here during the
strikes should they go ahead,

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:29.520
and I think we all might say a
big thank you for doing that.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:31.710
I know it comes at quite
considerable personal cost,

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.780
so thank you for doing that,

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:36.870
and hopefully we can avert it.

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:37.913
- Thanks, Penny.

00:20:37.913 --> 00:20:38.746
- But should that not happen,

00:20:38.746 --> 00:20:41.100
then it's good to know that you'll be here

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:43.620
as well as other colleagues obviously.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:48.510
Okay, so let's move on to
Performance and Delivery.

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:49.650
David.

00:20:49.650 --> 00:20:52.770
- Thanks, Penny. So
good morning, everybody.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:56.160
This is our Integrated
Operational Performance Report,

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.430
which, as you'll see, is
built into five key sections,

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:02.790
and I'm very conscious that
my colleagues around the table

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:04.560
are responsible for
each of those sections,

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:06.090
so I'm sure will be delighted

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:09.630
to take any detailed
questions on each of them.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:11.070
And Elizabeth, of course, will pick up

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:13.500
the finance detail afterwards.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:15.810
So, just from the outset,
maybe if I summarise

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.120
where I think performance is
in each of those sections.

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:20.460
Firstly to pay great credit again

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:21.930
to our hardworking NHS staff

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:24.690
who are continuing to deliver well

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.360
against a set of challenging
targets this year.

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:28.680
We know we're doing well,

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:31.170
as Jim has set out in
a number of key areas,

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:33.630
but particularly with
urgent emergency care

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:34.463
and elective waits,

00:21:34.463 --> 00:21:37.485
we know we have further to
go to achieve the ambitions

00:21:37.485 --> 00:21:40.230
that we have set out in the 10 Year Plan.

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:41.063
Just to start with,

00:21:41.063 --> 00:21:43.020
if we pick up quality and safety,

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:45.930
you'll see that this is
one of the first times

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:48.390
we've tried to focus on as
much transparency as possible.

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:50.520
There will be more to come

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:53.667
as the quality board that
Penny's outlined before is set up

00:21:53.667 --> 00:21:57.330
and the details are flowing
through this report,

00:21:57.330 --> 00:21:59.730
but we want, we absolutely
have an ambition

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:03.450
to make the NHS the most
transparent healthcare organisation

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:04.283
in the world,

00:22:04.283 --> 00:22:06.480
and this is our starting beginning.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:09.360
We are looking to publish
league tables relatively soon

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:12.600
to rank our providers against
these key quality metrics,

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:13.830
and we're very, very keen.

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:15.630
We've made good progress
in giving patients

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:18.540
real access to transparency,
of course, through the app

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:20.130
and more of that to follow.

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:22.407
Just in terms of quality
and patient safety,

00:22:22.407 --> 00:22:23.240
you will see from the report

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:27.330
that the Summary Hospital-Level
Mortality Indicator,

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:29.310
or SHMI as we know it,

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:31.560
is at its lowest level
for the last two years.

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:34.680
You'll also see that
from a CQC perspective

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:35.700
we have seen an increase

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:37.650
in the amount of good
and outstanding results.

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:40.290
But, again, in the spirit of transparency,

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:41.123
we have to be clear

00:22:41.123 --> 00:22:42.810
that there are less
inspections at the moment,

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:44.490
and they are risk-based at the moment

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:47.043
which will be skewing those numbers.

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:50.550
In terms of urgent and emergency care,

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:52.200
Sarah-Jane is coming on later

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.590
to talk about the fantastic
work underway to plan for winter

00:22:55.590 --> 00:22:58.110
which we know will be
challenging yet again,

00:22:58.110 --> 00:22:59.970
but it's really important to set out

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:03.480
that we continue to see
huge demand on our services.

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:06.270
June, depends on how you
feel about the hot weather,

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:10.320
but certainly almost half the
days we faced a heat challenge

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:13.530
which put a heath alert,
health alert, sorry,

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.080
which put a tremendous
challenge on our services.

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:18.210
Yet, despite that, we're
continuing to operate

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:20.790
at around nearly 76%.

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:23.850
So we have a lot of work to do,

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.220
but you can see also from
the ambulance performance

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:27.420
that good work is underway,

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:30.600
particularly around handover and flow.

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.570
So our focus is on today,
but our focus is on tomorrow,

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.010
and Sarah-Jane, I know, will pick that up

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:37.560
when we come on to talk about winter.

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:39.060
In terms of elective care,

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:40.380
I guess the headline would be

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:42.060
that patients are still waiting too long,

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.070
but we are making good progress,

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:47.010
particularly focused
around our long waiters

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:51.300
where you'll see us moving
towards constitutional standard.

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:53.040
We've seen a record number of patients.

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:54.750
Just to give you an example, in May alone,

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:59.100
we saw 75,000 planned
treatments per day in May,

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.760
the highest number on record

00:24:01.979 --> 00:24:02.970
and certainly somewhere ahead

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:05.400
of where we were in pre-pandemic levels.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:07.890
So people are working incredibly hard

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:09.570
to get to where we want to be,

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:12.540
both in terms of treatment
and in terms of diagnostics,

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:15.093
and you can see that
from the figures set out.

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.770
We are working hard on the
longest-waiting patients,

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.620
particularly the 52-weekers
and the longest waiters,

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:23.820
and the balance at the moment

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.320
for our colleagues in provider land

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:27.750
is how we deal with what's known as

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:29.580
dealing with those
longer waits tailgunning

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:32.580
but also reducing the
larger picture of RTT.

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:34.710
And you can see that we are on track,

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:36.480
and our ambition still remains, of course,

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.543
to hit the 65% target that's been set.

00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:41.820
The next section is cancer,

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.820
where I'm pleased to say we
are treating more patients,

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:46.890
and we are treating them faster.

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:48.690
You'll see this relative success

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:53.040
in the faster diagnostic
standard and the 62-day standard.

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:56.220
We have, quite rightly, set
our ambitions further this year

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:57.420
and as we move forward,

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:58.680
and that will be a challenge,

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:00.000
and we're not where we want to be

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.100
on that ambitious standard,

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:04.380
but I think we should pay
great credit to the work

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:05.790
of those providing cancer care

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.190
that we are making such
significant progress

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:09.783
in a number of key areas.

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:14.400
Very importantly, mental
health continues to be

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:18.150
a key objective and improvement
in mental health services,

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:22.500
particularly around child and
young adults' access and flow

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:24.450
in and around our mental health providers.

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:26.490
And you'll see from
the statistics provided

00:25:26.490 --> 00:25:29.040
that, again, more
children and young people

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:32.520
are accessing mental health
services than ever before.

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:35.370
The percentage of
outpatients being discharged

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:38.070
with a 60-day length of stay
is lower than previous months,

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:40.170
which shows that elements of our flow

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:41.670
are starting to improve.

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:44.160
But, again, there is more work to do.

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:44.993
You can also see that

00:25:44.993 --> 00:25:47.010
in the percentage of outpatients in crisis

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:50.910
who are now receiving face-to-face
contact within 24 hours,

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:52.170
which has also increased,

00:25:52.170 --> 00:25:55.620
which is again a good
credit to those colleagues

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:57.870
that are involved in
mental health provision.

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:58.920
But there is more to do,

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:01.920
particularly in the
provision of community care.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:04.680
So, final thoughts just
around the performance view,

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:06.570
and then an opportunity for colleagues

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.610
to pick up any key areas.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:11.100
I think it's absolutely clear

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:12.840
that we are continuing to make,

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.360
remain focused on both the
constitutional standards

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:18.600
and the plans that we have
set out in the 10 year.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:19.980
During the first half of the year,

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:21.900
we've made significant
progress, as I said,

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:22.860
on those long waiters,

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:25.413
but the RTT in general remains key.

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:29.760
Urgent and emergency care
remains in a good position

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:30.990
but more work to do.

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:32.070
But encouraging signs,

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:33.960
particularly around ambulance handover

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:35.580
and the four-hour target

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:38.460
and cancer and diagnostics are improving.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:40.320
But to meet the ambitions
that we set ourself,

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:42.090
we know we need to do more.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:43.560
Everything we do now is focused

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:45.090
around those 10 Year ambitions.

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:48.000
Everything we do is focused
around transparency.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:49.620
And I hope you can start to see that

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:50.870
in the beginning of this report.

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:53.550
So very happy to take questions, chair.

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:55.226
