of this service in

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:45.300
to have the discussions with us,

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:48.810
and, for example, a lot
of effort and energy

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:50.400
coming from our mental health sector,

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:52.410
saying, "How can we be involved?"

00:34:52.410 --> 00:34:55.800
So we recently met with a group of CFOs

00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:59.130
to start talking through what
would be meaningful for them,

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:02.670
recognising the data sets are
not quite what we would hope,

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:03.870
and then taking the challenge

00:35:03.870 --> 00:35:05.580
that we've had previously from the board,

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.700
we do have a high measure of productivity

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:11.700
to report into government.

00:35:11.700 --> 00:35:14.010
That's not particularly
meaningful for the sector,

00:35:14.010 --> 00:35:16.440
so we are breaking that down
into its component parts.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:18.840
So there's something
that everyone can use,

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:20.340
and there's a huge amount of interest.

00:35:20.340 --> 00:35:23.460
So we are just about end of July

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:25.500
we are going to be launching a dashboard

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:30.500
that takes productivity and
cost data down to HRG level,

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:35.520
and I would say there were 50
providers that were involved

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:37.050
in some of these recent discussions,

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:39.450
and everybody's saying, "When
can we get our hands on it?"

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:41.070
We will be publishing that data.

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:42.420
It's not a league table.

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:43.650
It's going to be something

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:46.080
that sparks people's curiosity action.

00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:48.420
- Great. Good to hear.

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:49.770
Jane, did you want to come in?

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:51.180
- Yeah, thanks.

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:54.030
Picking up a little bit
on some of the aspects

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:57.870
around reporting
performance in primary care.

00:35:57.870 --> 00:36:00.030
It's really just putting a
marker down for the future

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:02.610
around prevention because if
you look at the two measures

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:05.280
that will be reported on a biannual basis,

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:06.720
they're prevention of further harm.

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:09.480
They're not really
prevention of the condition.

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:11.051
And although we know

00:36:11.051 --> 00:36:13.560
that a lot of the big
picture preventative measures

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:15.690
you can take in health, you know,

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:17.850
change over a very slow period

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:20.550
and kind of quite hard
to land with the public

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:21.383
for that reason,

00:36:21.383 --> 00:36:24.120
I just wonder whether some
thought could be given

00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:27.390
to proxies for success in that area

00:36:27.390 --> 00:36:28.530
that you could measure through,

00:36:28.530 --> 00:36:30.840
particularly through
primary and community care.

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:32.190
So it's not one for now,

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:35.220
but I think something that,
you know, can be integrated

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:36.630
into these sort of statistics

00:36:36.630 --> 00:36:39.720
which is about, you know,
things genuinely prevented

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:41.190
rather than, for example, you know,

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:43.530
hypertension being treated,

00:36:43.530 --> 00:36:46.440
it's more kind of people
being proactively prevented

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:47.610
from getting to that point.

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:50.550
So I think it's worth thinking
about the design of that now

00:36:50.550 --> 00:36:52.680
as we move towards, you know,

00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:54.780
the left shift in the 10 Year plan.

00:36:54.780 --> 00:36:56.367
- [Penny] Yeah, well let's
take Mary's question,

00:36:56.367 --> 00:36:59.310
and then come back David
and Claire to come in.

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:02.040
- I just wanted to ask whether
we've got a proper structure

00:37:02.040 --> 00:37:04.650
to report on childhood vaccinations.

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:07.023
I mean we got this problem with measles,

00:37:08.010 --> 00:37:12.870
and I think we could focus
on pre-pandemic planning

00:37:12.870 --> 00:37:14.370
with older people

00:37:14.370 --> 00:37:16.530
rather than looking at
that across the board.

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:19.263
So I wonder how we report
that in the future.

00:37:21.461 --> 00:37:22.523
- [David] Thank you, chair.
- [Penny] David or Claire.

00:37:22.523 --> 00:37:24.210
- So both really important points

00:37:24.210 --> 00:37:26.880
and particularly thinking
about cardiovascular disease

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:27.990
in a different way

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:29.940
and how we prepare that work.

00:37:29.940 --> 00:37:32.970
On vaccinations, we do have
a lot of that data available.

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:35.610
So how best we, again in
summary form, bring that in

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:37.260
particularly with what's
been happening in Liverpool,

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:38.760
but Claire is...

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:40.143
- Yes, so it's the primary

00:37:40.143 --> 00:37:42.090
and secondary prevention, isn't it?

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.460
So I think we've got very good ways

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:45.570
of measuring secondary prevention,

00:37:45.570 --> 00:37:48.370
particularly around cardiovascular
disease and diabetes.

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:50.953
We've just launched

00:37:50.953 --> 00:37:52.890
a neighbourhood health
implementation programme,

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:56.220
and, as part of that, we've
got a design day in August

00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:58.350
which will be looking at how we judge

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:01.050
whether the programme has been a success,

00:38:01.050 --> 00:38:01.883
and the important bit

00:38:01.883 --> 00:38:03.930
about the way the programme was launched

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:05.700
with letters out to the ICB chief execs

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:07.710
as well as the local
authority chief execs.

00:38:07.710 --> 00:38:08.543
And one of that session

00:38:08.543 --> 00:38:11.790
will be looking at more
population outcomes-based measures

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:14.070
as well as the NHS outcome framework

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:16.350
and actually how we bring those together

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:18.810
to give a set of metrics that
will address, as you say,

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:20.820
that broader prevention
than wider determinants

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:23.490
but that also impact upon NHS performance.

00:38:23.490 --> 00:38:24.750
And I think when we've done that work,

00:38:24.750 --> 00:38:26.250
we then need to bring that through

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:29.043
back into the integrated
pack as we go forward.

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:33.060
- Okay, so I'm going to suggest,

00:38:33.060 --> 00:38:35.903
'cause obviously lots of
interest in this, which is great,

00:38:37.770 --> 00:38:40.800
we are going to do a
little bit of rejigging

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:43.860
of some of the board committees
from September anyway,

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:47.340
and Robert's already mentioned
changes to Quality Committee

00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:49.170
and National Quality Board,

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:52.830
so I think some of the first piece of work

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:54.450
through the National Quality Board

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:57.300
and Quality Committee of this
board needs to be agreeing.

00:38:57.300 --> 00:39:00.030
What do we think is a
comprehensive set of metrics.

00:39:00.030 --> 00:39:01.710
It will absolutely be a long list.

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:05.370
I suspect we'll be up into
the order of you know, 200,

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:07.200
on quality which would include things

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:10.110
like primary prevention,
childhood vaccination,

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:11.670
a whole host of things.

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:13.260
So what is it that we will want

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:15.870
that National Quality Board to look at

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:17.040
however frequently it meets,

00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:18.360
every couple of months let's say,

00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:20.670
in terms of a detailed
set of quality metrics

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:22.590
that look at all aspects of quality.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:24.390
That is a key piece of work.

00:39:24.390 --> 00:39:26.130
We've got a performance committee.

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:26.963
We need to say,

00:39:26.963 --> 00:39:30.184
"What are the key metrics
of performance overall,"

00:39:30.184 --> 00:39:32.190
which include all aspects of performance

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:34.140
that go through the Performance Committee.

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:35.880
How do we look at that in detail there,

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:36.840
bring a summary here,

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:40.800
but also have the ability to dive down.

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:42.510
And then crucially is to make sure

00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:45.270
that our sort of core set of metrics

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:47.340
that go into the Oversight Framework

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:49.530
we all get sent every week

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:50.550
and going to meetings

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:53.760
with Secretary of State and
other ministers are consistent

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:55.050
and that we're all seeing those,

00:39:55.050 --> 00:39:55.890
and that we all agree

00:39:55.890 --> 00:39:57.960
that they're the most
meaningful set of metrics

00:39:57.960 --> 00:39:59.160
that we want to be looking at.

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:01.380
So key bit of work that comes out of this

00:40:01.380 --> 00:40:02.213
that we should aim

00:40:02.213 --> 00:40:05.040
to be in a more consistent place, I think,

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:07.650
by the end of the year
at the latest on that.

00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:08.899
Is that okay for everyone?

00:40:08.899 --> 00:40:09.732
- Yeah.

00:40:09.732 --> 00:40:10.773
- Okay. Oh, Mark.

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:13.620
And we need, sorry.

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:15.090
Actually Mark, looking at you,

00:40:15.090 --> 00:40:17.430
just I would also say
now. I was going to say,

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:19.590
that as part of that will be the metrics

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:22.170
by which we are going to
monitor implementation

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:23.760
of the 10 Year Plan,

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:25.110
and as a crucial bit of that,

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:27.600
our favourite topic of
outpatient redesign.

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:28.433
- [Mark Cubbon] Yes.

00:40:28.433 --> 00:40:29.867
- Presumably, that's what
you're going to say, Mark.

00:40:32.917 --> 00:40:33.750
- It was in my mind.

00:40:33.750 --> 00:40:34.680
- [Penny] Yeah.

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:35.940
- Yeah, definitely.

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:37.350
Penny, can I just mention one thing

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:39.630
related to the quality of care aspects,

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:41.280
particularly around cancer patients

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:42.630
if I just would add to it?

00:40:42.630 --> 00:40:43.530
- [Penny] Yeah.

00:40:43.530 --> 00:40:45.690
- So, today we published the results

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:48.360
in the National Cancer Patient Survey,

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:50.280
and I just wanted to share
it with the board if I could.

00:40:50.280 --> 00:40:52.290
So it's now published,
at 9:30 this morning,

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:55.920
and we will make sure everyone
has a copy of the results,

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:57.270
but it's really positive.

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:58.680
So we've had the best results

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:01.890
for the overall care
provided for cancer patients

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:03.390
for four years.

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:04.223
We've been looking back

00:41:04.223 --> 00:41:08.280
for how some of the questions
go even further back,

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:09.420
you know, how comparable they are

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:11.850
'cause the data set was
changed four years ago,

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:16.800
but we have an overall
rating of just under 9%,

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:18.540
well 9 out of 10 rather,

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:22.860
so it's 894, 8.94 out of 10,

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:24.477
which is the most positive
rating that we've had.

00:41:24.477 --> 00:41:28.380
And just an indication, last
year was 8.89 out of 10.

00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:31.950
So it's a really significant step forward,

00:41:31.950 --> 00:41:34.560
and I know we hear a lot about things

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:36.690
that we need to do better,

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:38.070
but I think there's big
opportunities for us

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:39.870
to learn about things that are going well

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:42.240
with the experience for cancer patients

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:45.360
and think about that across
the broader groups of patients,

00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:49.470
around outpatients, urgent care,
and for inpatients as well.

00:41:49.470 --> 00:41:51.600
So really good feedback about things

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:53.550
that we're getting right
in terms of teamwork,

00:41:53.550 --> 00:41:57.060
across in the coordination
of care for patients

00:41:57.060 --> 00:42:00.480
and also looking at how patients
feel involved in the care

00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:01.980
and the contact they're
having with our clinicians.

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:04.301
So I just thought it would
be good to mention it today

00:42:04.301 --> 00:42:06.540
given the fact it's only just
been published this morning.

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:07.790
- Great. Thank you, Mark.

00:42:09.060 --> 00:42:10.890
Elizabeth, did you want to bring up

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:12.540
other aspects of finance or-

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:15.240
- Yeah, so hopefully
people have had a chance

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:16.950
to read the report.

00:42:16.950 --> 00:42:19.080
The paper sets out a brief update

00:42:19.080 --> 00:42:20.280
obviously on the spending review

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:22.180
just to conclude that for the record

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:27.063
and also an update on the
month 2 financial position.

00:42:27.900 --> 00:42:30.360
Would you like just the key headlines?

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:31.193
- [Penny] Yes, please.

00:42:31.193 --> 00:42:32.026
- Yeah, given the timing. Yeah.

00:42:32.026 --> 00:42:34.440
So, in terms of spending review,

00:42:34.440 --> 00:42:37.740
obviously in June concluded phase 2

00:42:37.740 --> 00:42:40.830
of the '25 spending review.

00:42:40.830 --> 00:42:43.800
We now have revenue
budgets through to 28/29

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:45.210
and capital for a further year,

00:42:45.210 --> 00:42:48.180
so three-year and four-year
revenue and capital budgets.

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:49.020
This will really help

00:42:49.020 --> 00:42:51.660
when we move into medium term planning.

00:42:51.660 --> 00:42:54.300
We also plan to give
some indicative budgets

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:56.310
for further years just
for planning purposes,

00:42:56.310 --> 00:42:59.340
and we're talking to both the
chief exec and CFO community

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:00.780
about that more recently

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:02.403
and engaging with clinicians.

00:43:03.570 --> 00:43:07.260
If I was to look at moving perhaps

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:09.150
straight to month 2 position,

00:43:09.150 --> 00:43:13.230
so planning for 25/26
concluded early this year.

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:14.760
That was after application

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:16.980
of 2.2 billion pounds deficit support,

00:43:16.980 --> 00:43:18.120
but we now have plans

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:22.440
where 42 systems have
submitted break-even plans.

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:24.293
In aggregate, you'll see in the report,

00:43:24.293 --> 00:43:25.860
including the NHS England position,

00:43:25.860 --> 00:43:27.660
the overspend year-to-date at month 2

00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:29.880
is a 51 million pound overspend.

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:34.560
So that's 0.2% of our year-to-date
allocation, which is low.

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:38.160
But within that, there is
a year-to-date overspend

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:41.040
in our systems of 110 million pounds.

00:43:41.040 --> 00:43:43.500
I say year-to-date overspend.

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:46.200
This is concentrated
predominantly in four systems.

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:49.560
You'll see in the annex
which are those systems.

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:52.410
So Northeast London, North Central, BSW,

00:43:52.410 --> 00:43:53.910
and Birmingham and Solihull,

00:43:53.910 --> 00:43:55.830
but that data is there.

00:43:55.830 --> 00:43:57.930
Encouragingly, half of our systems

00:43:57.930 --> 00:44:02.220
and 75% of our providers
are on plan at month 2.

00:44:02.220 --> 00:44:04.800
And at the same point
this year, comparatively,

00:44:04.800 --> 00:44:08.700
the 110 million pound
year-to-date overspend

00:44:08.700 --> 00:44:10.560
was actually 240 million.

00:44:10.560 --> 00:44:12.933
So we're in a better position overall.

00:44:14.040 --> 00:44:17.040
Efficiency is a key driver obviously.

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:19.920
So we've got 159 million
pound shortfall at month 2

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:23.310
in our ST plan, but that's been offset.

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:24.990
Provider pay remains flat,

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:27.210
but we are actually seeing
a decrease in head count

00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:29.543
compared to month 12 of last year at 1.4%.

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:35.250
And then, if I could, hot off
the press I guess, Q1 numbers.

00:44:35.250 --> 00:44:37.890
So very much month two was for the record.

00:44:37.890 --> 00:44:42.210
So, at Q1, comparably
the 110 million pound

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:46.680
year-to-date deficit has
reduced and is now 78 million

00:44:46.680 --> 00:44:49.293
with four systems making
up over half of that.

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:55.449
The systems are overall
showing this improvement,

00:44:55.449 --> 00:44:57.570
and we're also seeing a slowing

00:44:57.570 --> 00:45:00.270
of the run rate of overspend.

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:02.940
I just want to say again,
similar to David actually,

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:06.483
a tremendous sense of
energy and focus on many.

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:09.270
We are not tackling money

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:11.310
without thinking about quality and access.

00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:13.170
That's not what the
CFO community are doing

00:45:13.170 --> 00:45:15.150
in the discussions we've had.

00:45:15.150 --> 00:45:17.160
Definitely having been a regional director

00:45:17.160 --> 00:45:18.570
moving back into this role,

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:21.420
it's very much alive in my mind.

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:23.250
But I just want to say testament actually

00:45:23.250 --> 00:45:24.840
to the community out there

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:27.240
and the way that people are
focused and getting involved.

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:28.440
So, amazing work.

00:45:28.440 --> 00:45:30.930
There are some key risks in the paper.

00:45:30.930 --> 00:45:32.190
Andrew's picked up one of them

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:35.970
which is the potential
risk of industrial action.

00:45:35.970 --> 00:45:39.480
And whilst month 3 quarter 1

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:42.450
is a hugely positive position,

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:43.620
you know, it's high risk,

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:45.360
and we've got some back-ended plans.

00:45:45.360 --> 00:45:48.090
So we are day by day, week by week

00:45:48.090 --> 00:45:50.280
engaging with the service,

00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:53.010
but the response has been amazing.

00:45:53.010 --> 00:45:56.910
So, in conclusion, I think
the financial outlook provides

00:45:56.910 --> 00:45:58.710
real terms growth,

00:45:58.710 --> 00:45:59.970
and the systems start

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:02.640
from a balanced financial
position, which is good.

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:04.500
We're on track at Q3,

00:46:04.500 --> 00:46:07.530
but there's still a lot
of risk to mitigate,

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:10.530
and this is going to
make delivery challenging

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:12.797
for the remainder of the year.

00:46:12.797 --> 00:46:14.490
- Okay. Thank you.

00:46:14.490 --> 00:46:15.690
Mark.

00:46:15.690 --> 00:46:18.990
- Elizabeth, clearly there's
been an enormous improvement

00:46:18.990 --> 00:46:21.210
in performance form,

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:23.223
quite a large proportion of the system,

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:26.010
and like one quarter,

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:27.630
and it's super early
in the year to do this.

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:28.680
But are there lessons

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:30.300
that we can already start drawing out

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:32.553
about what people are
doing differently now?

00:46:34.020 --> 00:46:36.750
- Yeah, Jim might want to come in as well.

00:46:36.750 --> 00:46:40.800
The response I've had, particularly
from the CFO community,

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:42.540
and later today we'll be talking about

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:44.610
our Oversight Framework,

00:46:44.610 --> 00:46:46.290
the thing that people have really welcomed

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:48.993
is the transparency actually,

00:46:50.070 --> 00:46:53.700
not just the transparency
shared back with NHS England

00:46:53.700 --> 00:46:54.810
but across the systems,

00:46:54.810 --> 00:46:57.510
and there is definitely
learning that's going on.

00:46:57.510 --> 00:47:00.270
So there have been efficiency programmes,

00:47:00.270 --> 00:47:02.790
and our productivity
packs actually have driven

00:47:02.790 --> 00:47:05.770
a different quality of conversation

00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:09.150
both around the board table

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:11.160
but also across the CFO community.

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:13.890
So I think there's lessons there.

00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:18.420
I also think we've pointed
to quite a lot of examples

00:47:18.420 --> 00:47:21.420
where, I know people
don't like the term waste,

00:47:21.420 --> 00:47:23.730
but actually we're
seeing variation in spend

00:47:23.730 --> 00:47:26.550
that you can't really justify,

00:47:26.550 --> 00:47:28.590
and people have gone
back and tackled that,

00:47:28.590 --> 00:47:30.480
and where they haven't
been able to tackle it,

00:47:30.480 --> 00:47:33.660
I think it's remarkable in
such a short period of time

00:47:33.660 --> 00:47:35.100
that people have come forward

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:38.130
and genuinely say, "We don't
know when we need some help."

00:47:38.130 --> 00:47:40.830
And there has been a help on hand

00:47:40.830 --> 00:47:42.420
either within NHS England,

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:45.000
or there's someone somewhere in the NHS

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:46.435
that knows how to do it

00:47:46.435 --> 00:47:47.670
'cause that's how we've got variations.

00:47:47.670 --> 00:47:50.190
So we've been putting
people together to do that.

00:47:50.190 --> 00:47:53.640
So we've called it a little
bit of a finance amnesty

00:47:53.640 --> 00:47:54.630
through the summer.

00:47:54.630 --> 00:47:56.190
It's kind of like it doesn't really matter

00:47:56.190 --> 00:47:57.780
if you don't know.

00:47:57.780 --> 00:47:59.070
I've been six years of finance,

00:47:59.070 --> 00:48:00.540
so I'm building my muscle memory.

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:02.790
That's the way I've described it.

00:48:02.790 --> 00:48:04.680
So it doesn't really
matter that you don't know.

00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:06.290
But what does matter is that you know

00:48:06.290 --> 00:48:07.950
by the time we get into the autumn

00:48:07.950 --> 00:48:10.740
because this gets tougher
as a year progresses,

00:48:10.740 --> 00:48:12.600
and there's an awful lot
of other things to do

00:48:12.600 --> 00:48:14.550
including medium-term planning

00:48:14.550 --> 00:48:16.710
of which the whole service,
the whole population

00:48:16.710 --> 00:48:17.560
is looking at us.

00:48:19.770 --> 00:48:21.060
- Yeah, and I would just add to that

00:48:21.060 --> 00:48:23.360
we've completely reinforced
what's just been said actually,

00:48:23.360 --> 00:48:25.800
so really strong leadership response.

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:29.250
People are really facing into
the issues and engaging on it.

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:32.730
I think pointing people to
the variation, sharing data,

00:48:32.730 --> 00:48:36.960
making it more transparent,
encouraging boards to challenge

00:48:36.960 --> 00:48:38.310
where they're an outlier,

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:39.540
you know, get a hold of that again.

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:41.610
I thinks it's all been fantastic.

00:48:41.610 --> 00:48:44.400
And then, but a few of us
had an exchange last night.

00:48:44.400 --> 00:48:45.540
Fortunately yesterday I had

00:48:45.540 --> 00:48:47.670
three interactions with colleagues:

00:48:47.670 --> 00:48:50.130
one FD, two chief execs,

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:54.480
who all independently said this is tough

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:56.943
but really good actually,
really welcome it.

00:48:57.840 --> 00:48:59.040
Very, very keen.

00:48:59.040 --> 00:49:01.980
One of those was very keen to
use the platform of this year

00:49:01.980 --> 00:49:04.860
to become a new FT and knew
what they needed to do.

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:07.080
They had a medium-term plan development

00:49:07.080 --> 00:49:09.930
in terms of, you know, how
they could see a path to that.

00:49:11.070 --> 00:49:12.750
Conversation with chair the other day

00:49:12.750 --> 00:49:15.960
who was very, very keen to point out

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:18.450
that they were segment 3 now
because they have a deficit,

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:21.116
but when they get rid of
that deficit this year,

00:49:21.116 --> 00:49:22.890
they think they could be segment 1.

00:49:22.890 --> 00:49:26.640
And then finally, what Glen
and colleagues have done

00:49:26.640 --> 00:49:27.630
on the Oversight Framework.

00:49:27.630 --> 00:49:29.490
I think it's really fantastic actually.

00:49:29.490 --> 00:49:32.010
So we grappled with this at
the beginning end of the year.

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:34.500
It's not right. It's not perfect.

00:49:34.500 --> 00:49:36.870
But in terms of the behavioural response

00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:39.840
and the belief that actually now I know,

00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:42.720
if I deal with these
things, I can progress,

00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:43.980
I think's really powerful.

00:49:43.980 --> 00:49:46.470
And we will tweak that,

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:49.197
and we'll change how it
incentivizes the certain things,

00:49:49.197 --> 00:49:51.090
and it'll become dynamic over time.

00:49:51.090 --> 00:49:54.273
But I think that is really
good to see in real life.

00:49:55.276 --> 00:49:59.036
- Okay. I've got Jeremy,
Robert, and then Glen.

00:49:59.036 --> 00:50:00.060
- Just a comment and a request really.

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:02.700
Just stating the obvious
that this is very tight.

00:50:02.700 --> 00:50:03.930
It's good progress.

00:50:03.930 --> 00:50:05.970
It's great to see the level of engagement,

00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:09.090
but if there is a 1.5 billion challenge

00:50:09.090 --> 00:50:10.170
created by industrial action,

00:50:10.170 --> 00:50:12.690
and hopefully we can overcome it,

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:14.610
that creates a major challenge,

00:50:14.610 --> 00:50:15.630
and it's really a request really,

00:50:15.630 --> 00:50:18.000
that if we end up in that situation,

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:19.590
we have to make some
really difficult choices

00:50:19.590 --> 00:50:20.610
to bring those back here

00:50:20.610 --> 00:50:21.753
so we can discuss the trade offs there

00:50:21.753 --> 00:50:24.120
'cause I know that's
going to be very difficult

00:50:24.120 --> 00:50:27.210
given the challenge to just
balance the books at the moment.

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:29.340
So, really a request
on behalf of the board

00:50:29.340 --> 00:50:30.810
that we have a look at those

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:32.310
and see what those
difficult choices look like.

00:50:32.310 --> 00:50:34.620
- [Penny] Yeah. Okay, Robert.

00:50:34.620 --> 00:50:36.790
- Yeah, no great work, Elizabeth.

00:50:36.790 --> 00:50:40.143
Can I just ask you, probe
a little bit on capital.

00:50:41.340 --> 00:50:43.560
So I'm sure this capital
budget could be spent

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:46.350
several times over in the
secondary care sector,

00:50:46.350 --> 00:50:51.330
but I'm also interested in
the neighbourhood health model

00:50:51.330 --> 00:50:53.670
which, as Claire was saying
earlier, is to be defined.

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:55.050
But I would be very surprised

00:50:55.050 --> 00:50:56.310
if one of the consequences of that

00:50:56.310 --> 00:50:59.280
isn't that the premises
needs to be revised

00:50:59.280 --> 00:51:02.610
to consolidate and accommodate
multi-professional teams,

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:04.050
et cetera, et cetera.

00:51:04.050 --> 00:51:06.120
Is there any thought

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:09.832
as to how that capital cost might be met?

00:51:09.832 --> 00:51:14.143
- Yeah, so in the spending
review for coming years, we

00:51:14.143 --> 00:51:17.460
have put some early estimates
in for neighbourhood health.

00:51:17.460 --> 00:51:19.323
As you described, Robert,

00:51:20.610 --> 00:51:22.230
there's a variation of views

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:25.200
about how big and how costly

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:28.683
those buildings and premises
could potentially be.

00:51:29.580 --> 00:51:32.700
The strong sense is

00:51:32.700 --> 00:51:36.870
that a lot of those
premises actually exist.

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:38.370
So this is modifications.

00:51:38.370 --> 00:51:41.910
So we think we can do this in
quite a cost-effective way,

00:51:41.910 --> 00:51:43.950
that we've got some
fantastic CDCs, for example,

00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:46.200
that are not being fully utilised,

00:51:46.200 --> 00:51:49.380
and they would be a fabulous
vehicle to go forward.

00:51:49.380 --> 00:51:52.200
So I think it's hard
to predict and estimate

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:53.160
what the costs are going to be

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:55.530
until we really get into the model.

00:51:55.530 --> 00:51:58.020
But, equally, we have got a line of sight

00:51:58.020 --> 00:51:59.910
that some capital will be required,

00:51:59.910 --> 00:52:01.470
but, as you say, it's about trade offs

00:52:01.470 --> 00:52:04.020
'cause actually capital across
the spending view period

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:08.943
is broadly flat despite the
work that came back from Darzi.

00:52:09.915 --> 00:52:12.170
- I think we will need more
detailed review of capital

00:52:12.170 --> 00:52:14.100
at some stage coming.

00:52:14.100 --> 00:52:16.110
Glenn and then Duncan.

00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:17.550
- I was just going to emphasise the point

00:52:17.550 --> 00:52:20.670
about that accountability

00:52:20.670 --> 00:52:23.640
of individual trusts and boards.

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:25.950
We're looking here at
the the ICS level data,

00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:27.750
but through the Oversight Framework,

00:52:27.750 --> 00:52:29.940
looking down at the
individual organisations

00:52:29.940 --> 00:52:33.270
both from a finance but also
a productivity perspective.

00:52:33.270 --> 00:52:35.730
As Jim said, that's really
helped to focus people,

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:38.373
and we will see improvement
as a consequence.

00:52:40.620 --> 00:52:43.050
- I was going to say I
think one of the concerns

00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:43.980
that's out there at the moment,

00:52:43.980 --> 00:52:45.900
given the tightness of the finances,

00:52:45.900 --> 00:52:46.830
we should just recognise

00:52:46.830 --> 00:52:49.920
that there are newly-qualified
nurses and midwives

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:53.280
who are finding it more difficult
at the moment to get jobs.

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:54.810
We are working very hard on this.

00:52:54.810 --> 00:52:56.970
I held a summit on Monday
with our regional teams

00:52:56.970 --> 00:52:58.110
to go through this.

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:00.527
We've been working and looking
at support outside of the NHS

00:53:00.527 --> 00:53:02.280
'cause there are jobs in social care,

00:53:02.280 --> 00:53:04.080
the independent sector,

00:53:04.080 --> 00:53:05.250
and, of course, some of this

00:53:05.250 --> 00:53:07.140
is because we've got
better retention rates

00:53:07.140 --> 00:53:09.390
than we were expecting as well

00:53:09.390 --> 00:53:10.770
which is clearly a positive thing.

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:12.870
But we are doing as much as we can do

00:53:12.870 --> 00:53:15.480
to make sure that we
support people into jobs,

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:16.980
but that is clearly one of the perceptions

00:53:16.980 --> 00:53:19.500
around the financial
situation at the moment

00:53:19.500 --> 00:53:20.963
that's driving this.

00:53:20.963 --> 00:53:22.620
- Okay, and, at some stage,

00:53:22.620 --> 00:53:24.870
we need to work out how many we need

00:53:24.870 --> 00:53:26.250
which is a different question

00:53:26.250 --> 00:53:28.923
which presumably will be
through the workforce plan.

00:53:30.720 --> 00:53:33.120
- I just wanted to come back
on what Duncan has said.

00:53:33.120 --> 00:53:35.970
I mean, I really know how
hard everybody's working,

00:53:35.970 --> 00:53:38.760
but we also need to get the message out

00:53:38.760 --> 00:53:42.390
to newly graduating
healthcare professionals

00:53:42.390 --> 00:53:45.030
that you can't automatically
expect to get a job

00:53:45.030 --> 00:53:47.013
in the area that you've trained

00:53:47.013 --> 00:53:48.660
because there are vacancies

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:50.550
in different parts of the country.

00:53:50.550 --> 00:53:52.864
- And we've been working
with the HEIs on that.

00:53:52.864 --> 00:53:55.416
- [Penny] Okay, thank you. Andrew.

00:53:55.416 --> 00:53:58.320
- So, Elizabeth, just looking forward

00:53:58.320 --> 00:54:00.480
because organisations are faced

00:54:00.480 --> 00:54:04.050
with having to take 2%
out potentially every year

00:54:04.050 --> 00:54:08.190
during, you know, the plan period.

00:54:08.190 --> 00:54:12.570
Are we sharing the kind of
top 10 things with boards

00:54:12.570 --> 00:54:14.670
so that they can get a fix and plan ahead

00:54:14.670 --> 00:54:16.710
'cause I think the trick here

00:54:16.710 --> 00:54:20.100
is to, you know, get your
CIP for the following year

00:54:20.100 --> 00:54:22.590
sorted out during the summer months

00:54:22.590 --> 00:54:25.170
and, you know, really buttoned down.

00:54:25.170 --> 00:54:28.020
We don't want to miss out
on that kind of opportunity.

00:54:28.020 --> 00:54:29.970
- Yeah. Yeah, absolutely.

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:31.399
People really appreciated,

00:54:31.399 --> 00:54:33.570
the feedback we heard was
they really appreciated

00:54:33.570 --> 00:54:36.120
the productivity packs that we produced

00:54:36.120 --> 00:54:38.010
for this year's plan.

00:54:38.010 --> 00:54:39.900
We plan to reissue those.

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:41.940
I think it's, we're
hoping it's going to be

00:54:41.940 --> 00:54:44.550
the end of September,
beginning of October,

00:54:44.550 --> 00:54:46.830
so that a whole lot of extra data,

00:54:46.830 --> 00:54:48.600
the data quality will have improved

00:54:48.600 --> 00:54:51.030
'cause we'll be another year
on, and we will have used it.

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:52.770
And then there were some particular themes

00:54:52.770 --> 00:54:54.270
people wanted us to look at too,

00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:57.990
as well as, I'm really
keen, as is, I think, Penny,

00:54:57.990 --> 00:55:01.260
that we look at costs

00:55:01.260 --> 00:55:03.630
per 100,000 population

00:55:03.630 --> 00:55:05.097
including some of our staff,

00:55:05.097 --> 00:55:07.500
and it's really, when
you look at the data,

00:55:07.500 --> 00:55:12.120
it just leads at you to
ask so many questions,

00:55:12.120 --> 00:55:14.430
and many of them are
coming back unanswered.

00:55:14.430 --> 00:55:15.660
We're not judging people about that,

00:55:15.660 --> 00:55:18.840
but we're asking boards
to really face into it.

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:21.270
So I don't want to pick
particular staff groups,

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:24.600
but when you look at
the values of activity

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:25.890
versus the costs

00:55:25.890 --> 00:55:28.080
and how they've grown over recent times

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:30.270
in certain specialties and professions,

00:55:30.270 --> 00:55:32.241
it doesn't really stack up.

00:55:32.241 --> 00:55:33.510
Now there might be something,

00:55:33.510 --> 00:55:35.100
'cause equally quite a lot

00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:38.640
of operational and finance people

00:55:38.640 --> 00:55:40.890
are coming back and
saying we're doing things

00:55:40.890 --> 00:55:42.420
that we're not counting.

00:55:42.420 --> 00:55:44.400
So that's the other challenge I think

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:46.500
with Jeremy we've been talking about,

00:55:46.500 --> 00:55:48.000
making sure we understand

00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:50.190
not just the productivity measures

00:55:50.190 --> 00:55:51.750
we're traditionally counting,

00:55:51.750 --> 00:55:53.190
but what are the other things

00:55:53.190 --> 00:55:55.187
that we don't give the
service the benefit of,

00:55:55.187 --> 00:55:56.850
'cause they're different,

00:55:56.850 --> 00:55:59.523
as new models are coming on stream.

00:56:00.450 --> 00:56:01.283
- Yeah, I would just,

00:56:01.283 --> 00:56:02.850
I don't want to end on
a negative on all this,

00:56:02.850 --> 00:56:05.520
but we've had two very
strong published ones

00:56:05.520 --> 00:56:08.040
in a hot-off-the-press good
month, really strong start,

00:56:08.040 --> 00:56:09.900
all the things we just discussed,

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:11.640
which is absolutely fantastic.

00:56:11.640 --> 00:56:13.170
I'm not a big counterfactual man,

00:56:13.170 --> 00:56:15.090
but the counterfactual
was pretty horrible.

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:17.790
You know when we started this year,

00:56:17.790 --> 00:56:21.570
we should all know and
understand that we'll wobble.

00:56:21.570 --> 00:56:23.580
There'll be points at which
the numbers aren't great

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:24.413
through the year.

00:56:24.413 --> 00:56:26.550
It's how we respond to that.

00:56:26.550 --> 00:56:29.760
So we can all just get ourselves
mentally ready for that.

00:56:29.760 --> 00:56:31.260
You know, it'll be a miracle

00:56:31.260 --> 00:56:35.730
if we get through the year,
IA aside, for every month

00:56:35.730 --> 00:56:38.490
to just be consistently
better than the last.

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:40.470
So we'll work on that,

00:56:40.470 --> 00:56:41.850
make sure we respond appropriately.

00:56:41.850 --> 00:56:43.410
We'll have proper board discussion

00:56:43.410 --> 00:56:46.050
and importantly continue discussion

00:56:46.050 --> 00:56:47.460
with the service about it.

00:56:47.460 --> 00:56:48.420
- Yeah, great.

00:56:48.420 --> 00:56:49.923
So I think picking up on that,

00:56:51.930 --> 00:56:52.987
the more that we can try,

00:56:52.987 --> 00:56:54.540
and I know some of this happens

00:56:54.540 --> 00:56:56.100
in the more detailed conversations,

00:56:56.100 --> 00:56:57.840
but there is a bit about
trying to understand

00:56:57.840 --> 00:56:59.910
what are the drivers of
the financial position

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:01.290
in each system.

00:57:01.290 --> 00:57:04.923
So, on the Annex 1,

00:57:06.720 --> 00:57:07.620
couple of thoughts.

00:57:07.620 --> 00:57:09.090
One is it'd be good to show

00:57:09.090 --> 00:57:12.000
the absolute figures that are here

00:57:12.000 --> 00:57:13.770
as a percentage of their total budget

00:57:13.770 --> 00:57:15.600
because they are quite different sizes,

00:57:15.600 --> 00:57:19.830
and so we should probably be
looking at it as a percentage

00:57:19.830 --> 00:57:21.900
as well as the absolute figures.

00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:26.040
The second is to try
and separate out a bit

00:57:26.040 --> 00:57:29.880
ICB performance versus
provider performance.

00:57:29.880 --> 00:57:32.910
And so it would link a bit
to the previous conversation

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:34.290
if you've got an ICB

00:57:34.290 --> 00:57:38.610
where, for example, we've
got particularly poor rates

00:57:38.610 --> 00:57:41.490
of quality of care for
a number of conditions,

00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:44.580
or we've got high rates of
emergency admission to hospital,

00:57:44.580 --> 00:57:46.530
it would be good to see that

00:57:46.530 --> 00:57:49.770
as a driver of financial position

00:57:49.770 --> 00:57:51.300
as much as provider performance.

00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:53.490
So we can sort of get
a bit beneath the skin

00:57:53.490 --> 00:57:55.530
and understand what it is that's happening

00:57:55.530 --> 00:57:56.700
in different areas.

00:57:56.700 --> 00:57:59.610
So, I think again, there's a balance here

00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:01.470
between not swamping us with data,

00:58:01.470 --> 00:58:03.690
but having some sense of what's going on

00:58:03.690 --> 00:58:05.430
in each of these areas would be helpful.

00:58:05.430 --> 00:58:07.093
- [Elizabeth] Yeah, very happy to do it.

00:58:07.093 --> 00:58:08.670
- Presumably that comes through

00:58:08.670 --> 00:58:10.920
some of the Oversight
Framework work as well.

00:58:11.850 --> 00:58:13.440
Okay, good. Very good conversation.

00:58:13.440 --> 00:58:15.093
Thank you everybody for that.

00:58:16.170 --> 00:58:18.630
So, but picking up on where you were, Jim,

00:58:18.630 --> 00:58:20.220
let's move on to risk

00:58:20.220 --> 00:58:24.180
and how we apprise ourselves of that.

00:58:24.180 --> 00:58:28.680
So we've got John here
along with David and Jeremy.

00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:30.570
- Thank you very much, Penny,

00:58:30.570 --> 00:58:32.790
and thanks to you and
Jeremy for your support

00:58:32.790 --> 00:58:36.450
in developing this new two risk
registers which you'll see.

00:58:36.450 --> 00:58:38.550
One is strategic in its focus.

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:40.680
One is operational.

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:44.070
And both will work alongside
the risk management framework

00:58:44.070 --> 00:58:46.380
which we're also asking
the board to approve today

00:58:46.380 --> 00:58:48.780
which is included within the pack.

00:58:48.780 --> 00:58:50.130
John is going to take us through

00:58:50.130 --> 00:58:53.430
just a little bit of of
detail on both registers

00:58:53.430 --> 00:58:54.410
just so you can get a feel

00:58:54.410 --> 00:58:55.920
of the way we've tried to operate.

00:58:55.920 --> 00:58:58.023
But just a couple of quick observations.

00:58:59.100 --> 00:59:01.800
Firstly, we're delighted
that this is the first time

00:59:01.800 --> 00:59:03.750
we're bringing these
registers to a public board.

00:59:03.750 --> 00:59:05.880
We think again, in that
spirit of transparency,

00:59:05.880 --> 00:59:08.733
it's incredibly important that we do.

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:12.390
Also, just wearing my other hat,

00:59:12.390 --> 00:59:13.800
in terms of managing a hospital,

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:16.470
a risk register works when it feels real

00:59:16.470 --> 00:59:18.930
and when you can tangibly
look at the detail

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:21.517
and think, "Actually, that's
what keeps me awake at night,

00:59:21.517 --> 00:59:23.520
"and this is what I'm
talking about a lot."

00:59:23.520 --> 00:59:25.410
And I would argue when you
look through these risks,

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:26.820
both strategic and operational,

00:59:26.820 --> 00:59:29.400
they very much mirror what we
are going to be talking about

00:59:29.400 --> 00:59:30.873
an awful lot this morning.

00:59:34.710 --> 00:59:36.540
Risk register can sometimes be seen

00:59:36.540 --> 00:59:39.090
as a bit of a bureaucratic dry thing,

00:59:39.090 --> 00:59:40.500
and so we're really pleased

00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:43.470
this is towards the front of the agenda,

00:59:43.470 --> 00:59:45.630
nestled behind the strategic conversation

00:59:45.630 --> 00:59:47.250
because we're very much of the opinion

00:59:47.250 --> 00:59:49.560
that risks should drive strategy,

00:59:49.560 --> 00:59:50.850
and risks should be inherent

00:59:50.850 --> 00:59:53.220
in all the strategic
conversations we have.

00:59:53.220 --> 00:59:54.630
So it is within that light,

00:59:54.630 --> 00:59:56.487
both the strategic and
operational risk strategies

00:59:56.487 --> 00:59:57.780
are put together.

00:59:57.780 --> 00:59:58.637
But a big thank you to John and his team

00:59:58.637 --> 01:00:00.450
who've done an enormous amount of work.

01:00:00.450 --> 01:00:01.644
And perhaps if I hand over to John

01:00:01.644 --> 01:00:03.660
just to give a bit of summary on both.

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:05.340
- Thank you, and good morning.

01:00:05.340 --> 01:00:07.440
Yeah, so I'd like to put out three points

01:00:07.440 --> 01:00:09.663
from the strategic register.

01:00:10.691 --> 01:00:11.850
There's a new risk at the top

01:00:11.850 --> 01:00:15.570
around developing a
coherent set of strategies

01:00:15.570 --> 01:00:16.530
and delivery plans.

01:00:16.530 --> 01:00:17.970
So that's in relation to the 10 Year Plan

01:00:17.970 --> 01:00:19.830
but also our objectives more broadly,

01:00:19.830 --> 01:00:20.970
for example, our duties

01:00:20.970 --> 01:00:23.340
around reducing healthcare inequalities,

01:00:23.340 --> 01:00:24.597
and the mitigations to that

01:00:24.597 --> 01:00:26.730
are what you would expect are ongoing work

01:00:26.730 --> 01:00:28.950
around the 10 Year Health Plan delivery

01:00:28.950 --> 01:00:31.020
and also a new strategy committee

01:00:31.020 --> 01:00:32.250
we're standing up in the autumn

01:00:32.250 --> 01:00:33.960
that will give oversight and coordination

01:00:33.960 --> 01:00:36.240
to that development work.

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:38.340
The second point is around the second risk

01:00:38.340 --> 01:00:40.710
which is changed from the last version

01:00:40.710 --> 01:00:42.420
and is around the scale of change required

01:00:42.420 --> 01:00:44.100
to deliver the 10 Year Health Plan

01:00:44.100 --> 01:00:46.260
and create the conditions for success.

01:00:46.260 --> 01:00:49.290
And that's, again, relating to work

01:00:49.290 --> 01:00:50.370
the board will be familiar with

01:00:50.370 --> 01:00:53.291
around the Oversight Framework, model ICB

01:00:53.291 --> 01:00:56.100
and region blueprints in the
wider system architecture.

01:00:56.100 --> 01:00:58.080
And the third point is
just to put out the theme

01:00:58.080 --> 01:01:00.480
that some of our top risks

01:01:00.480 --> 01:01:03.450
continue to all be around
data, digital, and technology.

01:01:03.450 --> 01:01:06.510
So you'll see towards
the end of the register

01:01:06.510 --> 01:01:08.880
that we've got cyber
attacks against the NHS,

01:01:08.880 --> 01:01:11.070
the data breach within NHS England,

01:01:11.070 --> 01:01:13.290
and also being able to
maximise the opportunities

01:01:13.290 --> 01:01:15.150
from new technology and innovations

01:01:15.150 --> 01:01:17.580
all being very highly scored risks.

01:01:17.580 --> 01:01:19.980
So that remains critical for us.

01:01:19.980 --> 01:01:21.420
On the operational register,

01:01:21.420 --> 01:01:23.940
and this is our more
sort of short-term risks

01:01:23.940 --> 01:01:27.090
more likely to be mitigated
through systems and processes

01:01:27.090 --> 01:01:29.010
and also our mechanism
for escalating risks up

01:01:29.010 --> 01:01:30.990
from teams around the organisation.

01:01:30.990 --> 01:01:31.823
I won't go into detail,

01:01:31.823 --> 01:01:33.047
but there are three that
have gone up since last time

01:01:33.047 --> 01:01:35.280
that I just wanted to call out.

01:01:35.280 --> 01:01:38.280
One is industrial action
for obvious reasons.

01:01:38.280 --> 01:01:40.290
One is around the delivery of IT systems

01:01:40.290 --> 01:01:42.240
to support the performance list,

01:01:42.240 --> 01:01:44.760
which is our register of
primary care practitioners

01:01:44.760 --> 01:01:46.410
and revalidation of them,

01:01:46.410 --> 01:01:48.810
and also delivery of the
year financial position

01:01:48.810 --> 01:01:50.400
as we've just heard.

01:01:50.400 --> 01:01:51.420
And I'll touch very briefly

01:01:51.420 --> 01:01:53.010
on the risk management framework.

01:01:53.010 --> 01:01:55.470
This defines our approach
to risk management

01:01:55.470 --> 01:01:56.303
in the organisation,

01:01:56.303 --> 01:01:57.510
make sure we do it consistently

01:01:57.510 --> 01:01:59.340
and in line with good practise.

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:00.270
We already have one.

01:02:00.270 --> 01:02:02.160
What we've got here is shorter, simpler,

01:02:02.160 --> 01:02:03.630
and easier to follow.

01:02:03.630 --> 01:02:05.850
It formalises some of the
risk governance arrangements

01:02:05.850 --> 01:02:08.070
that we've already been
embedding over the last year

01:02:08.070 --> 01:02:10.830
and also enhances how
we develop and manage

01:02:10.830 --> 01:02:12.960
cross directorate risks.

01:02:12.960 --> 01:02:14.310
So that's here for approval,

01:02:14.310 --> 01:02:16.680
acknowledging that we'll
have to keep tweaking it

01:02:16.680 --> 01:02:19.053
as the committee structure evolves.

01:02:21.390 --> 01:02:22.223
- [Penny] Jeremy.

01:02:22.223 --> 01:02:24.360
- So great piece of work. Well done, John.

01:02:24.360 --> 01:02:25.410
This has moved on a lot.

01:02:25.410 --> 01:02:27.210
Anybody who's sat on the audit committee

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:28.080
over the last three or four years,

01:02:28.080 --> 01:02:29.760
this has moved on leaps and bounds.

01:02:29.760 --> 01:02:30.840
So thanks very much.

01:02:30.840 --> 01:02:34.230
But I guess I'm going to channel
Jim's counterfactual here.

01:02:34.230 --> 01:02:36.150
This is where you start
not where you finish

01:02:36.150 --> 01:02:38.370
'cause it's all right
articulating the risk,

01:02:38.370 --> 01:02:40.380
but if you look at the piece of paper,

01:02:40.380 --> 01:02:41.310
there's a lot of risk

01:02:41.310 --> 01:02:43.500
and a lot of them are 20 or 25 out of 25.

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:45.900
So having it written down and agreed

01:02:45.900 --> 01:02:47.220
is where we start, not where we finish,

01:02:47.220 --> 01:02:48.890
and I think the key pushback, I think,

01:02:48.890 --> 01:02:51.090
to the executive then, is to say,

01:02:51.090 --> 01:02:53.370
what are the mitigation plans around this?

01:02:53.370 --> 01:02:56.730
How do we take the 10 Year
Plan and mitigate these issues?

01:02:56.730 --> 01:02:58.230
We haven't got a lot of extra money,

01:02:58.230 --> 01:02:59.430
but how do we use capital?

01:02:59.430 --> 01:03:01.410
How do you use the money
we've currently got

01:03:01.410 --> 01:03:03.664
to mitigate these risks?

01:03:03.664 --> 01:03:05.820
Particularly when we look
at the delivery plan,

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:08.070
let's use this as an input
into the delivery plan

01:03:08.070 --> 01:03:11.130
to say what actions are we taking

01:03:11.130 --> 01:03:12.570
and over what timeframes

01:03:12.570 --> 01:03:14.430
do we expect these risks to be mitigated

01:03:14.430 --> 01:03:15.840
so that we can come back in a year's time

01:03:15.840 --> 01:03:18.847
and say, "That was 25. It's now 20.

01:03:18.847 --> 01:03:20.977
"It's now within our risk tolerance.

01:03:20.977 --> 01:03:21.870
"What progress we're making."

01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:25.170
Otherwise this becomes a
document that sits on the shelf,

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:27.090
or we talk about it every
two or three months,

01:03:27.090 --> 01:03:28.440
but it's not actually serving any use.

01:03:28.440 --> 01:03:30.540
So, my challenge back is to say

01:03:30.540 --> 01:03:32.040
this is the start, not the end.

01:03:32.040 --> 01:03:33.540
It's a good piece of work,

01:03:33.540 --> 01:03:35.010
but let's now use this in anger

01:03:35.010 --> 01:03:37.323
to drive some real change.

01:03:37.323 --> 01:03:41.402
- So Jeremy, as a chair of that committee,

01:03:41.402 --> 01:03:43.080
how would you like to see that happen?

01:03:43.080 --> 01:03:45.553
- So I think I would really like to see

01:03:45.553 --> 01:03:48.300
a delivery plan against a 10 Year Plan,

01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:50.850
and I'd like to see that
delivery plan then converted

01:03:50.850 --> 01:03:52.740
into a plan against each of these areas

01:03:52.740 --> 01:03:55.800
to say these are the
actions we're going to take,

01:03:55.800 --> 01:03:58.740
some will be qualitative,
some will be quantitative,

01:03:58.740 --> 01:03:59.940
and these are the measures

01:03:59.940 --> 01:04:03.240
that I think that we're
aiming for within the year,

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:04.560
that we are aiming for

01:04:04.560 --> 01:04:08.160
to say this is how we've
got this from 25 to 20.

01:04:08.160 --> 01:04:11.370
And then I'd like to see each
of the committees, not ARAC,

01:04:11.370 --> 01:04:14.100
the committees manage those outcomes,

01:04:14.100 --> 01:04:16.470
and then we can look at the
overall process around that

01:04:16.470 --> 01:04:17.303
to say checking.

01:04:17.303 --> 01:04:19.500
So ARAC will not take responsibility

01:04:19.500 --> 01:04:22.290
for cyber risk or workforce management,

01:04:22.290 --> 01:04:24.810
but we'd like to see the
other committees making sure

01:04:24.810 --> 01:04:27.240
they're taking those actions in place

01:04:27.240 --> 01:04:30.090
and mitigating the risks within
the committee structures.

01:04:30.090 --> 01:04:32.220
That would be my proposed approach.

01:04:32.220 --> 01:04:34.317
- So I'd strongly endorse that.

01:04:34.317 --> 01:04:38.490
But also going back to David's
point on this being live,

01:04:38.490 --> 01:04:41.520
a month ago and talking
about industrial action,

01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:43.557
and, you know, there'll be
other risks that pop out,

01:04:43.557 --> 01:04:46.560
and all of a sudden become
the real big thing for us.

01:04:46.560 --> 01:04:50.010
So how are we going to have
this as dynamic as that,

01:04:50.010 --> 01:04:52.470
and how are we going to
work through that, David?

01:04:52.470 --> 01:04:53.303
What's the...

01:04:53.303 --> 01:04:55.590
- Yeah, well I think we currently work

01:04:55.590 --> 01:04:57.720
on a quarterly basis,

01:04:57.720 --> 01:04:59.730
but I think, given some of the live risks,

01:04:59.730 --> 01:05:02.070
John and I will maybe keep a closer eye

01:05:02.070 --> 01:05:03.390
on how we report this.

01:05:03.390 --> 01:05:04.412
Obviously up to the board,

01:05:04.412 --> 01:05:07.290
we don't want to be changing
it regularly every month.

01:05:07.290 --> 01:05:08.670
I think, just going back

01:05:08.670 --> 01:05:10.650
to the live comments
of your points, Jeremy,

01:05:10.650 --> 01:05:12.537
when you look through that
operational risk register

01:05:12.537 --> 01:05:14.010
and the strategic one,

01:05:14.010 --> 01:05:17.130
when I think about all of
our last executive meetings,

01:05:17.130 --> 01:05:18.807
it is all this stuff
that we're talking about

01:05:18.807 --> 01:05:20.160
and the mitigation.

01:05:20.160 --> 01:05:21.210
So you're absolutely right.

01:05:21.210 --> 01:05:22.887
We need to find a way to capture that.

01:05:22.887 --> 01:05:24.030
The 10 Year Plan,

01:05:24.030 --> 01:05:26.220
which of course we're going to
come on to talk about later,

01:05:26.220 --> 01:05:28.110
the eight work streams underway.

01:05:28.110 --> 01:05:29.640
I think it's important that these risks

01:05:29.640 --> 01:05:30.473
are highlighted and triangulated

01:05:30.473 --> 01:05:32.010
in the way you've described.

01:05:32.010 --> 01:05:35.090
So we'll get those groups
to help us do that as well.

01:05:35.090 --> 01:05:36.105
So you get both things.
- It's not like he's taking

01:05:36.105 --> 01:05:36.938
calls from

01:05:36.938 --> 01:05:37.771
- Absolutely.

01:05:37.771 --> 01:05:38.640
And it's a pointless document

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:41.250
if you don't make it feel live and real.

01:05:41.250 --> 01:05:42.083
Absolutely.

01:05:42.083 --> 01:05:42.954
- [Jeremy] Thank you.

01:05:42.954 --> 01:05:43.787
- Mark.

01:05:43.787 --> 01:05:45.940
- Could I reinforce something from Jeremy

01:05:47.670 --> 01:05:50.940
and reflect back to the
amazing work that's been done

01:05:50.940 --> 01:05:52.990
around finance and financial performance?

01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:56.433
A lot of these risks are
behavioural in nature,

01:05:57.330 --> 01:06:01.800
and it's when we,

01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:03.120
if I take cyber

01:06:03.120 --> 01:06:08.120
or the ability to take
advantage of digital technology,

01:06:08.160 --> 01:06:11.010
a lot of what holds us back today

01:06:11.010 --> 01:06:13.650
is too much choice,

01:06:13.650 --> 01:06:14.970
a lack of a coherent view,

01:06:14.970 --> 01:06:16.440
and a lack of a collective belief

01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:18.090
about what wants to be delivered.

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:20.940
That's really a cultural issue

01:06:20.940 --> 01:06:23.280
not a technical issue
or a cyber risk issue,

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:26.470
and I would love to see

01:06:27.510 --> 01:06:31.440
the level of really,
really good performance

01:06:31.440 --> 01:06:32.550
you've had on financial

01:06:32.550 --> 01:06:34.320
and the belief driven into the system

01:06:34.320 --> 01:06:35.910
about we're going to compromise on this

01:06:35.910 --> 01:06:37.650
because we want the overall result

01:06:37.650 --> 01:06:39.480
to then be reflected
across the risk register,

01:06:39.480 --> 01:06:41.283
the delivery register as well.

01:06:42.840 --> 01:06:45.300
- So I think one of
the things that we need

01:06:45.300 --> 01:06:46.166
is that, as you said, Jeremy,

01:06:46.166 --> 01:06:49.950
is the risks allocated

01:06:49.950 --> 01:06:52.440
to each of our different board committees

01:06:52.440 --> 01:06:54.780
so that we can go into
more detail in those

01:06:54.780 --> 01:06:57.180
and then fully aligned to
the executive committee

01:06:57.180 --> 01:06:58.590
so that we can get assurance

01:06:58.590 --> 01:07:00.990
that they are being
systematically looked at

01:07:00.990 --> 01:07:02.790
and with clear mitigation plans

01:07:02.790 --> 01:07:04.060
'cause some of them,

01:07:04.060 --> 01:07:06.090
I think I know where they're picked up,

01:07:06.090 --> 01:07:08.343
some of them I'm not really sure.

01:07:09.540 --> 01:07:10.860
- Exactly. Exactly.

01:07:10.860 --> 01:07:13.050
So I think that feels like a key action.

01:07:13.050 --> 01:07:16.470
So, when is your next audit risk?

01:07:16.470 --> 01:07:17.303
- [Jeremy] Next week.

01:07:17.303 --> 01:07:18.136
- Okay, great. So,

01:07:19.509 --> 01:07:21.174
look forward to seeing
that the week after.

01:07:21.174 --> 01:07:23.414
- [Jeremy] No time like
the present, isn't it?

01:07:23.414 --> 01:07:24.247
- Okay, good.

01:07:24.247 --> 01:07:25.530
And thank you, John,

01:07:25.530 --> 01:07:27.636
for the work that you've
done supporting that.

01:07:27.636 --> 01:07:29.103
Okay. Good.

01:07:29.103 --> 01:07:31.950
- Penny, can I just raise
one point about the dates

01:07:31.950 --> 01:07:35.760
for the target score
that we're aiming for?

01:07:35.760 --> 01:07:36.647
I assume not all of them

01:07:36.647 --> 01:07:38.703
are at the end of the financial year.

01:07:40.440 --> 01:07:41.460
- [David] No, correct. They're not.

01:07:41.460 --> 01:07:43.110
So actually I think what we'll do on this

01:07:43.110 --> 01:07:45.690
is include the appropriate committee,

01:07:45.690 --> 01:07:48.420
be it executive or board,
where everything falls in

01:07:48.420 --> 01:07:52.140
and then just the
expected dates by quarter

01:07:52.140 --> 01:07:53.280
that we would expect the mitigation.

01:07:53.280 --> 01:07:55.350
- [Jeremy] I do think for
future planning, Andrew,

01:07:55.350 --> 01:07:56.640
I think it'd be really useful

01:07:56.640 --> 01:08:01.620
to align what the annual
plan is with the financing

01:08:01.620 --> 01:08:02.910
and then with the risk mitigation

01:08:02.910 --> 01:08:05.430
so that the whole thing is joined up.

01:08:05.430 --> 01:08:07.260
Otherwise, from a
management point of view,

01:08:07.260 --> 01:08:09.240
it gets very hard to work
out the different timeframes,

01:08:09.240 --> 01:08:11.460
and the timeframes can then tend to slip,

01:08:11.460 --> 01:08:12.690
and you never know.

01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:15.180
Even if it's these five things

01:08:15.180 --> 01:08:16.230
is what we're going to do in the year,

01:08:16.230 --> 01:08:17.700
we can then agree all that as a board

01:08:17.700 --> 01:08:18.680
as that's what the prioritisation is.

01:08:18.680 --> 01:08:20.190
it just helps with the whole process.

01:08:20.190 --> 01:08:24.270
So I would ask that, for
next year's planning cycle,

01:08:24.270 --> 01:08:25.680
we add the risk mitigation

01:08:25.680 --> 01:08:28.530
and the resource required against
it into the planning cycle

01:08:28.530 --> 01:08:31.710
so that there's at least some timeline

01:08:31.710 --> 01:08:32.850
against which you're measuring it.

01:08:32.850 --> 01:08:34.950
That will change depending
on the circumstances,

01:08:34.950 --> 01:08:36.330
and it should be a fluid plan,

01:08:36.330 --> 01:08:38.760
but at least we're all
working to an agenda,

01:08:38.760 --> 01:08:41.070
and we can use that as
a prioritisation model

01:08:41.070 --> 01:08:43.200
within the budgeting process as well.

01:08:43.200 --> 01:08:44.100
- No, understood.

01:08:44.100 --> 01:08:47.133
Noting that things like
industrial action is sideways.

01:08:48.074 --> 01:08:49.209
- [Jeremy] A plan is a plan

01:08:49.209 --> 01:08:50.089
to be changed.
- Absolutely.

01:08:50.089 --> 01:08:51.210
- [Jeremy] But at least you've got a plan.

01:08:51.210 --> 01:08:54.210
- [Penny] Yep. Okay, good.

01:08:54.210 --> 01:08:55.953
Winter planning. Sarah-Jane.

01:08:57.240 --> 01:08:58.073
- Thanks, Penny.

01:08:58.073 --> 01:09:00.120
As Jim said in his introduction,

01:09:00.120 --> 01:09:01.530
winter planning in summer.

01:09:01.530 --> 01:09:05.160
So the first thing to
say about the winter plan

01:09:05.160 --> 01:09:09.480
is it's really embedded in what
we do all year round in UEC.

01:09:09.480 --> 01:09:10.960
One of my favourite phrases is,

01:09:10.960 --> 01:09:13.800
UEC's definitely all year
round, not just for Christmas.

01:09:13.800 --> 01:09:16.350
So we have to be
interested in it every day

01:09:16.350 --> 01:09:20.490
because it's the underlying
systems, processes, and delivery

01:09:20.490 --> 01:09:23.070
that goes on throughout
each part of the pathway

01:09:23.070 --> 01:09:27.600
that we need to be able to
escalate during a winter period,

01:09:27.600 --> 01:09:30.450
not pull out a completely
different way of working.

01:09:30.450 --> 01:09:32.580
So the starting point really of this

01:09:32.580 --> 01:09:35.940
is making sure that the things
that we said we would do

01:09:35.940 --> 01:09:37.950
in 25/26, we do do.

01:09:37.950 --> 01:09:40.140
So very much in line with
other conversations we've had

01:09:40.140 --> 01:09:41.460
in the board today.

01:09:41.460 --> 01:09:45.870
We've got trajectories that
we've tracking really closely

01:09:45.870 --> 01:09:50.250
in terms of progress on
category 2 response times,

01:09:50.250 --> 01:09:53.550
4-hour performance, 12-hour performance,

01:09:53.550 --> 01:09:55.710
and also, as David mentioned,

01:09:55.710 --> 01:09:57.450
the link there with the handover delay

01:09:57.450 --> 01:09:59.130
given that can be such a big driver

01:09:59.130 --> 01:10:01.260
of Cat 2 response times.

01:10:01.260 --> 01:10:03.210
So that is an everyday job,

01:10:03.210 --> 01:10:06.420
and that's the foundation
stone of our winter planning.

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:08.520
But clearly on top of that then,

01:10:08.520 --> 01:10:10.500
we need to be able to escalate

01:10:10.500 --> 01:10:15.500
both in terms of the capacity
and the ways that we work

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:18.690
but also our ability to be able to respond

01:10:18.690 --> 01:10:20.370
and make decisions in a timely way

01:10:20.370 --> 01:10:23.460
because both of those
things are really important

01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:25.530
when we come under pressure.

01:10:25.530 --> 01:10:30.530
So we have worked with
colleagues in the system

01:10:30.540 --> 01:10:33.060
to design the way we do
winter planning this year.

01:10:33.060 --> 01:10:37.380
We've had two sessions in two
of the chief execs meetings,

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:40.320
and we've also worked with
chief operating officers as well

01:10:40.320 --> 01:10:43.027
to say, "How do we enable you

01:10:43.027 --> 01:10:45.757
"to have the best
conversations you can locally

01:10:45.757 --> 01:10:47.700
"about what you need to do?"

01:10:47.700 --> 01:10:50.040
So we've got some
structure and some sharing

01:10:50.040 --> 01:10:51.930
of important practise,

01:10:51.930 --> 01:10:54.960
but we are not weighing people
down in lots of requirements

01:10:54.960 --> 01:10:57.630
to fill in templates and spreadsheets,

01:10:57.630 --> 01:10:59.670
which is really important

01:10:59.670 --> 01:11:02.070
because there isn't really any template

01:11:02.070 --> 01:11:03.360
that gives us assurance

01:11:03.360 --> 01:11:04.290
that's really the thing

01:11:04.290 --> 01:11:07.920
that's going to help those
systems with their winter plans.

01:11:07.920 --> 01:11:09.990
So, earlier in the week,

01:11:09.990 --> 01:11:13.470
we shared with colleagues
across the system,

01:11:13.470 --> 01:11:14.410
the way that we're approaching this.

01:11:14.410 --> 01:11:17.100
We're asking people to
do their winter planning

01:11:17.100 --> 01:11:19.350
over the course of the next few weeks

01:11:19.350 --> 01:11:23.310
based on the UEC delivery
plan, the 25/26 delivery plan.

01:11:23.310 --> 01:11:25.380
That sets out really clearly

01:11:25.380 --> 01:11:28.140
what makes for a good effective UEC system

01:11:28.140 --> 01:11:29.670
within the constraints of how we work.

01:11:29.670 --> 01:11:30.630
Now obviously we're waiting

01:11:30.630 --> 01:11:32.820
for some of the more
transformational change

01:11:32.820 --> 01:11:34.470
through the 10 Year Plan.

01:11:34.470 --> 01:11:36.990
So organisations work through that now.

01:11:36.990 --> 01:11:40.890
We have produced one for
ICBs and one for trusts,

01:11:40.890 --> 01:11:44.190
a board assurance statement,

01:11:44.190 --> 01:11:47.160
and that sets out the key questions

01:11:47.160 --> 01:11:50.250
that we are asking the chairs
and non-executive directors

01:11:50.250 --> 01:11:52.413
to ask of executive colleagues.

01:11:53.295 --> 01:11:56.223
It's deliberately written
in an assurance way.

01:11:57.150 --> 01:11:59.310
We've also provided some prompts

01:11:59.310 --> 01:12:00.840
that can be followed up around that.

01:12:00.840 --> 01:12:02.250
So we're asking that that happens

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:05.820
over the course of the next few weeks,

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:08.073
and then, at the beginning of September,

01:12:08.073 --> 01:12:11.700
we are asking system and trust colleagues

01:12:11.700 --> 01:12:13.320
to come to a series

01:12:13.320 --> 01:12:16.440
of regionally-led stress-testing exercises

01:12:16.440 --> 01:12:18.540
where we're going to
use more of an approach

01:12:18.540 --> 01:12:19.897
that we would use in EPRR

01:12:20.760 --> 01:12:24.180
to actually test whether
those plans stand up

01:12:24.180 --> 01:12:27.180
to three different scenarios
that we have designed,

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:29.370
a kind of a moderate,

01:12:29.370 --> 01:12:30.960
well we're going to get
winter no matter what,

01:12:30.960 --> 01:12:33.810
but a sort of moderate scenario,

01:12:33.810 --> 01:12:35.370
a more severe one,

01:12:35.370 --> 01:12:37.650
and then an extreme case,

01:12:37.650 --> 01:12:41.310
so that we're clear that
those plans are resilient

01:12:41.310 --> 01:12:43.800
not just in terms of the
capacity that they build,

01:12:43.800 --> 01:12:45.177
but in terms of the response

01:12:45.177 --> 01:12:47.820
and the way that the system
and trust work together.

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:50.130
Given we have interchange
of operating model here,

01:12:50.130 --> 01:12:53.400
we don't want people to
suddenly be unsure what to do,

01:12:53.400 --> 01:12:55.260
you know, the third week in December.

01:12:55.260 --> 01:12:57.190
We want to test that out in September.

01:12:57.190 --> 01:12:59.553
We're then asking people to go back,

01:13:00.540 --> 01:13:02.670
ensure that they capture the lessons,

01:13:02.670 --> 01:13:04.980
and then sign off the
board assurance framework

01:13:04.980 --> 01:13:07.590
and return that to us
by the end of September.

01:13:07.590 --> 01:13:10.050
I am confident that it
only takes you 10 minutes

01:13:10.050 --> 01:13:12.120
to complete the framework.

01:13:12.120 --> 01:13:14.790
It's going to take quite a
long time to really do the work

01:13:14.790 --> 01:13:16.980
to know whether we can provide

01:13:16.980 --> 01:13:18.930
the assurance statements or not.

01:13:18.930 --> 01:13:22.050
Now there are a smaller
number of organisations.

01:13:22.050 --> 01:13:25.850
I think this links to Andrew's
point, what are we doing now,

01:13:25.850 --> 01:13:28.650
that we know we'll find
this really difficult.

01:13:28.650 --> 01:13:31.680
They've got challenging
UEC performance now.

01:13:31.680 --> 01:13:33.630
There's hospitals with ambulances outside

01:13:33.630 --> 01:13:34.860
for multiple hours now

01:13:34.860 --> 01:13:36.870
or with patients in corridors now

01:13:36.870 --> 01:13:38.730
before we even get to winter.

01:13:38.730 --> 01:13:42.870
So, in terms of our approach
there, it's twofold really.

01:13:42.870 --> 01:13:46.110
Again, one is just to
make it absolutely clear

01:13:46.110 --> 01:13:49.570
through, again, chairs,
also chief executives

01:13:50.430 --> 01:13:53.880
that the things that work, work.

01:13:53.880 --> 01:13:56.190
They work in the places
that get good performance.

01:13:56.190 --> 01:13:57.665
We all know what they are,

01:13:57.665 --> 01:14:00.454
and what we are looking
for is that consistency.

01:14:00.454 --> 01:14:01.593
Sorry, that might be my phone.

01:14:01.593 --> 01:14:03.720
What we're looking for is that consistency

01:14:03.720 --> 01:14:04.950
in the way these things are applied.

01:14:04.950 --> 01:14:07.680
Basically we need
delivery of your own plan.

01:14:07.680 --> 01:14:09.690
So part of it is about, you know,

01:14:09.690 --> 01:14:11.040
we really need to own this now.

01:14:11.040 --> 01:14:13.080
We need to see progress every day.

01:14:13.080 --> 01:14:14.460
But the second part of that

01:14:14.460 --> 01:14:16.110
is many of these organisations

01:14:16.110 --> 01:14:18.780
have got quite profound challenges

01:14:18.780 --> 01:14:22.050
in terms of some of the
clinical models of care,

01:14:22.050 --> 01:14:23.370
and therefore we've changed the way

01:14:23.370 --> 01:14:25.590
our improvement teams work this year

01:14:25.590 --> 01:14:29.370
to be able to give more
dedicated and focused support

01:14:29.370 --> 01:14:31.890
to organisations with
the biggest challenges.

01:14:31.890 --> 01:14:35.613
So we will be deploying
colleagues from ECIST,

01:14:36.660 --> 01:14:39.240
the Emergency Care Intensive Support Team,

01:14:39.240 --> 01:14:42.840
for at least three days a
week over a six-month period,

01:14:42.840 --> 01:14:45.600
so they can guide some
of those clinical teams

01:14:45.600 --> 01:14:46.500
through the changes.

01:14:46.500 --> 01:14:48.780
Most of the people in ECIST
are clinicians themselves,

01:14:48.780 --> 01:14:51.510
but they can guide
organisations through that.

01:14:51.510 --> 01:14:55.260
But I will keep a close
working relationship

01:14:55.260 --> 01:14:58.710
with those chief executives
and board members as well

01:14:58.710 --> 01:15:01.425
to make sure that they are
following the advice of ECIST

01:15:01.425 --> 01:15:05.010
'cause sometimes we've got
really strong suggestions

01:15:05.010 --> 01:15:06.990
of what needs to happen and change,

01:15:06.990 --> 01:15:09.600
but we don't see it.

01:15:09.600 --> 01:15:12.210
And Jim and I then have been
reinforcing that message

01:15:12.210 --> 01:15:14.250
with chairs and chief execs as well.

01:15:14.250 --> 01:15:18.360
So, in a way it's that combination

01:15:18.360 --> 01:15:20.520
of hands on and hands off
simultaneously, isn't it?

01:15:20.520 --> 01:15:21.600
We're taking a step back

01:15:21.600 --> 01:15:24.480
and allowing people to do this their way,

01:15:24.480 --> 01:15:27.240
but we are saying that this
is about really testing,

01:15:27.240 --> 01:15:29.250
responding, and being
honest with ourselves

01:15:29.250 --> 01:15:30.750
and taking corrective action

01:15:30.750 --> 01:15:33.630
rather than feeling
assured by a big document

01:15:33.630 --> 01:15:36.183
with loads of ticks in it.

01:15:37.260 --> 01:15:38.250
Thanks, Penny.

01:15:38.250 --> 01:15:39.510
- Okay. Thank you.

01:15:39.510 --> 01:15:43.260
So we've got Mark and then Mary.

01:15:43.260 --> 01:15:44.093
- [Mark Cubbon] I just wanted to add,

01:15:44.093 --> 01:15:45.780
obviously working very
closely with Sarah-Jane

01:15:45.780 --> 01:15:48.360
and the sessions that Sarah-Jane described

01:15:48.360 --> 01:15:50.400
with the chief execs last week,

01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:51.840
we did them together, didn't we,

01:15:51.840 --> 01:15:53.580
'cause we want to reinforce the message

01:15:53.580 --> 01:15:54.750
that the plan is not just about

01:15:54.750 --> 01:15:57.540
the urgent care performance delivery,

01:15:57.540 --> 01:15:59.820
it's also about keeping plans on track

01:15:59.820 --> 01:16:04.079
that we have for RTT waiting
list and for cancer as well.

01:16:04.079 --> 01:16:07.104
And we know quite a
number of organisations

01:16:07.104 --> 01:16:08.520
have built that into their plans,

01:16:08.520 --> 01:16:10.470
very sophisticated plans of this year,

01:16:10.470 --> 01:16:13.260
and others are still got some
modification I think to do

01:16:13.260 --> 01:16:15.450
to make sure we're trying to
bring as much work forward

01:16:15.450 --> 01:16:17.853
in the months where it's more protected.

01:16:19.410 --> 01:16:20.760
- [Penny] Okay. Mary.

01:16:20.760 --> 01:16:22.500
- I really wanted to ask what we're doing

01:16:22.500 --> 01:16:23.970
about discharge planning

01:16:23.970 --> 01:16:27.090
in order to get this to work

01:16:27.090 --> 01:16:29.940
because I'm aware that, as you've said,

01:16:29.940 --> 01:16:32.793
there's such variability
across the service,

01:16:34.170 --> 01:16:38.580
but my belief from some trusts,

01:16:38.580 --> 01:16:40.800
is that if you could get
discharge planning right

01:16:40.800 --> 01:16:45.800
by somebody doing it every day
right across the acute trust,

01:16:46.440 --> 01:16:51.440
you would free beds that
would then enable A&amp;E to cope

01:16:51.540 --> 01:16:53.673
and get ambulances back on the road.

01:16:54.593 --> 01:16:56.160
So is there a criteria

01:16:56.160 --> 01:16:58.770
that we're really pushing people on that?

01:16:58.770 --> 01:17:00.150
- Yeah, absolutely,

01:17:00.150 --> 01:17:01.950
both in terms of the discharge

01:17:01.950 --> 01:17:04.470
that the trust, the
organisations themselves, own

01:17:04.470 --> 01:17:06.255
which is the pathway that the majority,

01:17:06.255 --> 01:17:08.940
86% or so of patients leave on.

01:17:08.940 --> 01:17:11.820
So do we understand
the standards for that?

01:17:11.820 --> 01:17:12.870
That's one of the questions

01:17:12.870 --> 01:17:14.010
I'm sure some boards will be having

01:17:14.010 --> 01:17:15.210
lively conversations about,

01:17:15.210 --> 01:17:17.700
but do we understand
the standards for that,

01:17:17.700 --> 01:17:21.240
and is that appropriate,
especially at the weekends

01:17:21.240 --> 01:17:22.200
because what we see,

01:17:22.200 --> 01:17:23.730
we tend to see a tail off

01:17:23.730 --> 01:17:25.320
in the number of patients discharged

01:17:25.320 --> 01:17:26.520
which is why we get the problems

01:17:26.520 --> 01:17:27.420
at the beginning of the week.

01:17:27.420 --> 01:17:30.600
So, that's something
that we all need to own.

01:17:30.600 --> 01:17:32.280
But then, in terms of the other pathways,

01:17:32.280 --> 01:17:33.690
where people or patients

01:17:33.690 --> 01:17:35.790
need to leave hospital with support,

01:17:35.790 --> 01:17:38.070
do we have a completely
shared understanding

01:17:38.070 --> 01:17:40.440
between the trust, the community service,

01:17:40.440 --> 01:17:43.650
local authority, and social care providers

01:17:43.650 --> 01:17:46.620
about how that should be
happening in normal time,

01:17:46.620 --> 01:17:48.450
so what should be happening today,

01:17:48.450 --> 01:17:52.200
but how in each of those
three surge scenarios,

01:17:52.200 --> 01:17:56.490
we expect some of those to
also ramp up as part of this.

01:17:56.490 --> 01:17:58.770
And that was one of the
questions that we tested

01:17:58.770 --> 01:18:02.190
through the Better Care Fund
planning process this year

01:18:02.190 --> 01:18:05.460
so that there is an understanding

01:18:05.460 --> 01:18:08.550
of what we are expecting
to happen that's shared.

01:18:08.550 --> 01:18:10.200
I agree with what you're saying, Mary.

01:18:10.200 --> 01:18:15.150
Often this gets identified
as a capacity issue,

01:18:15.150 --> 01:18:18.570
when it's really related
to a process issue,

01:18:18.570 --> 01:18:20.250
whether that's a clinical process issue

01:18:20.250 --> 01:18:23.040
or a handoff issue between
different parts of the system.

01:18:23.040 --> 01:18:24.780
So the one thing we ask people

01:18:24.780 --> 01:18:26.790
to really focus their attention on

01:18:26.790 --> 01:18:29.220
is not just the number
of patients in hospital

01:18:29.220 --> 01:18:32.880
that don't meet the criteria
to reside, delayed discharge,

01:18:32.880 --> 01:18:34.890
but the days that then pass

01:18:34.890 --> 01:18:36.720
from the day that they're ready to leave.

01:18:36.720 --> 01:18:40.230
And the drive is to
try and get those down.

01:18:40.230 --> 01:18:41.760
Even if we can only reduce hours,

01:18:41.760 --> 01:18:43.800
it is the thing that creates flow,

01:18:43.800 --> 01:18:45.270
but if we can get the patients

01:18:45.270 --> 01:18:49.260
that are going home on home
care out within 24 hours,

01:18:49.260 --> 01:18:51.030
that can make a huge difference.

01:18:51.030 --> 01:18:52.410
So it's not trying to do

01:18:52.410 --> 01:18:54.420
really heroic and impossible things.

01:18:54.420 --> 01:18:56.880
It's trying to standardise the work

01:18:56.880 --> 01:18:59.370
and sort of reduce for
each of the pathways

01:18:59.370 --> 01:19:00.570
over a period of time.

01:19:00.570 --> 01:19:03.360
So it's definitely a key part of this,

01:19:03.360 --> 01:19:06.840
and we've asked, as part
of the assurance return,

01:19:06.840 --> 01:19:09.960
for the trust board to
understand their role in that,

01:19:09.960 --> 01:19:12.990
but for the system to understand
how it all links together

01:19:12.990 --> 01:19:15.420
and, in particular, how it's coordinated

01:19:15.420 --> 01:19:18.333
with local authority and social
care provider colleagues.

01:19:19.752 --> 01:19:21.870
And that includes for mental health.

01:19:21.870 --> 01:19:22.817
- So I know Claire and
Vin want to come in.

01:19:22.817 --> 01:19:24.150
Just a quick question for me.

01:19:24.150 --> 01:19:26.790
How far off are we having
all services available

01:19:26.790 --> 01:19:28.500
seven days a week?

01:19:28.500 --> 01:19:30.133
- [Sarah-Jane] Quite far.

01:19:30.133 --> 01:19:31.530
- And why?

01:19:31.530 --> 01:19:33.150
- There's significant...

01:19:33.150 --> 01:19:35.730
Well, it depends what services.

01:19:35.730 --> 01:19:38.010
I mean most hospitals
now have got the ability

01:19:38.010 --> 01:19:40.500
to do the core decision-making
seven days a week

01:19:40.500 --> 01:19:42.120
in terms of moving people forward

01:19:42.120 --> 01:19:46.560
on the P0 type of pathways,
although not as robustly.

01:19:46.560 --> 01:19:48.360
Sometimes there's only one ward round

01:19:48.360 --> 01:19:50.220
or one review in through the day

01:19:50.220 --> 01:19:52.260
rather than two for example.

01:19:52.260 --> 01:19:54.510
But quite a lot of the
other support services

01:19:54.510 --> 01:19:55.500
that sit behind that,

01:19:55.500 --> 01:19:57.450
the really important
clinical support services

01:19:57.450 --> 01:19:58.920
are less available.

01:19:58.920 --> 01:20:01.560
Whether that's some of the
routine therapies, for example,

01:20:01.560 --> 01:20:04.230
or some of the social work
assessments and so on,

01:20:04.230 --> 01:20:08.130
things do tend to tail off on a weekend.

01:20:08.130 --> 01:20:10.410
So I think Meghana's not here today,

01:20:10.410 --> 01:20:12.770
but one of the things
that she is going to lead

01:20:12.770 --> 01:20:13.860
is a piece of work

01:20:13.860 --> 01:20:16.020
on some of the things
that we've done previously

01:20:16.020 --> 01:20:17.560
around seven-day services

01:20:17.560 --> 01:20:19.710
and is there more that
we now should be doing

01:20:19.710 --> 01:20:22.020
certainly to standardise
the things we would expect

01:20:22.020 --> 01:20:23.220
seven days a week.

01:20:23.220 --> 01:20:27.540
Clearly there are really big
trusts that are sort of closer.

01:20:27.540 --> 01:20:29.230
There are smaller organisations

01:20:30.300 --> 01:20:31.440
where things are challenging.

01:20:31.440 --> 01:20:33.840
But what we always say,

01:20:33.840 --> 01:20:35.850
certainly when we do our
improvement interventions,

01:20:35.850 --> 01:20:38.040
is control what you can control,

01:20:38.040 --> 01:20:40.080
and if you keep your P0,

01:20:40.080 --> 01:20:42.240
your non-complex discharge going,

01:20:42.240 --> 01:20:45.690
even if you reduce down to 70% or 50%

01:20:45.690 --> 01:20:47.400
of what you normally do,

01:20:47.400 --> 01:20:49.410
that will be enough to maintain,

01:20:49.410 --> 01:20:52.920
but we can't have a tail off
where we only have, you know,

01:20:52.920 --> 01:20:55.440
single-figure patients
leaving hospital on a Sunday.

01:20:55.440 --> 01:20:56.910
And that does happen sometimes

01:20:56.910 --> 01:20:58.740
in some of our most challenged providers.

01:20:58.740 --> 01:21:00.901
- Okay. So can we keep an eye on that?

01:21:00.901 --> 01:21:01.734
- [Sarah-Jane] Yes.

01:21:01.734 --> 01:21:02.833
- 'Cause some of this is,

01:21:02.833 --> 01:21:07.380
I recognise local authorities
aren't within gift,

01:21:07.380 --> 01:21:09.630
but community services certainly are,

01:21:09.630 --> 01:21:11.133
primary care ought to be,

01:21:12.131 --> 01:21:14.640
and acute trust certainly are.

01:21:14.640 --> 01:21:16.890
So bit about how we keep an eye on that.

01:21:16.890 --> 01:21:17.723
- 100%, yeah.

01:21:17.723 --> 01:21:19.500
One of our key things again

01:21:19.500 --> 01:21:22.080
is we should not other problems away here.

01:21:22.080 --> 01:21:22.913
There's always things

01:21:22.913 --> 01:21:25.710
that other people could
do better that aren't us,

01:21:25.710 --> 01:21:27.750
but we don't other it away

01:21:27.750 --> 01:21:29.820
because it's our problem to solve.

01:21:29.820 --> 01:21:33.850
- I forgot. Claire and
then Vin and then Andrew.

01:21:33.850 --> 01:21:35.610
- So it's partly, just to carry on

01:21:35.610 --> 01:21:37.500
from the point you've
just made there actually,

01:21:37.500 --> 01:21:39.900
so at the chief exec event last Thursday,

01:21:39.900 --> 01:21:40.955
for the first time

01:21:40.955 --> 01:21:43.740
we had a significant number
of GP leaders in the room

01:21:43.740 --> 01:21:46.050
involved in the winter planning

01:21:46.050 --> 01:21:47.940
which completely alters the conversation

01:21:47.940 --> 01:21:49.380
around the way you have.

01:21:49.380 --> 01:21:51.810
Again, coming back to
the performance pack,

01:21:51.810 --> 01:21:53.790
although the numbers reflect

01:21:53.790 --> 01:21:56.520
on performance within the hospital,

01:21:56.520 --> 01:21:58.230
it's part of a dynamic system,

01:21:58.230 --> 01:22:01.080
and so the more we can
actually start to reflect

01:22:01.080 --> 01:22:03.240
the proactive care and the planning bit

01:22:03.240 --> 01:22:05.220
and how urgent care, the
majority of urgent care

01:22:05.220 --> 01:22:07.770
actually doesn't happen
within a hospital building,

01:22:07.770 --> 01:22:09.210
the more complete picture we'll get.

01:22:09.210 --> 01:22:11.190
But I think it changed the conversation

01:22:11.190 --> 01:22:13.740
by having different people
in the room on Thursday.

01:22:13.740 --> 01:22:15.270
- [Penny] Good. Vin?

01:22:15.270 --> 01:22:17.640
- Yeah, so obviously one of
the most important things

01:22:17.640 --> 01:22:19.080
is discharge is actually everybody

01:22:19.080 --> 01:22:21.120
in the multidisciplinary
team having access

01:22:21.120 --> 01:22:22.470
to the same data.

01:22:22.470 --> 01:22:24.570
So one of the earliest tools we developed

01:22:24.570 --> 01:22:26.460
in the Federated Data Platform

01:22:26.460 --> 01:22:28.590
was a platform where you
could see all your patients

01:22:28.590 --> 01:22:31.320
regardless of whether you
were a resident doctor

01:22:31.320 --> 01:22:32.880
or a discharge coordinator

01:22:32.880 --> 01:22:34.740
or someone working in social care.

01:22:34.740 --> 01:22:36.510
Everybody could see
where those patients were

01:22:36.510 --> 01:22:38.310
and where they are on the journey.

01:22:38.310 --> 01:22:39.143
Haven't forced it on people

01:22:39.143 --> 01:22:41.280
'cause if you've got a good
discharge system already,

01:22:41.280 --> 01:22:42.113
you don't need it,

01:22:42.113 --> 01:22:44.790
but it's available to
any trust in the country

01:22:44.790 --> 01:22:45.623
who wants to use it?

01:22:45.623 --> 01:22:47.047
- [Penny] What impact has that had?

01:22:47.047 --> 01:22:49.800
- So in the trusts where it's been used,

01:22:49.800 --> 01:22:52.740
we've seen a sort of double
percentage point reduction

01:22:52.740 --> 01:22:55.650
in the numbers of patients
who've been in hospital

01:22:55.650 --> 01:22:58.347
for longer than 21 days
where they've used it,

01:22:58.347 --> 01:23:00.150
and obviously the two things are connected

01:23:00.150 --> 01:23:02.850
'cause when you have data you
also redesign your processes.

01:23:02.850 --> 01:23:04.800
But we've seen that in a number of places.

01:23:04.800 --> 01:23:06.460
- [Penny] So how do we get
it into all the hospitals?

01:23:06.460 --> 01:23:08.850
- Well, Sarah-Jane and
I work closely together

01:23:08.850 --> 01:23:10.470
'cause we don't want to
foist tools on people

01:23:10.470 --> 01:23:11.430
if they've already got it,

01:23:11.430 --> 01:23:13.560
but where people haven't, it's available,

01:23:13.560 --> 01:23:15.810
and we can work with them to implement it.

01:23:15.810 --> 01:23:16.643
- [Sarah-Jane] Yeah.

01:23:17.605 --> 01:23:18.990
- To that question,

01:23:18.990 --> 01:23:22.380
do we have a correlation
between the maturity of adoption

01:23:22.380 --> 01:23:24.780
of the Federated Data Platform

01:23:24.780 --> 01:23:27.900
and trusts that are
struggling operationally,

01:23:27.900 --> 01:23:31.620
'cause having seen some
very, very good trusts

01:23:31.620 --> 01:23:33.780
with very high levels of adoption,

01:23:33.780 --> 01:23:36.453
intuitively that seems to be true.

01:23:37.800 --> 01:23:39.483
Have we actually laid it out?

01:23:40.380 --> 01:23:43.230
- There's been no study of
that specifically, Mark,

01:23:43.230 --> 01:23:45.630
but I am absolutely confident

01:23:45.630 --> 01:23:49.320
that it's the organisations
that are using data

01:23:49.320 --> 01:23:50.850
and have a systematic approach

01:23:50.850 --> 01:23:52.800
to the way that they're
controlling across pathways,

01:23:52.800 --> 01:23:55.020
they can see each other's
data and so forth.

01:23:55.020 --> 01:23:56.640
I mean the best example of this

01:23:56.640 --> 01:23:58.620
is Maidstone and Tunbridge Wells.

01:23:58.620 --> 01:24:01.770
Their control system, I mean
it's absolutely exceptional.

01:24:01.770 --> 01:24:04.650
I can see some of my chief exec colleagues

01:24:04.650 --> 01:24:06.150
looking at me saying, "No, my hospital."

01:24:06.150 --> 01:24:07.110
No, they're amazing.

01:24:08.077 --> 01:24:10.260
I mean all the hospitals
are amazing as well,

01:24:10.260 --> 01:24:12.540
and I'm sure there are many others.

01:24:12.540 --> 01:24:15.300
But anyway, yes, that's
a brilliant example

01:24:15.300 --> 01:24:17.850
of how data has absolutely transformed

01:24:17.850 --> 01:24:21.240
right down to knowing that
a bed is being cleaned

01:24:21.240 --> 01:24:23.580
and is going to be available
in the next half an hour.

01:24:23.580 --> 01:24:25.380
Therefore a patient can be pulled through

01:24:25.380 --> 01:24:26.730
and waiting to go on the ward.

01:24:26.730 --> 01:24:29.580
But not all of those systems do use

01:24:29.580 --> 01:24:30.885
the Federated Data Platform

01:24:30.885 --> 01:24:31.830
which I think is Vin's point,

01:24:31.830 --> 01:24:35.280
is it may be unwelcome at
this stage if we were to...

01:24:35.280 --> 01:24:36.750
- Yeah, if you've got a good system,

01:24:36.750 --> 01:24:37.737
we wouldn't force it on you.

01:24:37.737 --> 01:24:39.840
But if Sarah-Jane's team
identifies somewhere

01:24:39.840 --> 01:24:41.040
that's not using data,

01:24:41.040 --> 01:24:42.990
there is a tool that's freely available,

01:24:42.990 --> 01:24:45.220
and you don't have to
purchase it, that can be used.

01:24:45.220 --> 01:24:48.317
- So it comes back to what
are the top 10 actions?

01:24:48.317 --> 01:24:49.150
- Mmm.
- Yeah.

01:24:49.150 --> 01:24:53.253
- And do we make some of them unoptional?

01:24:53.253 --> 01:24:56.700
- We have a top 12 action list which we,

01:24:56.700 --> 01:24:59.160
in our better performers,
we see people do,

01:24:59.160 --> 01:25:02.670
and this is what ECIST
essentially take that two people

01:25:02.670 --> 01:25:05.733
and start working it through with them.

01:25:07.320 --> 01:25:10.650
But, yeah, there's quite
significant cultural issues

01:25:10.650 --> 01:25:11.850
in quite a lot of these places

01:25:11.850 --> 01:25:13.650
that struggle to implement what works.

01:25:13.650 --> 01:25:15.567
- Okay, but there may be something about,

01:25:15.567 --> 01:25:16.830
and we'll hand over to Andrew,

01:25:16.830 --> 01:25:21.420
but, you know, if we've got
a list of the top 12 actions,

01:25:21.420 --> 01:25:24.807
if we could identify the trusts
that are not adopting those,

01:25:24.807 --> 01:25:28.740
and I think a phone call
from Jim to the chief exec

01:25:28.740 --> 01:25:31.350
followed by a phone call
from me to the chair,

01:25:31.350 --> 01:25:33.990
might make the point that
we absolutely expect them

01:25:33.990 --> 01:25:36.420
to adopt the top 12 actions.

01:25:36.420 --> 01:25:37.917
- And we've shared them
directly, haven't we, Jim?

01:25:37.917 --> 01:25:41.370
Jim and I met with a few
chairs and chief execs,

01:25:41.370 --> 01:25:43.950
and part of that was actually explaining

01:25:43.950 --> 01:25:45.240
that these resources were there,

01:25:45.240 --> 01:25:47.940
and our expectation was
that they were being used

01:25:47.940 --> 01:25:50.010
because they do work.

01:25:50.010 --> 01:25:51.900
I know people are always
looking for silver bullets,

01:25:51.900 --> 01:25:54.390
but, you know, if you align
your staffing to your demand,

01:25:54.390 --> 01:25:57.510
which I'm sure is going to
be Andrew's next question,

01:25:57.510 --> 01:25:59.040
then, I can preempt,

01:25:59.040 --> 01:26:00.900
then, you know, that works.

01:26:00.900 --> 01:26:01.733
- [Board member] Another one there.

01:26:01.733 --> 01:26:02.850
- [Sarah-Jane] Who knew?

01:26:02.850 --> 01:26:05.943
- So I think this is a
really good opportunity

01:26:05.943 --> 01:26:07.650
that we've given boards

01:26:07.650 --> 01:26:10.500
to really, you know, button down

01:26:10.500 --> 01:26:13.620
what the arrangements are
for it for the winter,

01:26:13.620 --> 01:26:15.960
and having to sign this off

01:26:15.960 --> 01:26:18.690
prompts a whole series of conversations

01:26:18.690 --> 01:26:22.800
which is the best way that
you can kind of reinvent this

01:26:22.800 --> 01:26:25.230
that I can think of.

01:26:25.230 --> 01:26:27.930
So, you know, really brilliant idea

01:26:27.930 --> 01:26:30.510
in terms of, you know, getting
boards to have that debate,

01:26:30.510 --> 01:26:33.390
and, being on a trust board,

01:26:33.390 --> 01:26:36.000
I know that this conversation
is being, you know,

01:26:36.000 --> 01:26:37.863
taken very, very seriously.

01:26:38.910 --> 01:26:43.910
So there's some big challenges
in here including, you know,

01:26:44.070 --> 01:26:46.410
the senior decision
makers on the front door

01:26:46.410 --> 01:26:48.540
which I've got to think about,

01:26:48.540 --> 01:26:51.883
which I think most
organisations will debate

01:26:51.883 --> 01:26:52.716
and work through.

01:26:52.716 --> 01:26:55.260
So the chances are that this will elevate

01:26:55.260 --> 01:26:58.530
to kind of the top of
the list or second top

01:26:58.530 --> 01:27:00.563
if finance is the biggest challenge

01:27:00.563 --> 01:27:02.670
in a particular organisation,

01:27:02.670 --> 01:27:05.010
which is a really welcome
kind of innovation.

01:27:05.010 --> 01:27:06.153
So well done for that.

01:27:07.643 --> 01:27:09.120
- And it did genuinely.

01:27:09.120 --> 01:27:12.330
It's a piecing together of some things

01:27:12.330 --> 01:27:14.760
that we've learned from
the elective programme,

01:27:14.760 --> 01:27:18.840
but a big part of it was actually
the chief execs themselves

01:27:18.840 --> 01:27:22.530
saying, "This is how we do
X, Y, Z in our organisation."

01:27:22.530 --> 01:27:25.530
Like the idea of the
planning on the three levels

01:27:25.530 --> 01:27:26.850
came directly from something

01:27:26.850 --> 01:27:29.010
that a high-performing trust does.

01:27:29.010 --> 01:27:32.940
So I think it just shows the
power of kind of co-designing,

01:27:32.940 --> 01:27:34.500
and I was able to send this out

01:27:34.500 --> 01:27:36.570
72 hours after the chief execs event

01:27:36.570 --> 01:27:38.497
to say, "Look what we've, you know,

01:27:38.497 --> 01:27:40.290
"designed together essentially."

01:27:40.290 --> 01:27:42.060
So it does sort of hopefully changes

01:27:42.060 --> 01:27:43.530
the way it's been received.

01:27:43.530 --> 01:27:44.850
I haven't had a complaint about it yet,

01:27:44.850 --> 01:27:46.859
which is unlike any other
correspondence I've sent.

01:27:48.540 --> 01:27:49.417
I keep asking every day,

01:27:49.417 --> 01:27:50.910
"Has anybody complained about it yet?"

01:27:50.910 --> 01:27:52.110
But not so far.

01:27:52.110 --> 01:27:54.150
So I think that's, yeah,

01:27:54.150 --> 01:27:55.650
hopefully it will be really helpful.

01:27:55.650 --> 01:27:56.483
- Okay, great.

01:27:56.483 --> 01:27:59.280
So it'd be good to see,
at the next board meeting,

01:27:59.280 --> 01:28:01.560
which will be in early autumn,

01:28:01.560 --> 01:28:03.060
the list of organisations

01:28:03.060 --> 01:28:07.470
that haven't adopted the top 12 action.

01:28:07.470 --> 01:28:09.690
- Yeah. Absolutely, Penny.

01:28:09.690 --> 01:28:14.147
We can perhaps align that then
with the performance data.

01:28:14.147 --> 01:28:17.790
'cause we tend to be
more stressed about that

01:28:17.790 --> 01:28:18.810
if there's not performance.

01:28:18.810 --> 01:28:20.520
If people have found a
different way then...

01:28:20.520 --> 01:28:21.600
- Yeah, absolutely.

01:28:21.600 --> 01:28:24.720
But that in itself, it would
be interesting to see, yeah.

01:28:24.720 --> 01:28:26.160
- [Sarah-Jane] 100%.

01:28:26.160 --> 01:28:27.070
- Okay. Good

01:28:28.320 --> 01:28:29.970
Pandemic preparedness.

01:28:29.970 --> 01:28:34.050
- Yeah so just perhaps this
be a little briefer, Penny.

01:28:34.050 --> 01:28:37.440
There's just a couple of
things I want to highlight.

01:28:37.440 --> 01:28:40.515
The first is that the colleagues in DHSC

01:28:40.515 --> 01:28:43.570
are leading an overall
pandemic preparedness programme

01:28:44.926 --> 01:28:48.390
and a series of sort of
sub-pandemic response plans

01:28:48.390 --> 01:28:51.120
for various different respiratory

01:28:51.120 --> 01:28:54.030
or airborne pandemic scenarios.

01:28:54.030 --> 01:28:57.240
We're obviously working
closely with them as partners,

01:28:57.240 --> 01:28:58.500
and the first one of those,

01:28:58.500 --> 01:29:01.170
which is focused on respiratory,

01:29:01.170 --> 01:29:04.890
is the first that will emerge.

01:29:04.890 --> 01:29:08.880
It obviously very relates
to the recommendations

01:29:08.880 --> 01:29:11.370
and the early sort of
conversations and findings

01:29:11.370 --> 01:29:13.350
from the COVID inquiry as well.

01:29:13.350 --> 01:29:15.480
So it's a really important document,

01:29:15.480 --> 01:29:18.180
and it remains one of the
highest risks I think overall

01:29:18.180 --> 01:29:19.780
on the government Risk Register.

01:29:20.820 --> 01:29:24.540
I guess where we have that particular role

01:29:24.540 --> 01:29:27.600
is that we are testing our own ability

01:29:27.600 --> 01:29:30.270
to be able to respond
in a pandemic scenario,

01:29:30.270 --> 01:29:32.640
and there is a big
cross-government exercise

01:29:32.640 --> 01:29:35.130
due to take place from
September to November

01:29:35.130 --> 01:29:37.110
called Exercise Pegasus.

01:29:37.110 --> 01:29:39.450
So it's a Tier 1 exercise

01:29:39.450 --> 01:29:42.630
which will involve
ministerial participation,

01:29:42.630 --> 01:29:45.600
COBRA activation, and so forth.

01:29:45.600 --> 01:29:48.510
It's led by DHSC and UKHSA,

01:29:48.510 --> 01:29:50.400
but we're really important
partners in that,

01:29:50.400 --> 01:29:53.640
and we've committed that
several members of the executive

01:29:53.640 --> 01:29:55.650
will fully participate in that one

01:29:55.650 --> 01:29:59.550
so we can test some of our own plans,

01:29:59.550 --> 01:30:01.890
but also we're going to
learn a significant amount.

01:30:01.890 --> 01:30:06.360
We had a warmup exercise,
Alkarab a few weeks ago,

01:30:06.360 --> 01:30:08.160
which some of us attended,

01:30:08.160 --> 01:30:09.780
and there was significant learning

01:30:09.780 --> 01:30:11.340
just from that warmup exercise

01:30:11.340 --> 01:30:13.860
in terms of sort of the resilience

01:30:13.860 --> 01:30:15.990
of some of our plans and responses

01:30:15.990 --> 01:30:20.250
based on all different
sorts of flu scenarios.

01:30:20.250 --> 01:30:22.170
So that is due to take place.

01:30:22.170 --> 01:30:24.330
It won't be a heavy
burden on the NHS itself.

01:30:24.330 --> 01:30:27.120
It's more testing NHS England scenarios.

01:30:27.120 --> 01:30:28.440
But I think it's really important,

01:30:28.440 --> 01:30:29.640
one, the board's aware,

01:30:29.640 --> 01:30:31.225
and that we show our commitment to it

01:30:31.225 --> 01:30:33.090
'cause it's such an important exercise,

01:30:33.090 --> 01:30:34.890
but also we put a flag in need in

01:30:34.890 --> 01:30:36.450
to bring our lessons learned

01:30:36.450 --> 01:30:39.213
and increasingly as we
come together with DHSC,

01:30:41.265 --> 01:30:45.120
our risk register that we
hold between us at the moment

01:30:45.120 --> 01:30:49.560
is quite lively, I think would
be the way to describe it,

01:30:49.560 --> 01:30:52.830
in terms of some of the
things that are ongoing risks

01:30:52.830 --> 01:30:54.690
should we face a future pandemic scenario.

01:30:54.690 --> 01:30:56.700
I think it's really important
to bring those together

01:30:56.700 --> 01:30:59.040
that this board has
full sight of the areas

01:30:59.040 --> 01:31:01.203
and, like I say, I think
a good moment to do that

01:31:01.203 --> 01:31:03.510
will be once we've done the exercise,

01:31:03.510 --> 01:31:06.600
and we can test whether
our current understanding

01:31:06.600 --> 01:31:09.930
of our ability to respond is borne out

01:31:09.930 --> 01:31:11.553
as the exercise progresses.

01:31:13.613 --> 01:31:14.446
- Okay.

01:31:14.446 --> 01:31:18.480
- Sarah-Jane, one of the
issues in the last pandemic

01:31:18.480 --> 01:31:20.790
was the indirect impact on children,

01:31:20.790 --> 01:31:22.110
but if we have a pandemic

01:31:22.110 --> 01:31:24.000
that has more of a direct
impact on children,

01:31:24.000 --> 01:31:26.700
our ability to stand up PICU capacity

01:31:26.700 --> 01:31:29.067
is different to that
in terms of adult ICU.

01:31:29.067 --> 01:31:32.430
Are we confident that
this will test scenarios

01:31:32.430 --> 01:31:34.200
on children specifically

01:31:34.200 --> 01:31:37.170
'cause I think that's probably
one of the areas of concern

01:31:37.170 --> 01:31:39.930
that we would have for a future pandemic.

01:31:39.930 --> 01:31:41.820
- So I don't know the scenario

01:31:41.820 --> 01:31:44.332
because that's part of the going into it,

01:31:44.332 --> 01:31:46.920
that's the idea of going into it cold.

01:31:46.920 --> 01:31:50.370
We have got someone from here
that's part of the group.

01:31:50.370 --> 01:31:54.750
The warmup exercise did involve something

01:31:54.750 --> 01:31:59.400
that was actually having
a disproportionate impact

01:31:59.400 --> 01:32:01.140
on younger people,

01:32:01.140 --> 01:32:04.410
and the exposure that that
created was really quite clear

01:32:04.410 --> 01:32:06.000
not just in terms of our services

01:32:06.000 --> 01:32:07.080
for children and young people,

01:32:07.080 --> 01:32:10.233
but also in terms of our workforce.

01:32:11.430 --> 01:32:16.410
So I would be surprised
if it didn't, Duncan,

01:32:16.410 --> 01:32:18.600
but I think, either way,
that is one of the things

01:32:18.600 --> 01:32:23.600
that's quite high on our
risk registers of concern.

01:32:23.850 --> 01:32:24.900
- [Duncan] Okay.

01:32:24.900 --> 01:32:27.123
- Let's take other questions. Robert, Vin.

01:32:28.050 --> 01:32:29.967
- So two questions, Sarah-Jane.

01:32:29.967 --> 01:32:33.480
One is, those with a
long memory will remember

01:32:33.480 --> 01:32:35.490
Exercise Cygnus, I think it was,

01:32:35.490 --> 01:32:37.230
which was about 15 years ago,

01:32:37.230 --> 01:32:39.660
which was a pandemic simulation.

01:32:39.660 --> 01:32:41.460
It gave some false reassurance

01:32:41.460 --> 01:32:42.990
because it was very much based around flu,

01:32:42.990 --> 01:32:44.520
and of course the cussed thing about COVID

01:32:44.520 --> 01:32:47.100
was it was transmitted
before you're symptomatic.

01:32:47.100 --> 01:32:48.837
But I just wonder if
there's any lessons learned,

01:32:48.837 --> 01:32:50.790
now that someone's got a corporate memory,

01:32:50.790 --> 01:32:52.410
of how sickness went

01:32:52.410 --> 01:32:55.800
and whether we could improve
Pegasus as a consequence.

01:32:55.800 --> 01:32:56.730
The second question is,

01:32:56.730 --> 01:32:59.700
one of the commitments I recall being made

01:32:59.700 --> 01:33:00.870
as we emerged from COVID

01:33:00.870 --> 01:33:03.810
was that some key infrastructure,

01:33:03.810 --> 01:33:07.470
namely vaccine manufacture capacity

01:33:07.470 --> 01:33:10.050
and test and trace
capacity would be preserved

01:33:10.050 --> 01:33:12.000
not at a scale that it
was needed of course

01:33:12.000 --> 01:33:12.870
during the pandemic,

01:33:12.870 --> 01:33:16.530
but it could easily be
dusted off and re-expanded,

01:33:16.530 --> 01:33:18.300
and I just wonder if you can reassure us

01:33:18.300 --> 01:33:20.460
that some of those bits of infrastructure

01:33:20.460 --> 01:33:21.460
have been preserved.

01:33:24.210 --> 01:33:25.590
- So, on the latter point,

01:33:25.590 --> 01:33:28.650
this is a piece of work that
we're doing with the department

01:33:28.650 --> 01:33:30.420
in preparation for the exercise,

01:33:30.420 --> 01:33:32.520
which is what what would it take

01:33:32.520 --> 01:33:34.110
to stand up some of those responses

01:33:34.110 --> 01:33:37.140
and what would be the timeliness of those

01:33:37.140 --> 01:33:41.370
given where we are with the
infrastructure that we had.

01:33:41.370 --> 01:33:45.090
So I can't give you a confident yes,

01:33:45.090 --> 01:33:46.760
but I can give you an absolutely-

01:33:46.760 --> 01:33:47.593
- [Robert] It's on the radar.

01:33:47.593 --> 01:33:48.900
- That's hugely on the radar,

01:33:48.900 --> 01:33:52.170
and I've began meeting
regularly with Catherine Frances

01:33:52.170 --> 01:33:55.380
is the kind of counterpoint
in the department,

01:33:55.380 --> 01:33:58.290
to make sure we've got
the same understanding

01:33:58.290 --> 01:34:00.420
of what the absolute
number one risks would be.

01:34:00.420 --> 01:34:02.670
And, as for the corporate memory,

01:34:02.670 --> 01:34:05.190
I don't recall that
specific exercise myself,

01:34:05.190 --> 01:34:09.220
but yes, because I think one
of the things that has caused

01:34:10.819 --> 01:34:12.130
a big acceleration of this

01:34:12.130 --> 01:34:14.880
is it has been so long
since an exercise like this

01:34:14.880 --> 01:34:16.500
has been undertaken.

01:34:16.500 --> 01:34:18.390
So things have been looked back on

01:34:18.390 --> 01:34:20.940
to say, "What are our
particular risks and exposures,"

01:34:20.940 --> 01:34:23.010
and that's gone into the
design of this exercise.

01:34:23.010 --> 01:34:25.350
As I say, it's been designed
by a series of experts,

01:34:25.350 --> 01:34:29.250
but those who participate
are not clear what it is yet.

01:34:29.250 --> 01:34:30.083
- Thanks.

01:34:30.083 --> 01:34:32.300
- Vin and then Jeremy and then Jim.

01:34:33.557 --> 01:34:34.920
- It was a question really.

01:34:34.920 --> 01:34:37.350
So one of the things that we had to do

01:34:37.350 --> 01:34:39.210
during the COVID-19 pandemic

01:34:39.210 --> 01:34:43.920
was actually significantly
upgrade our data systems

01:34:43.920 --> 01:34:46.350
because, until the pandemic
came along, for example,

01:34:46.350 --> 01:34:48.330
we didn't have an end-to-end tool

01:34:48.330 --> 01:34:51.960
for looking at the whole
end-to-end vaccine supply chain.

01:34:51.960 --> 01:34:53.880
And I just wondered, 'cause
it's not in the paper,

01:34:53.880 --> 01:34:56.040
whether any of these are going to look at

01:34:56.040 --> 01:34:58.743
our data infrastructure
and data requirements.

01:35:00.450 --> 01:35:02.190
- [Penny] Right, let's take
the other questions. Jeremy.

01:35:02.190 --> 01:35:03.480
- Yeah, so having just been through

01:35:03.480 --> 01:35:05.130
a massive cyber incident at M&amp;S,

01:35:05.130 --> 01:35:07.770
it'd be very remiss of me
not to say this is great,

01:35:07.770 --> 01:35:09.597
but it's probably one for DDaT

01:35:09.597 --> 01:35:12.870
to have a think about a
similar exercise around cyber

01:35:12.870 --> 01:35:14.550
that involves not just NHSE

01:35:14.550 --> 01:35:18.764
but ministers, other
third-party groups, I think.

01:35:18.764 --> 01:35:19.920
I would just say,

01:35:19.920 --> 01:35:21.450
you can protect all you like,

01:35:21.450 --> 01:35:24.027
but until you get to the DR side of it,

01:35:24.027 --> 01:35:24.930
you don't know what you don't know.

01:35:24.930 --> 01:35:27.870
So just a thought that if we
can do something like this,

01:35:27.870 --> 01:35:29.373
something similar on cyber.

01:35:30.300 --> 01:35:32.220
- [David] Get into an
update where we are on that.

01:35:32.220 --> 01:35:35.640
- Yeah, so firstly, they've
just done an exercise

01:35:35.640 --> 01:35:38.250
in the southeast region,
not involving ministers,

01:35:38.250 --> 01:35:40.050
but doing everything
else you've described.

01:35:40.050 --> 01:35:43.170
And then, as part of resilience planning,

01:35:43.170 --> 01:35:46.140
last year we wrote out
to all resilience teams

01:35:46.140 --> 01:35:47.580
and said there were seven scenarios

01:35:47.580 --> 01:35:49.770
we wanted them to exercise
over a fixed period,

01:35:49.770 --> 01:35:52.080
and one of them was a cyber outage.

01:35:52.080 --> 01:35:54.630
The particular one of
interest in the southeast

01:35:54.630 --> 01:35:58.650
was they looked for the
impact of a prolonged outage

01:35:58.650 --> 01:36:00.090
as a result of a cyber attack

01:36:00.090 --> 01:36:01.440
because of course as you know,

01:36:01.440 --> 01:36:03.510
and as you found in Marks and Spencers,

01:36:03.510 --> 01:36:05.370
you can't just deal with a cyber attack

01:36:05.370 --> 01:36:06.600
and switch the system back on.

01:36:06.600 --> 01:36:08.460
It can take several months to rebuild it.

01:36:08.460 --> 01:36:10.530
So your resilience planning needs to cover

01:36:10.530 --> 01:36:13.440
several months of disruption,
not just a short period.

01:36:13.440 --> 01:36:14.273
- [Mark Bailie] What we're going to do,

01:36:14.273 --> 01:36:15.990
is we're going to take that southeast,

01:36:15.990 --> 01:36:18.840
which is effectively a
ransomware simulation,

01:36:18.840 --> 01:36:19.740
- [Jeremy] Yeah.

01:36:19.740 --> 01:36:20.910
- [Mark Bailie] take
the lessons from there,

01:36:20.910 --> 01:36:22.440
and then come back with
some recommendations

01:36:22.440 --> 01:36:23.760
about what else should be done.

01:36:23.760 --> 01:36:25.290
- [Jeremy] That was going to
be my follow up point for this,

01:36:25.290 --> 01:36:27.840
and for that to feed
those recommendations back

01:36:27.840 --> 01:36:29.970
into the mitigation plans
and the risk registers.

01:36:29.970 --> 01:36:31.290
Otherwise, again, that's an exercise

01:36:31.290 --> 01:36:33.360
that's going on outside.

01:36:33.360 --> 01:36:34.290
- [Penny] I mean I think this is flagging.

01:36:34.290 --> 01:36:36.360
We need to come back on this
with quite a few things.

01:36:36.360 --> 01:36:38.370
So I think you wanted to come in Tom,

01:36:38.370 --> 01:36:40.620
and then I'll go to Jim,
and then we'll finish.

01:36:40.620 --> 01:36:42.210
- Yeah, I just wanted to ask about

01:36:42.210 --> 01:36:44.430
what the definition of success is

01:36:44.430 --> 01:36:47.010
in terms of managing the response.

01:36:47.010 --> 01:36:51.600
So in the Darzi investigation,

01:36:51.600 --> 01:36:55.320
it highlighted the fact that
the NHS was a real outlier

01:36:55.320 --> 01:36:59.190
in terms of the amount of
routine care that was cancelled

01:36:59.190 --> 01:37:03.570
during the pandemic by a
very significant degree.

01:37:03.570 --> 01:37:06.311
And so when you have
an exercise like this,

01:37:06.311 --> 01:37:09.210
would it look at the ability
to manage the response

01:37:09.210 --> 01:37:11.760
to the pandemic threat,

01:37:11.760 --> 01:37:15.660
but would it also look at
the consequential impacts

01:37:15.660 --> 01:37:18.150
on routine care and the
overall health and care

01:37:18.150 --> 01:37:19.323
for the population?

01:37:21.240 --> 01:37:22.170
- That's the question.

01:37:22.170 --> 01:37:24.840
- So I was going to make
partly the same point.

01:37:24.840 --> 01:37:27.600
Many of us will remember vividly forever

01:37:27.600 --> 01:37:30.390
the experience of the buildup

01:37:30.390 --> 01:37:32.933
in sort of February, March, 2020

01:37:32.933 --> 01:37:34.230
and then when it kicked off.

01:37:34.230 --> 01:37:35.063
And from my point of view,

01:37:35.063 --> 01:37:38.280
the three things I would've always hoped

01:37:38.280 --> 01:37:40.260
that were better next time than last time.

01:37:40.260 --> 01:37:44.070
So one was the absence
of PPE, especially gowns.

01:37:44.070 --> 01:37:47.370
Never forget the fear of
knowing that we didn't have any

01:37:47.370 --> 01:37:50.340
and being told that we
wouldn't have any at peak time.

01:37:50.340 --> 01:37:52.680
Our inability to test,

01:37:52.680 --> 01:37:54.690
and, again, relative to other countries

01:37:54.690 --> 01:37:59.690
that were unable to mobilise
quickly a testing response.

01:38:00.510 --> 01:38:02.070
And then also, as Tom's just said,

01:38:02.070 --> 01:38:04.110
the broader impact and the frustration

01:38:04.110 --> 01:38:06.660
of seeing the impact build up

01:38:06.660 --> 01:38:08.500
whilst the activity was curtailed

01:38:11.190 --> 01:38:13.230
out of line with other systems.

01:38:13.230 --> 01:38:15.000
There's got to be some
learning in all of that.

01:38:15.000 --> 01:38:15.960
So we've got the inquiry.

01:38:15.960 --> 01:38:18.057
We've got all kinds of
other processes going on.

01:38:18.057 --> 01:38:21.300
For me, I need to feel more assured

01:38:21.300 --> 01:38:23.190
that if this happens again,

01:38:23.190 --> 01:38:24.690
which it will at some point,

01:38:24.690 --> 01:38:26.850
we're better prepared
than we were last time.

01:38:26.850 --> 01:38:28.380
- Yep.

01:38:28.380 --> 01:38:29.213
- Just briefly, Penny,

01:38:29.213 --> 01:38:33.240
it definitely looks at wider
societal and wider impacts

01:38:33.240 --> 01:38:38.240
than whatever's immediately
presenting as the issue.

01:38:38.430 --> 01:38:40.230
And in the warmup exercise,

01:38:40.230 --> 01:38:41.730
this was a huge, huge issue

01:38:41.730 --> 01:38:46.710
because society's response
would clearly be very different

01:38:46.710 --> 01:38:49.230
based on having been through
a pandemic previously.

01:38:49.230 --> 01:38:52.230
So even the sort of starting point of it

01:38:52.230 --> 01:38:53.973
became quite problematic,

01:38:55.140 --> 01:38:56.100
children in school, you know,

01:38:56.100 --> 01:38:57.390
there's a whole range of things.

01:38:57.390 --> 01:38:59.160
So it definitely goes broader.

01:38:59.160 --> 01:39:02.220
As I say, I'm not sure what it is.

01:39:02.220 --> 01:39:03.840
To go to Jim's point,

01:39:03.840 --> 01:39:07.170
yeah, I think the key things

01:39:07.170 --> 01:39:09.840
are having conversations
with DHSC colleagues about,

01:39:09.840 --> 01:39:11.550
and, in particular,
whether there are things

01:39:11.550 --> 01:39:13.800
that we should be doing
that are no regrets anyway

01:39:13.800 --> 01:39:15.240
because, as you know,

01:39:15.240 --> 01:39:16.410
part of the issue with testing

01:39:16.410 --> 01:39:18.390
was we weren't networked sufficiently

01:39:18.390 --> 01:39:20.160
between different laboratories.

01:39:20.160 --> 01:39:21.270
We don't have the data flows.

01:39:21.270 --> 01:39:22.830
We can't test a group of people here

01:39:22.830 --> 01:39:24.540
and get a result over there,

01:39:24.540 --> 01:39:28.770
which is actually just inefficient
pathology service anyway.

01:39:28.770 --> 01:39:30.720
So all the things that we could be doing

01:39:30.720 --> 01:39:32.490
to make the NHS better every day,

01:39:32.490 --> 01:39:34.560
that would also make us
better in a pandemic,

01:39:34.560 --> 01:39:36.600
and can we prioritise some of those?

01:39:36.600 --> 01:39:39.083
So they're some of the
conversations we're having.

01:39:40.080 --> 01:39:43.230
Just to loop back to where
we started on capacity

01:39:43.230 --> 01:39:44.790
to do this test,

01:39:44.790 --> 01:39:46.230
which is clearly like critical.

01:39:46.230 --> 01:39:48.210
I've talked about the September exercise.

01:39:48.210 --> 01:39:49.200
We've talked about this one.

01:39:49.200 --> 01:39:50.853
We've talked about the cyber one.

01:39:51.870 --> 01:39:53.670
The team of people that
would be doing this

01:39:53.670 --> 01:39:56.703
are currently now doing
the industrial action,

01:39:57.810 --> 01:40:00.783
and they will need to continue to do so,

01:40:02.400 --> 01:40:03.930
you know, whenever.

01:40:03.930 --> 01:40:06.813
So we just need to take
into account as well

01:40:06.813 --> 01:40:09.510
that that will impact on our ability

01:40:09.510 --> 01:40:12.480
to be able to thoroughly
do all the different things

01:40:12.480 --> 01:40:14.730
that we would want to do
in terms of exercising,

01:40:14.730 --> 01:40:16.050
but, whatever the case,

01:40:16.050 --> 01:40:20.100
we'll make sure we prioritise this 100%

01:40:20.100 --> 01:40:21.600
as per the risk register

01:40:21.600 --> 01:40:25.170
because doesn't matter
what else is going on,

01:40:25.170 --> 01:40:26.730
if there is an emergency,

01:40:26.730 --> 01:40:28.320
we still have to be able to respond

01:40:28.320 --> 01:40:30.350
no matter what the
underlying situation is.

01:40:30.350 --> 01:40:31.567
So we will do that.

01:40:31.567 --> 01:40:33.510
But, as you say, it does stretch,

01:40:33.510 --> 01:40:35.460
doing some of these other
things that have been described

01:40:35.460 --> 01:40:36.610
become more stretching.

01:40:37.560 --> 01:40:39.068
- Jane.

01:40:39.068 --> 01:40:40.716
- It was just a brief point just to say,

01:40:40.716 --> 01:40:41.880
you know, I think there's always a danger

01:40:41.880 --> 01:40:43.410
of fighting the last war, isn't there?

01:40:43.410 --> 01:40:47.610
And, you know, I think that
WHO is picking up thousands

01:40:47.610 --> 01:40:50.400
of potential emerging threats every month.

01:40:50.400 --> 01:40:53.550
We don't really know what
the next pandemic will be,

01:40:53.550 --> 01:40:55.590
just that there will be one,

01:40:55.590 --> 01:40:58.197
and I would just encourage,
in this exercise,

01:40:58.197 --> 01:41:00.660
and I remember I was
sort of on the fringes

01:41:00.660 --> 01:41:02.250
of Operation Cygnus,

01:41:02.250 --> 01:41:05.797
I think there's a danger of
the exercise of just saying,

01:41:05.797 --> 01:41:08.250
"Does it respond to everything
come out the COVID inquiry,"

01:41:08.250 --> 01:41:09.727
instead of casting a mind, saying,

01:41:09.727 --> 01:41:12.210
"Does it respond to everything
that might be out there?"

01:41:12.210 --> 01:41:13.200
So I would encourage us

01:41:13.200 --> 01:41:17.100
to proactively engage
with and look at countries

01:41:17.100 --> 01:41:20.070
that have done really
well on standing capacity

01:41:20.070 --> 01:41:21.390
like South Korea

01:41:21.390 --> 01:41:22.897
and not just narrowly think,

01:41:22.897 --> 01:41:24.817
"Let's address the issues that are raised

01:41:24.817 --> 01:41:26.190
"out of the COVID inquiry,"

01:41:26.190 --> 01:41:28.710
but actually look out into the wider world

01:41:28.710 --> 01:41:31.207
and say, "You know, who
are the people out there

01:41:31.207 --> 01:41:35.977
"who are really good at looking
at sort of standing capacity

01:41:35.977 --> 01:41:39.180
"for any emerging threats,
particularly zoonotic ones."

01:41:39.180 --> 01:41:40.950
- Okay, so let's not preempt this.

01:41:40.950 --> 01:41:43.830
Thank you for taking the leadership on it,

01:41:43.830 --> 01:41:46.650
and thank you for joining the exercise.

01:41:46.650 --> 01:41:49.320
I think, you know, almost
the most important point

01:41:49.320 --> 01:41:54.320
you've made is that we
have a team of people

01:41:54.420 --> 01:41:57.630
who lead all of our work
on emergency preparedness

01:41:57.630 --> 01:42:01.320
which covers many things
including industrial action,

01:42:01.320 --> 01:42:03.270
and that team has a lot on their plate,

01:42:03.270 --> 01:42:04.680
which sometimes we forget.

01:42:04.680 --> 01:42:08.370
It's sort of in the background
until there's a crisis.

01:42:08.370 --> 01:42:12.360
So thank you to you and
everyone else for doing that.

01:42:12.360 --> 01:42:14.217
But I think it's something
we need to come back to

01:42:14.217 --> 01:42:15.693
and pick up on that later.

01:42:16.650 --> 01:42:18.603
Tom, 10 Year Health Plan.

01:42:19.620 --> 01:42:22.020
- Thanks. So chance for a verbal update.

01:42:22.020 --> 01:42:23.520
So there isn't a paper for this.

01:42:23.520 --> 01:42:25.590
I'm just going to run
through where we stand.

01:42:25.590 --> 01:42:28.830
So the 10 Year Plan has
obviously been published,

01:42:28.830 --> 01:42:30.180
and we're now working

01:42:30.180 --> 01:42:35.040
to maintain and build
momentum for delivery,

01:42:35.040 --> 01:42:37.170
working in very close
partnership with our colleagues

01:42:37.170 --> 01:42:40.230
at the Department of
Health and Social Care.

01:42:40.230 --> 01:42:43.570
The most important message is
that our approach to delivery

01:42:44.520 --> 01:42:47.640
is consistent with the
things that we've said

01:42:47.640 --> 01:42:48.950
in the plan itself.

01:42:48.950 --> 01:42:50.880
So the plan envisages

01:42:50.880 --> 01:42:54.540
a devolved and diverse operating system.

01:42:54.540 --> 01:42:56.580
What that can't then mean

01:42:56.580 --> 01:42:59.910
is issuing a series of
instructions from the centre

01:42:59.910 --> 01:43:02.280
about exactly what everybody should do

01:43:02.280 --> 01:43:06.000
at every moment in the coming decade.

01:43:06.000 --> 01:43:08.220
And so, in terms of the principles

01:43:08.220 --> 01:43:10.530
that are informing our approach,

01:43:10.530 --> 01:43:11.910
the first and most important one

01:43:11.910 --> 01:43:14.490
is that this is a collaborative effort

01:43:14.490 --> 01:43:16.860
between the centre and the service

01:43:16.860 --> 01:43:20.370
to take forward the delivery planning.

01:43:20.370 --> 01:43:23.400
We're creating protected time and space

01:43:23.400 --> 01:43:24.630
for teams to focus on it,

01:43:24.630 --> 01:43:26.940
particularly during July and August.

01:43:26.940 --> 01:43:29.830
We're maintaining the
urgency and the discipline

01:43:31.020 --> 01:43:34.530
that you would expect with
weekly planning cycles

01:43:34.530 --> 01:43:37.950
and a clear deadline
set for early September,

01:43:37.950 --> 01:43:42.060
and we're encouraging, you
know, broad-based participation

01:43:42.060 --> 01:43:44.010
in that delivery-planning process.

01:43:44.010 --> 01:43:46.290
So, for example, as
Claire mentioned earlier,

01:43:46.290 --> 01:43:48.240
we had our primary care colleagues,

01:43:48.240 --> 01:43:49.650
a number of them in the room

01:43:49.650 --> 01:43:53.310
at the chief execs event last week.

01:43:53.310 --> 01:43:54.960
So in terms of our overall approach,

01:43:54.960 --> 01:43:57.450
we have a joint governance
arrangement with the department.

01:43:57.450 --> 01:44:01.860
So it's one team, one approach
to the delivery planning.

01:44:01.860 --> 01:44:03.510
And then we've got a division of labour

01:44:03.510 --> 01:44:06.270
in terms of the different work streams

01:44:06.270 --> 01:44:07.500
as to who leads on what,

01:44:07.500 --> 01:44:11.220
which makes sense according to
the scope of responsibilities

01:44:11.220 --> 01:44:13.260
and the skills within each area.

01:44:13.260 --> 01:44:15.630
So there are nine work streams in total.

01:44:15.630 --> 01:44:17.160
The department's leading on three,

01:44:17.160 --> 01:44:19.290
so they're leading on life sciences,

01:44:19.290 --> 01:44:21.660
prevention, and legislation.

01:44:21.660 --> 01:44:23.580
And then NHS England is leading

01:44:23.580 --> 01:44:25.470
on the other six work streams.

01:44:25.470 --> 01:44:27.030
And I'll just give a brief overview

01:44:27.030 --> 01:44:30.510
of what's in each of
those six work streams.

01:44:30.510 --> 01:44:32.940
The first is neighbourhood health,

01:44:32.940 --> 01:44:35.040
which the immediate priorities include

01:44:35.040 --> 01:44:38.550
designing the model
contracts, payment reform,

01:44:38.550 --> 01:44:42.090
financial flows,
workforce, digital estates,

01:44:42.090 --> 01:44:43.890
all of those issues,
trying to get them together

01:44:43.890 --> 01:44:46.890
and say not necessarily
what is the complete answer

01:44:46.890 --> 01:44:49.320
on each of them, but what
are the starting things,

01:44:49.320 --> 01:44:50.280
how can we get moving

01:44:50.280 --> 01:44:53.190
on each of those different important areas

01:44:53.190 --> 01:44:55.440
and selecting the 42 places

01:44:55.440 --> 01:44:59.700
for the National Neighbourhood
Implementation Programme.

01:44:59.700 --> 01:45:01.470
The next is the financial foundations

01:45:01.470 --> 01:45:03.273
and medium-term planning.

01:45:04.272 --> 01:45:05.105
So we're going to publish

01:45:05.105 --> 01:45:09.000
the new three-year planning
framework in October.

01:45:09.000 --> 01:45:10.800
There's a lot of work already underway

01:45:10.800 --> 01:45:13.020
on the reform of block contracts

01:45:13.020 --> 01:45:15.630
and the development of
a neighbourhood tariff.

01:45:15.630 --> 01:45:20.630
There's work that's going on
on delivering the model region

01:45:21.390 --> 01:45:25.320
and the ICB clustering
arrangements that Glen is leading.

01:45:25.320 --> 01:45:27.120
On digital and data,

01:45:27.120 --> 01:45:29.520
we have a work stream that's the NHS app

01:45:29.520 --> 01:45:31.830
and the single patient record

01:45:31.830 --> 01:45:33.360
where the immediate priorities

01:45:33.360 --> 01:45:36.270
are to launch the user
and supplier engagement

01:45:36.270 --> 01:45:39.210
and complete the business
cases for 2026 procurement

01:45:39.210 --> 01:45:42.810
by the end of this calendar year.

01:45:42.810 --> 01:45:45.060
On quality, which is a big
and important work stream,

01:45:45.060 --> 01:45:48.180
we've already touched on
in the discussion today,

01:45:48.180 --> 01:45:51.030
there's the revamp of the
National Quality Board,

01:45:51.030 --> 01:45:54.030
the creation of a new quality strategy,

01:45:54.030 --> 01:45:56.670
the development of
modern service frameworks

01:45:56.670 --> 01:46:01.560
for CVD, mental health,
sepsis, and cancer.

01:46:01.560 --> 01:46:04.020
And then the big discussion that we had

01:46:04.020 --> 01:46:08.670
that David led around this
real focus on transparency.

01:46:08.670 --> 01:46:12.060
We're also, as we picked
up on a little earlier,

01:46:12.060 --> 01:46:16.170
rolling out the Maternity
Outcomes Signal System, MOSS,

01:46:16.170 --> 01:46:19.200
to try and pick up on
issues in maternity care

01:46:19.200 --> 01:46:21.720
as early as possible.

01:46:21.720 --> 01:46:26.340
The fifth work stream
then is Oversight Model FT

01:46:26.340 --> 01:46:30.360
and Model Integrated Health Organisation.

01:46:30.360 --> 01:46:32.340
So we launched a performance segmentation

01:46:32.340 --> 01:46:33.300
as we've talked about.

01:46:33.300 --> 01:46:36.810
We bring in more public league tables,

01:46:36.810 --> 01:46:38.820
and we have a kind of
national improvement offer

01:46:38.820 --> 01:46:40.890
that has been developed.

01:46:40.890 --> 01:46:43.650
We're finalising and developing

01:46:43.650 --> 01:46:47.130
the revised foundation
trust licencing model.

01:46:47.130 --> 01:46:50.790
And the other priorities include
the kind of early scoping

01:46:50.790 --> 01:46:53.130
of what this integrated
health organisation model

01:46:53.130 --> 01:46:56.820
will look like and what it
will take to become one.

01:46:56.820 --> 01:47:00.720
And then the sixth work
stream is on workforce

01:47:00.720 --> 01:47:02.850
where we've committed to
developing and publishing

01:47:02.850 --> 01:47:05.550
a new 10 Year workforce plan.

01:47:05.550 --> 01:47:08.880
We're publishing nursing
and midwifery strategies.

01:47:08.880 --> 01:47:11.610
For England, focuses are,

01:47:11.610 --> 01:47:13.470
as we talked about a bit
earlier in the context

01:47:13.470 --> 01:47:15.330
of industrial action,

01:47:15.330 --> 01:47:19.260
improving staff and
student trainee experience.

01:47:19.260 --> 01:47:20.700
That's not just for resident doctors.

01:47:20.700 --> 01:47:24.810
That's more broadly across the workforce.

01:47:24.810 --> 01:47:26.820
And then making sure that
there is an alignment

01:47:26.820 --> 01:47:29.910
between workforce service
and financial planning

01:47:29.910 --> 01:47:33.363
in the medium term plans.

01:47:34.365 --> 01:47:37.170
At the NHS leadership meeting last week,

01:47:37.170 --> 01:47:38.940
we had obviously the CEOs.

01:47:38.940 --> 01:47:41.040
We also had primary care leaders,

01:47:41.040 --> 01:47:43.110
and we asked for
volunteers to come forward

01:47:43.110 --> 01:47:46.560
and say who would like to be
involved in planning delivery

01:47:46.560 --> 01:47:48.957
and contribute to those groups.

01:47:48.957 --> 01:47:53.957
And so far we've had 150
expressions of interest.

01:47:54.390 --> 01:47:57.900
And I think that really speaks
to the point Jim was making

01:47:57.900 --> 01:47:58.890
at the beginning

01:47:58.890 --> 01:48:03.270
about the real ambition
and appetite and enthusiasm

01:48:03.270 --> 01:48:05.580
that exists out there in the service.

01:48:05.580 --> 01:48:09.510
They want to grab hold
of this and run with it.

01:48:09.510 --> 01:48:13.200
And so I think that's
a really good indicator

01:48:13.200 --> 01:48:16.380
that it's not just us saying
that we want to bridge the gap

01:48:16.380 --> 01:48:18.030
between the centre and the service.

01:48:18.030 --> 01:48:20.730
The service wants to do
exactly the same thing.

01:48:20.730 --> 01:48:24.750
And so I think we've got some
really strong momentum there.

01:48:24.750 --> 01:48:27.570
So we'll be working at
pace over the summer

01:48:27.570 --> 01:48:31.200
so that we really get
into the autumn, you know,

01:48:31.200 --> 01:48:33.360
going at full pelt,

01:48:33.360 --> 01:48:37.560
and we'll be able to update
the NHS leadership community

01:48:37.560 --> 01:48:41.010
as our event that we have
planned in mid-September

01:48:41.010 --> 01:48:42.720
where we'll bring everyone together,

01:48:42.720 --> 01:48:44.130
and we'll be able to share the progress

01:48:44.130 --> 01:48:46.440
that we collectively have made

01:48:46.440 --> 01:48:51.220
from the contributions that
the people within NHS England,

01:48:51.220 --> 01:48:53.340
DHSC, and all the leaders
out in the service

01:48:53.340 --> 01:48:56.820
who've come together to
contribute to that process.

01:48:56.820 --> 01:48:59.130
So that's a bit of a brief
update on where we stand,

01:48:59.130 --> 01:49:02.730
and I think Claire's going to
describe some of the progress

01:49:02.730 --> 01:49:05.280
that we've made on neighbourhood health

01:49:05.280 --> 01:49:06.930
in different aspects.

01:49:06.930 --> 01:49:07.763
- Thank you, Tom.

01:49:07.763 --> 01:49:08.970
So, not that I'm competitive,

01:49:08.970 --> 01:49:12.394
but of the 150 people who put
in an expression of interest

01:49:12.394 --> 01:49:14.250
of which work stream they
wanted to be involved in,

01:49:14.250 --> 01:49:17.460
the most people we're
interested in working with

01:49:17.460 --> 01:49:19.110
the neighbourhood work stream.

01:49:19.110 --> 01:49:20.733
So I'll let that hang.

01:49:21.750 --> 01:49:22.583
I think one of the bits

01:49:22.583 --> 01:49:24.927
that's really important,
exciting about the plan

01:49:24.927 --> 01:49:28.230
is, so we have spent a
lot of time here today

01:49:28.230 --> 01:49:32.910
talking about activity and
performance in hospitals,

01:49:32.910 --> 01:49:35.580
and that largely impacts upon adults.

01:49:35.580 --> 01:49:37.860
The thing that's really
exciting about the plan

01:49:37.860 --> 01:49:40.170
is actually with most of
the plan is about activity

01:49:40.170 --> 01:49:41.490
that doesn't happen in hospitals,

01:49:41.490 --> 01:49:44.490
and actually we start to talk
about more vulnerable groups.

01:49:44.490 --> 01:49:45.720
So we've launched

01:49:45.720 --> 01:49:47.730
the Neighbourhood Health
Implementation Plan this week.

01:49:47.730 --> 01:49:51.420
We had 1,400 people on the webinar.

01:49:51.420 --> 01:49:53.790
I have to say my phone is going crazy

01:49:53.790 --> 01:49:55.530
with people going, "We
really want to do this."

01:49:55.530 --> 01:49:58.500
I think we are going to be
inundated with applications

01:49:58.500 --> 01:50:00.963
which is a fantastic position to be in.

01:50:02.100 --> 01:50:03.300
We've today opened

01:50:03.300 --> 01:50:08.300
our first 24/7 mental health
crisis centre in Tower Hamlets.

01:50:08.490 --> 01:50:11.010
And again, this is hugely important

01:50:11.010 --> 01:50:13.343
just so in terms of people
with serious mental illness,

01:50:13.343 --> 01:50:15.953
they're some of the most
vulnerable people in our society,

01:50:17.754 --> 01:50:19.680
and there are pilots, six
pilots across the country.

01:50:19.680 --> 01:50:21.990
And what we're doing is actually
bringing together services

01:50:21.990 --> 01:50:23.940
that will support people
across all of their lives,

01:50:23.940 --> 01:50:26.850
not just from the physical
and mental health.

01:50:26.850 --> 01:50:28.770
So we actually bring together

01:50:28.770 --> 01:50:30.930
employment support, debt support,

01:50:30.930 --> 01:50:33.360
and actually helping
people live their lives

01:50:33.360 --> 01:50:34.763
in the communities in which they are.

01:50:34.763 --> 01:50:36.930
I think the other threads that go through,

01:50:36.930 --> 01:50:38.430
particularly, we haven't talked that much

01:50:38.430 --> 01:50:40.620
about mental illness today either.

01:50:40.620 --> 01:50:41.831
I think it's really important.

01:50:41.831 --> 01:50:42.780
So we've got the six hubs.

01:50:42.780 --> 01:50:46.410
We've got the 24/7 access in EDs.

01:50:46.410 --> 01:50:48.270
We've got the mental health crisis line,

01:50:48.270 --> 01:50:51.240
and actually the Mental Health
Modern Service Framework

01:50:51.240 --> 01:50:53.160
and the Modern Service Framework Refresh

01:50:53.160 --> 01:50:55.800
has been so well received
by the clinical communities.

01:50:55.800 --> 01:50:58.860
You can see people are
really keen to define

01:50:58.860 --> 01:51:00.540
what good service looks like

01:51:00.540 --> 01:51:03.180
and how we can actually
drive consistent improvement.

01:51:03.180 --> 01:51:06.000
But the modern service
framework for mental health,

01:51:06.000 --> 01:51:07.110
that'll be led by Adrian James,

01:51:07.110 --> 01:51:09.780
our medical director for mental
health and neurodiversity,

01:51:09.780 --> 01:51:12.600
is going start focus on
serious mental illness as well.

01:51:12.600 --> 01:51:14.970
So I think it is a really exciting plan,

01:51:14.970 --> 01:51:17.070
and I'm obviously particularly excited

01:51:17.070 --> 01:51:18.600
about the focus and the progress

01:51:18.600 --> 01:51:19.770
that we've already made
to our neighbourhoods.

01:51:19.770 --> 01:51:20.670
So thank you, Tom.

01:51:21.570 --> 01:51:25.083
- Okay. So, great to hear.

01:51:26.580 --> 01:51:27.960
Just want to check.

01:51:27.960 --> 01:51:31.690
We will still be seeing
a detailed delivery plan

01:51:32.550 --> 01:51:33.900
by the beginning of September.

01:51:33.900 --> 01:51:34.890
- Yeah.
- Yes.

01:51:34.890 --> 01:51:37.200
- Okay, and so a quick
question then, Claire.

01:51:37.200 --> 01:51:39.240
How can we launch an implementation plan

01:51:39.240 --> 01:51:41.460
when we haven't yet seen a delivery plan?

01:51:41.460 --> 01:51:45.000
- It's the implementation of the

01:51:45.000 --> 01:51:47.370
Yes, all right. It's P is for programme.

01:51:47.370 --> 01:51:49.380
It's the implementation program for the

01:51:49.380 --> 01:51:53.310
National Neighbourhood Health
Implementation Programme

01:51:53.310 --> 01:51:55.860
which will focus on multi-morbidity.

01:51:55.860 --> 01:51:58.290
So that's the programme
being led by John Oldham

01:51:58.290 --> 01:51:59.490
via the task force

01:51:59.490 --> 01:52:02.673
that will lead us across the
42 places across the country.

01:52:03.660 --> 01:52:05.280
- [Board member] It's more of
an improvement collaborative,

01:52:05.280 --> 01:52:06.436
isn't it, in lots of ways?

01:52:06.436 --> 01:52:07.269
- Well, it is.

01:52:07.269 --> 01:52:11.070
But it's focusing on multi-morbidity

01:52:11.070 --> 01:52:12.120
because, actually, if you can fix

01:52:12.120 --> 01:52:13.470
multi-morbidity across a place,

01:52:13.470 --> 01:52:15.570
you've got to bring
together acute community.

01:52:15.570 --> 01:52:16.629
- [Penny] Okay.

01:52:16.629 --> 01:52:17.462
- Mental health-

01:52:17.462 --> 01:52:18.295
- Well maybe we should call it that then

01:52:18.295 --> 01:52:20.160
'cause otherwise it'll confuse all of us.

01:52:20.160 --> 01:52:22.110
So if we've got a specific chapter

01:52:22.110 --> 01:52:24.120
on neighbourhood healthcare,

01:52:24.120 --> 01:52:27.030
and we're going to see a
very detailed delivery plan

01:52:27.030 --> 01:52:27.960
by the beginning of September

01:52:27.960 --> 01:52:30.240
about how we're going to implement that,

01:52:30.240 --> 01:52:33.060
I think we need to see what that is.

01:52:33.060 --> 01:52:35.580
And it sounds like this is
something a bit different.

01:52:35.580 --> 01:52:37.770
- [Claire] Yeah, okay. P is for programme.

01:52:37.770 --> 01:52:40.650
- Yeah, yeah, okay. Vin.

01:52:40.650 --> 01:52:42.063
- So one of the work streams

01:52:42.063 --> 01:52:45.090
will be on innovation, life
sciences, and research.

01:52:45.090 --> 01:52:46.830
So, just for noting,

01:52:46.830 --> 01:52:48.600
I think the life sciences sector plan

01:52:48.600 --> 01:52:50.820
was published by the government yesterday.

01:52:50.820 --> 01:52:52.500
It's got a number of commitments in it

01:52:52.500 --> 01:52:53.940
because we developed the chapter

01:52:53.940 --> 01:52:55.230
in the 10 Year Plan together

01:52:55.230 --> 01:52:57.900
with our section in the
life sciences sector plan

01:52:57.900 --> 01:52:59.070
to a number of commitments:

01:52:59.070 --> 01:53:02.190
to improve research, being
the best place to grow

01:53:02.190 --> 01:53:03.210
and scale a business,

01:53:03.210 --> 01:53:06.030
and also making ours, the NHS, better

01:53:06.030 --> 01:53:10.950
at regulation and speeding
up access for innovators.

01:53:10.950 --> 01:53:12.630
So we are doing that together

01:53:12.630 --> 01:53:14.070
so we don't have two separate plans.

01:53:14.070 --> 01:53:16.860
We'll have one plan jointly
across science, innovation,

01:53:16.860 --> 01:53:18.410
technology, and the department.

01:53:19.470 --> 01:53:21.810
- Good. Mary.

01:53:21.810 --> 01:53:23.746
- I think this is really exciting

01:53:23.746 --> 01:53:24.870
and it won't surprise anybody

01:53:24.870 --> 01:53:26.910
that I'm delighted to hear about the focus

01:53:26.910 --> 01:53:30.270
on acute mental health services.

01:53:30.270 --> 01:53:34.410
But I'm very aware that this will require

01:53:34.410 --> 01:53:36.540
transfer of some income

01:53:36.540 --> 01:53:40.650
from acute trusts to neighbourhood
services to make it work

01:53:40.650 --> 01:53:42.780
because the argument has always been

01:53:42.780 --> 01:53:47.780
20% minimum of healthcare
is mental health,

01:53:48.600 --> 01:53:52.590
but only about 8 1/2% of
our budget is spent on it.

01:53:52.590 --> 01:53:54.807
So, if we're going to get there,

01:53:54.807 --> 01:53:56.610
are we working in tandem

01:53:56.610 --> 01:53:59.193
with how we start to work that through?

01:54:00.690 --> 01:54:01.523
- Yeah, absolutely.

01:54:01.523 --> 01:54:03.450
I think, Tom, you highlighted

01:54:03.450 --> 01:54:06.600
when you ran through the
neighbourhood health bucket,

01:54:06.600 --> 01:54:08.910
and yesterday actually,
I met with all the CFOs.

01:54:08.910 --> 01:54:10.710
It was a virtual meeting yesterday,

01:54:10.710 --> 01:54:12.150
and the question they had was,

01:54:12.150 --> 01:54:14.160
I think I had more than 150 volunteers.

01:54:14.160 --> 01:54:15.111
- [Board member] Oh, here we go!

01:54:16.350 --> 01:54:17.550
But they were in person.

01:54:18.457 --> 01:54:21.631
- Wouldn't it be 48 were
from Glen Burley as well.

01:54:21.631 --> 01:54:24.513
It was actually, 148 on
one topic, which is Glen.

01:54:25.380 --> 01:54:29.437
But, in all honesty, the
CFO community was saying,

01:54:29.437 --> 01:54:30.987
"Well how do we enable it?"

01:54:30.987 --> 01:54:33.210
And people are talking
about stranded fixed costs

01:54:33.210 --> 01:54:35.400
and all the usual things
that you would expect.

01:54:35.400 --> 01:54:37.020
That's why actually unbundling

01:54:37.020 --> 01:54:40.890
and understanding what's
in the UEC bucket,

01:54:40.890 --> 01:54:43.293
as we're calling it, is so important.

01:54:44.160 --> 01:54:47.300
We will have, so a piece
of work we shared yesterday

01:54:47.300 --> 01:54:48.720
at a high level,

01:54:48.720 --> 01:54:50.570
it's going out in the next two weeks.

01:54:51.690 --> 01:54:53.583
Week by week, we're going
to have to be doing this

01:54:53.583 --> 01:54:54.720
even with the service.

01:54:54.720 --> 01:54:56.670
We can't do it because
there's local prices

01:54:56.670 --> 01:55:01.050
and all sorts of things
within the financing model.

01:55:01.050 --> 01:55:03.270
So, huge appetite.

01:55:03.270 --> 01:55:04.740
Everybody wants to be involved

01:55:04.740 --> 01:55:08.363
as much because they want to
influence it, in all honesty

01:55:08.363 --> 01:55:09.750
as well as to be part of it

01:55:09.750 --> 01:55:11.760
'cause they're feeling excited.

01:55:11.760 --> 01:55:13.260
But in all honesty though,

01:55:13.260 --> 01:55:16.140
I think Penny and Jim have
had some correspondence

01:55:16.140 --> 01:55:17.850
coming through, a bit anxious

01:55:17.850 --> 01:55:21.150
about the amount of financial
change and disruption

01:55:21.150 --> 01:55:23.370
you can take in one go.

01:55:23.370 --> 01:55:25.800
So I think we've got to do
the work, see what it says,

01:55:25.800 --> 01:55:27.810
step back, and see how
much risk we can take.

01:55:27.810 --> 01:55:29.880
And by then we'll be at month 6

01:55:29.880 --> 01:55:31.830
in terms of the numbers as well.

01:55:31.830 --> 01:55:32.790
- But we've got to

01:55:32.790 --> 01:55:35.553
20% of activity doesn't mean 20% of spend.

01:55:36.540 --> 01:55:38.730
We need to understand
what things should cost,

01:55:38.730 --> 01:55:39.563
which is why
- [Mary] Yeah,

01:55:39.563 --> 01:55:42.420
I want to support what you're saying,

01:55:42.420 --> 01:55:44.760
but also acknowledge that actually,

01:55:44.760 --> 01:55:47.040
if some of those mental health issues,

01:55:47.040 --> 01:55:49.500
that are currently dealt
with in ordinary A&amp;Es

01:55:49.500 --> 01:55:52.170
are dealt with in a different way,

01:55:52.170 --> 01:55:54.630
it will actually be very cost effective

01:55:54.630 --> 01:55:55.800
for acute trusts as well.

01:55:55.800 --> 01:55:59.489
So, you know, got to get
it right, haven't we?

01:55:59.489 --> 01:56:00.322
- [Penny] Glen.

01:56:00.322 --> 01:56:04.800
- Yeah, just to add, part
of the financial modelling

01:56:04.800 --> 01:56:07.640
for applicant acute new FTs,

01:56:07.640 --> 01:56:09.690
we would expect them to be looking

01:56:09.690 --> 01:56:13.263
at the impact of left
shift in a variety of ways.

01:56:14.160 --> 01:56:14.993
- Absolutely.

01:56:14.993 --> 01:56:16.400
Again, that's something
we'll need to come back to

01:56:16.400 --> 01:56:19.530
is what that evaluation
framework will look like.

01:56:19.530 --> 01:56:22.550
I presume that's sitting with
you, Glen, your worksheet.

01:56:22.550 --> 01:56:25.380
- It is. I've had lots
of volunteers to work

01:56:28.410 --> 01:56:31.200
- Okay, we'll have that as a
new performance metric, David,

01:56:31.200 --> 01:56:34.740
for the exec team, popularity contest.

01:56:34.740 --> 01:56:37.950
Okay, great. Let's move
on to Freedom to Speak Up.

01:56:37.950 --> 01:56:40.143
So thank you, Tom, Bethany for joining us.

01:56:41.633 --> 01:56:44.310
Want to give a high-level
summary of the paper?

01:56:44.310 --> 01:56:45.930
- Yeah. So hi, everyone.

01:56:45.930 --> 01:56:48.420
So three reflections I'd like to share

01:56:48.420 --> 01:56:51.570
from the internal Freedom
to Speak Up report.

01:56:51.570 --> 01:56:55.110
We launched a unified internal
Freedom to Speak Up service

01:56:55.110 --> 01:56:56.520
just over a year ago,

01:56:56.520 --> 01:57:01.380
and it's been good to see that
so many people have felt able

01:57:01.380 --> 01:57:03.630
to speak up and use that service

01:57:03.630 --> 01:57:06.903
and significantly more numbers
than the previous year.

01:57:08.670 --> 01:57:11.613
However, their experience when
they speak up is variable.

01:57:12.810 --> 01:57:15.630
And so only 40% of those who speak up

01:57:15.630 --> 01:57:16.950
say they would speak up again.

01:57:16.950 --> 01:57:20.520
And those who have used the service

01:57:20.520 --> 01:57:22.140
and have provided anonymous feedback

01:57:22.140 --> 01:57:24.180
indicates that while
support from guardians

01:57:24.180 --> 01:57:26.100
is really valued and welcomed,

01:57:26.100 --> 01:57:27.420
the response received

01:57:27.420 --> 01:57:29.370
from the organisation and from leaders

01:57:29.370 --> 01:57:30.753
is not always the same.

01:57:32.430 --> 01:57:35.460
And that is really
important in the context of

01:57:35.460 --> 01:57:38.250
I think, a staff across NHS England

01:57:38.250 --> 01:57:40.770
who are probably feeling
a bit more fragile

01:57:40.770 --> 01:57:43.650
than they might be going into
further organisational change,

01:57:43.650 --> 01:57:45.780
and I know that's
something raised with you,

01:57:45.780 --> 01:57:46.613
didn't they, Jim,

01:57:46.613 --> 01:57:48.513
when you spoke to the guardians recently.

01:57:50.250 --> 01:57:52.560
And so clearly it's really vital

01:57:52.560 --> 01:57:55.290
that our staff feel listened
to when they speak up.

01:57:55.290 --> 01:57:58.380
And so really high-quality leadership

01:57:58.380 --> 01:57:59.733
and management's essential.

01:58:01.290 --> 01:58:04.260
Unfortunately leadership behaviours

01:58:04.260 --> 01:58:05.670
are sometimes part of the challenge

01:58:05.670 --> 01:58:06.503
and part of the problem

01:58:06.503 --> 01:58:10.620
with what causes people to speak up,

01:58:10.620 --> 01:58:12.720
both in terms of the response they get

01:58:12.720 --> 01:58:14.160
when they try to raise issues,

01:58:14.160 --> 01:58:17.250
but also in terms of maybe not
feeling able to raise things

01:58:17.250 --> 01:58:20.100
in terms of the environment
that's created by leaders.

01:58:20.100 --> 01:58:22.325
And so setting the right expectations

01:58:22.325 --> 01:58:23.820
around those behaviours

01:58:23.820 --> 01:58:28.820
and also ensuring that
our leaders and managers

01:58:29.190 --> 01:58:32.700
recognise the value of what
people bother and feel,

01:58:32.700 --> 01:58:34.407
it's important enough to speak up about

01:58:34.407 --> 01:58:36.480
and really value what they have to say

01:58:36.480 --> 01:58:39.780
and listen compassionately
and respond fairly.

01:58:39.780 --> 01:58:43.890
So those are the three
reflections I wanted to share.

01:58:43.890 --> 01:58:46.200
What are we doing to sort of improve

01:58:46.200 --> 01:58:47.160
and take things forward?

01:58:47.160 --> 01:58:51.120
Well, one, we're improving our processes

01:58:51.120 --> 01:58:53.370
to make sure that we are
maximising opportunity

01:58:53.370 --> 01:58:55.950
for effective resolution of cases.

01:58:55.950 --> 01:58:58.050
Two, is working more with senior leaders

01:58:58.050 --> 01:58:59.530
across the organisation

01:59:00.630 --> 01:59:03.390
and in conjunction with
HR and OD colleagues.

01:59:03.390 --> 01:59:06.600
And then the third thing is
about making sure we do more

01:59:06.600 --> 01:59:09.060
to capture and share the
learning and improvement

01:59:09.060 --> 01:59:11.160
that follows from speaking up,

01:59:11.160 --> 01:59:13.440
which creates that loop
of encouraging more people

01:59:13.440 --> 01:59:14.940
to come forward.

01:59:14.940 --> 01:59:18.210
- Yeah. Andrew,

01:59:18.210 --> 01:59:23.210
Where people feel that their
concern hasn't been listened to

01:59:23.520 --> 01:59:24.843
or heard or resolved,

01:59:25.890 --> 01:59:27.630
do we know why?

01:59:27.630 --> 01:59:31.170
Do we know sort of what hasn't worked?

01:59:31.170 --> 01:59:33.240
- I think initial listening

01:59:33.240 --> 01:59:35.250
is actually genuinely understanding

01:59:35.250 --> 01:59:36.810
what it is the person is saying

01:59:36.810 --> 01:59:38.110
and why they're saying it.

01:59:39.720 --> 01:59:42.270
If they don't feel listened on,

01:59:42.270 --> 01:59:43.520
sometimes what happens is

01:59:44.940 --> 01:59:46.980
that becomes as much part of the problem

01:59:46.980 --> 01:59:49.830
as the original issue
they're speaking up about.

01:59:49.830 --> 01:59:54.240
And certainly when, say,
I get drawn into cases

01:59:54.240 --> 01:59:55.680
that might get escalated,

01:59:55.680 --> 01:59:56.513
those are the things

01:59:56.513 --> 01:59:59.460
that I'm trying to bring out.

01:59:59.460 --> 02:00:03.690
And also I think engaging
senior leaders really early

02:00:03.690 --> 02:00:05.047
and trying to work out,

02:00:05.047 --> 02:00:07.590
"Okay, how can we move this forward?"

02:00:07.590 --> 02:00:09.180
- [Penny] Yeah. Andrew.

02:00:09.180 --> 02:00:12.060
- So, I mean, on this,

02:00:12.060 --> 02:00:15.210
we still haven't got the culture
right in the organisation,

02:00:15.210 --> 02:00:18.690
and we've had, I think,
two kind of attempts now.

02:00:18.690 --> 02:00:20.610
When E&amp;I came together,

02:00:20.610 --> 02:00:23.970
we said we were going to have
a kind of cultural reset.

02:00:23.970 --> 02:00:27.960
We said exactly the same
with that last restructuring.

02:00:27.960 --> 02:00:29.550
And I think it's really important

02:00:29.550 --> 02:00:32.940
that, as we go into the new world,

02:00:32.940 --> 02:00:36.960
that someone leads on the work on culture,

02:00:36.960 --> 02:00:41.960
and we just haven't bottomed
all of the behaviours

02:00:41.970 --> 02:00:43.380
that we expect to see.

02:00:43.380 --> 02:00:45.060
They don't kind of translate

02:00:45.060 --> 02:00:49.920
into taking this subject
matter really seriously.

02:00:49.920 --> 02:00:53.640
So, in contrast to a trust,

02:00:53.640 --> 02:00:58.230
where the focus is on
resolving things quite quickly,

02:00:58.230 --> 02:01:01.380
here we've had cases that
have gone on and on and on,

02:01:01.380 --> 02:01:04.410
and despite Tom's, you
know, valiant efforts

02:01:04.410 --> 02:01:09.410
to try and get leaders to get
involved in resolving issues,

02:01:09.570 --> 02:01:11.910
these things just go on and on and on,

02:01:11.910 --> 02:01:13.620
and it's embarrassing.

02:01:13.620 --> 02:01:18.090
So I think, going forward
into the new organisation,

02:01:18.090 --> 02:01:20.430
we just need to take this really seriously

02:01:20.430 --> 02:01:22.890
because people may listen

02:01:22.890 --> 02:01:26.433
but you don't always see
action on the back of it.

02:01:27.570 --> 02:01:31.110
And a lot of the guardians
are getting quite frustrated

02:01:31.110 --> 02:01:36.110
by the lack of, you know,
attention really in this area.

02:01:36.990 --> 02:01:40.863
So, you know, I would urge
that all the directors,

02:01:41.711 --> 02:01:43.920
and the directors aren't
the problem, right?

02:01:43.920 --> 02:01:47.700
It's right down into the kind
of bowels of the organisation.

02:01:47.700 --> 02:01:51.600
It's the first-line management tranche

02:01:51.600 --> 02:01:52.470
that's the real issue.

02:01:52.470 --> 02:01:57.000
And NEDs have had a session

02:01:57.000 --> 02:02:00.270
on this in PRC under Jane's leadership.

02:02:00.270 --> 02:02:02.160
We're glad to see that we're rolling out

02:02:02.160 --> 02:02:04.050
the kind of management
development programme

02:02:04.050 --> 02:02:06.030
on a compulsory basis,

02:02:06.030 --> 02:02:08.040
but we've still got a hell
of a lot of work to do

02:02:08.040 --> 02:02:09.390
in this area.

02:02:09.390 --> 02:02:11.487
I don't want to sound too negative.

02:02:13.080 --> 02:02:15.420
- So our management appraisals,

02:02:15.420 --> 02:02:18.958
'cause in some ways culture
is an output of how we manage,

02:02:18.958 --> 02:02:20.460
you know, how people are managed
and how they're appraised,

02:02:20.460 --> 02:02:24.660
so what does the management
appraisal system look like?

02:02:24.660 --> 02:02:26.427
What are the metrics that we're using?

02:02:26.427 --> 02:02:27.483
What's the-

02:02:28.500 --> 02:02:30.967
- So, for a long time,
we've been trying to say,

02:02:30.967 --> 02:02:34.530
"Look, everyone's guaranteed
an appraisal once a year."

02:02:34.530 --> 02:02:38.157
We've never got anywhere near 90%, so-

02:02:43.200 --> 02:02:45.360
- We don't appraise people once a year?

02:02:45.360 --> 02:02:46.193
- Well, we do.

02:02:46.193 --> 02:02:48.930
We do appraise them, but it's patchy.

02:02:48.930 --> 02:02:50.190
So, in some directorates,

02:02:50.190 --> 02:02:52.620
it's, you know, very comprehensive.

02:02:52.620 --> 02:02:55.206
In others, it's less so,

02:02:55.206 --> 02:02:58.923
and that's something I think
we again need to focus on.

02:03:00.315 --> 02:03:01.890
- So, Tom, just to reinforce that,

02:03:01.890 --> 02:03:05.310
Tom said I met with
colleagues a few weeks ago,

02:03:05.310 --> 02:03:09.120
and there's a very, very strong feeling

02:03:09.120 --> 02:03:13.380
about management capability

02:03:13.380 --> 02:03:15.093
in dealing with issues.

02:03:16.020 --> 02:03:18.030
That then spills off
into a lack of confidence

02:03:18.030 --> 02:03:20.100
about HR process.

02:03:20.100 --> 02:03:23.730
But at the heart is just the
quality of that conversation

02:03:23.730 --> 02:03:25.647
to try to seek resolution in something,

02:03:25.647 --> 02:03:27.797
and I think we ought to
really try and focus on that.

02:03:27.797 --> 02:03:29.640
In the culture thing,

02:03:29.640 --> 02:03:34.350
there's the kind of softer
edges, developmental edges of it,

02:03:34.350 --> 02:03:36.270
but there's the fundamental processes

02:03:36.270 --> 02:03:38.280
that make people feel fair and safe.

02:03:38.280 --> 02:03:40.020
Our processes are really poor,

02:03:40.020 --> 02:03:40.853
- [Andrew] Yeah.

02:03:40.853 --> 02:03:42.660
- I think, or it feels like it,

02:03:42.660 --> 02:03:44.160
being new into the organisation,

02:03:44.160 --> 02:03:45.170
and that discipline that you'd have

02:03:45.170 --> 02:03:47.580
in an organisation like a trust,

02:03:47.580 --> 02:03:50.040
colleagues will describe here,

02:03:50.040 --> 02:03:52.410
appraisal rates and appraisal quality

02:03:52.410 --> 02:03:53.820
are things that you're really going after

02:03:53.820 --> 02:03:55.656
all the time, you know,

02:03:55.656 --> 02:03:57.330
and I can't see that here.

02:03:57.330 --> 02:03:59.940
So we're going to have
to build some things here

02:03:59.940 --> 02:04:02.010
that are evident elsewhere,

02:04:02.010 --> 02:04:05.310
but I think do sit behind
some of these problems.

02:04:05.310 --> 02:04:07.470
- Penny, I think, as a
board, we should, you know,

02:04:07.470 --> 02:04:10.200
offer all of our employees the chance

02:04:10.200 --> 02:04:12.840
to have the one
conversation with their boss

02:04:12.840 --> 02:04:14.103
every 12 months.

02:04:15.009 --> 02:04:15.891
- [Jim] It's not a high bar, is it?

02:04:17.222 --> 02:04:19.693
- We should be raising that
ladder to be a standard.

02:04:19.693 --> 02:04:22.890
- But it's, you know, it's not happened.

02:04:22.890 --> 02:04:24.237
It's not happened.

02:04:24.237 --> 02:04:27.003
And it's been very
frustrating, I have to say.

02:04:27.870 --> 02:04:30.390
You know, many of us have the session,

02:04:30.390 --> 02:04:34.110
those NEDs have sessions
with the guardians, with Tom,

02:04:34.110 --> 02:04:37.680
and the frustration over this is enormous.

02:04:37.680 --> 02:04:41.610
And I think it's an
issue that we can resolve

02:04:41.610 --> 02:04:42.960
quite simply and easily,

02:04:42.960 --> 02:04:44.073
and, in the trust,

02:04:45.046 --> 02:04:48.180
you know, you would really
focus on getting this right

02:04:48.180 --> 02:04:52.080
and we've unfortunately,
you know, missed the goal.

02:04:52.080 --> 02:04:56.673
- Okay, so in our feedback
from PRC committee,

02:04:57.990 --> 02:04:59.240
we should have some data.

02:05:00.241 --> 02:05:03.540
We should be looking at this
in terms of what's happening.

02:05:03.540 --> 02:05:04.373
- [Andrew] Yeah, definitely.

02:05:04.373 --> 02:05:05.206
- Sarah-Jane.

02:05:06.420 --> 02:05:10.890
- So, I totally, the annual
appraisal is important,

02:05:10.890 --> 02:05:12.900
but we need to be providing the feedback

02:05:12.900 --> 02:05:14.400
every time we have an interaction,

02:05:14.400 --> 02:05:16.830
whether that's in a regular
one-to-one or in the moment

02:05:16.830 --> 02:05:19.950
because that's an absolute backstop,

02:05:19.950 --> 02:05:21.000
and I think it connects

02:05:21.000 --> 02:05:24.510
to some of the challenges
that Tom is raising

02:05:24.510 --> 02:05:27.630
because we're not, I'm sure some are,

02:05:27.630 --> 02:05:29.070
but we're not always very good

02:05:29.070 --> 02:05:32.490
at having the initial conversation
that manages expectations

02:05:32.490 --> 02:05:33.810
about what might be possible

02:05:33.810 --> 02:05:35.160
with the raising of the concern,

02:05:35.160 --> 02:05:38.610
and therefore it becomes
difficult to close it down

02:05:38.610 --> 02:05:41.670
because we haven't agreed at the outset

02:05:41.670 --> 02:05:43.050
what a good outcome would look like.

02:05:43.050 --> 02:05:45.240
And that can not always
look like the thing

02:05:45.240 --> 02:05:46.290
that someone's asking for.

02:05:46.290 --> 02:05:49.170
That's just the same as in a trust.

02:05:49.170 --> 02:05:51.690
But I think there's quite a strong sense

02:05:51.690 --> 02:05:53.130
that people just avoid

02:05:53.130 --> 02:05:55.350
having some of those more
challenging conversations,

02:05:55.350 --> 02:05:58.560
which I think is all linked to

02:05:58.560 --> 02:06:00.450
we're not really giving
feedback in the moment,

02:06:00.450 --> 02:06:02.220
we're not having the regular conversation,

02:06:02.220 --> 02:06:03.960
and, in some instances,

02:06:03.960 --> 02:06:06.060
we're not even having the appraisal.

02:06:06.060 --> 02:06:08.027
So, I think the training was,

02:06:08.027 --> 02:06:08.860
I don't know where I'm looking,

02:06:08.860 --> 02:06:09.810
but the training that was developed

02:06:09.810 --> 02:06:12.993
was focused on trying to address that,

02:06:14.730 --> 02:06:16.650
but it's pushing against
a culture, I think,

02:06:16.650 --> 02:06:20.880
which is more valuing of expertise

02:06:20.880 --> 02:06:23.670
than it is of general management.

02:06:23.670 --> 02:06:27.783
- Okay. Anything else you'd
like to see happen, Tom?

02:06:29.269 --> 02:06:33.540
- I think just keep pushing
on the leadership behaviours

02:06:33.540 --> 02:06:34.980
and our expectations,

02:06:34.980 --> 02:06:37.260
and I think part of that is also induction

02:06:37.260 --> 02:06:39.570
when people join our organisation,

02:06:39.570 --> 02:06:42.690
not seeing that being sort
of laid out clearly to people

02:06:42.690 --> 02:06:44.340
when they come into the organisation,

02:06:44.340 --> 02:06:46.890
and therefore it's more difficult

02:06:46.890 --> 02:06:49.980
to then start setting the
bar around what we think,

02:06:49.980 --> 02:06:51.663
well, how we see that working.

02:06:52.641 --> 02:06:54.930
- Okay, I think we've got an opportunity

02:06:54.930 --> 02:06:56.070
with the integration.

02:06:56.070 --> 02:06:57.330
- Absolutely.

02:06:57.330 --> 02:06:59.160
I think just another thing to add,

02:06:59.160 --> 02:07:01.140
we discussed this Tom when we met,

02:07:01.140 --> 02:07:03.420
that the burden on guardians themselves

02:07:03.420 --> 02:07:05.130
is actually really quite significant.

02:07:05.130 --> 02:07:08.250
And when, you know that's
fine in a stable organisation

02:07:08.250 --> 02:07:09.930
that's doing really well.

02:07:09.930 --> 02:07:12.930
We've got an exceptionally
difficult set of circumstances.

02:07:12.930 --> 02:07:13.830
So we have to think about

02:07:13.830 --> 02:07:15.450
how we look after those individuals

02:07:15.450 --> 02:07:16.590
'cause they are the sponge

02:07:16.590 --> 02:07:20.100
for an awful lot of grief
and anxiety and anger,

02:07:20.100 --> 02:07:22.950
and they're not jobs
you can do for very long

02:07:22.950 --> 02:07:25.560
without having a kind of an impact on you.

02:07:25.560 --> 02:07:28.660
So I've, you know, got
to think about that.

02:07:28.660 --> 02:07:29.660
- Jane.

02:07:29.660 --> 02:07:31.290
- Just a couple of points,

02:07:31.290 --> 02:07:34.230
and echoing a lot of what,
you know, Andrew has said

02:07:34.230 --> 02:07:36.840
about our regular interactions
with the guardians.

02:07:36.840 --> 02:07:39.690
I mean, one thing that PRC
has looked at consistently

02:07:39.690 --> 02:07:43.260
over the last year or so
and I'm sure before that,

02:07:43.260 --> 02:07:46.440
is span of control and how
many managers you've got

02:07:46.440 --> 02:07:48.555
and you do, I think it's quite common

02:07:48.555 --> 02:07:49.890
to a lot of healthcare systems,

02:07:49.890 --> 02:07:52.530
you kind of end up putting
people in charge of other people

02:07:52.530 --> 02:07:54.900
because they're experts or senior

02:07:54.900 --> 02:07:55.733
but, actually, you know,

02:07:55.733 --> 02:07:58.800
they don't need to
necessarily be managers,

02:07:58.800 --> 02:08:00.720
and I think we have a very high proportion

02:08:00.720 --> 02:08:04.933
of line managers to staff in NHS England.

02:08:04.933 --> 02:08:07.710
I think there's a chance
in the reorganisation

02:08:07.710 --> 02:08:09.210
to correct some of that,

02:08:09.210 --> 02:08:11.793
you know, to get people in the right jobs.

02:08:13.020 --> 02:08:14.160
So that's one thing.

02:08:14.160 --> 02:08:16.970
And just to, you know,
reinforce what I said earlier

02:08:16.970 --> 02:08:18.900
in the brief committee report,

02:08:18.900 --> 02:08:19.860
there is an attempt

02:08:19.860 --> 02:08:24.210
in some of the leadership
performance training

02:08:24.210 --> 02:08:27.843
as it was designed before the
announcement of the merger,

02:08:28.770 --> 02:08:30.600
some of that is being pivoted

02:08:30.600 --> 02:08:33.510
to try to address the
particular stresses and strains

02:08:33.510 --> 02:08:35.337
coming out of this process.

02:08:35.337 --> 02:08:37.920
And so there is a
recognition that, you know,

02:08:37.920 --> 02:08:39.870
you can't just carry on business as usual

02:08:39.870 --> 02:08:43.110
but trying to look at
giving people the skills

02:08:43.110 --> 02:08:47.010
to manage people through a
period of particular stress,

02:08:47.010 --> 02:08:48.867
you know, supporting the guardians.

02:08:50.369 --> 02:08:51.756
- Is it?

02:08:51.756 --> 02:08:52.589
- [Jeremy] Just very quickly,

02:08:52.589 --> 02:08:54.660
this isn't the only area within HR

02:08:54.660 --> 02:08:56.280
that we picked up from an
audit committee point of view

02:08:56.280 --> 02:08:57.700
where the processes-

02:08:57.700 --> 02:08:58.533
- [Penny] Yeah.

02:08:58.533 --> 02:08:59.366
- are nonexistent.

02:08:59.366 --> 02:09:00.360
So the leavers process was...

02:09:00.360 --> 02:09:03.900
So I just wonder whether
there's a bigger cultural reset

02:09:03.900 --> 02:09:04.733
to pick up

02:09:04.733 --> 02:09:06.510
and whether there's a
role for internal audit

02:09:06.510 --> 02:09:07.500
as part of that

02:09:07.500 --> 02:09:09.420
in checking that we're
actually delivering on it

02:09:09.420 --> 02:09:12.030
'cause this isn't the only
space where we're weak.

02:09:12.030 --> 02:09:13.290
- Absolutely, and we need to link that

02:09:13.290 --> 02:09:16.680
into the integration work, okay.

02:09:16.680 --> 02:09:19.449
So thank you, Tom and to colleagues

02:09:19.449 --> 02:09:23.223
for doing good work in
difficult circumstances.

02:09:24.060 --> 02:09:25.320
Bethany, welcome.

02:09:25.320 --> 02:09:26.700
- Thank you.

02:09:26.700 --> 02:09:28.620
So I'll just give a
few highlights as well.

02:09:28.620 --> 02:09:30.510
So, first of all, to give an update

02:09:30.510 --> 02:09:32.640
following the publication
of the 10 Year Plan

02:09:32.640 --> 02:09:34.290
and the Dash Review.

02:09:34.290 --> 02:09:35.670
So we are meeting regularly

02:09:35.670 --> 02:09:37.800
with the Department of
Health and Social Care

02:09:37.800 --> 02:09:39.540
at both the senior and working level

02:09:39.540 --> 02:09:42.030
to begin the planning
and transition activity

02:09:42.030 --> 02:09:44.430
for National Guardian's Office functions.

02:09:44.430 --> 02:09:46.200
Some immediate issues that we've discussed

02:09:46.200 --> 02:09:50.010
include staffing, communications,
high-level timelines,

02:09:50.010 --> 02:09:52.173
and any legal or contractual issues

02:09:52.173 --> 02:09:54.570
that are connected to the functions.

02:09:54.570 --> 02:09:56.160
And of course focusing on ensuring

02:09:56.160 --> 02:10:00.240
that there is continuation of
guardians within NHS trusts.

02:10:00.240 --> 02:10:03.600
Detail planning is at very
early stage at the moment,

02:10:03.600 --> 02:10:06.540
but we do aim to have
substantially delivered

02:10:06.540 --> 02:10:08.550
the National Guardian's Office shut down

02:10:08.550 --> 02:10:11.733
by Quarter 1 2026/27.

02:10:12.690 --> 02:10:15.510
So some other key
highlights in the report.

02:10:15.510 --> 02:10:18.750
We now have more than 1,400
guardians trained and registered

02:10:18.750 --> 02:10:20.400
and on our published directory

02:10:20.400 --> 02:10:22.230
from a variety of organisations,

02:10:22.230 --> 02:10:24.120
as you can see in the report,

02:10:24.120 --> 02:10:25.260
and we continue to collect

02:10:25.260 --> 02:10:28.170
the quarterly data from guardians.

02:10:28.170 --> 02:10:33.170
The most reported theme
through guardians in 23/24

02:10:33.450 --> 02:10:34.440
were cases that involved

02:10:34.440 --> 02:10:37.530
an element of inappropriate
behaviours and attitudes,

02:10:37.530 --> 02:10:39.060
closely followed by cases

02:10:39.060 --> 02:10:42.033
that involved an element of
worker safety or wellbeing.

02:10:43.080 --> 02:10:44.670
But out of all those workers

02:10:44.670 --> 02:10:46.200
that did speak up through guardians,

02:10:46.200 --> 02:10:48.870
just short of 80% said that
they would speak up again,

02:10:48.870 --> 02:10:50.403
which is really encouraging.

02:10:51.750 --> 02:10:54.030
The biggest gap that we've
noticed in the staff survey

02:10:54.030 --> 02:10:56.850
and that kind of links into
some of what Tom's been saying,

02:10:56.850 --> 02:10:58.290
is actually that there's disparity

02:10:58.290 --> 02:11:02.520
between confidence in workers
feeling safe to raise concerns

02:11:02.520 --> 02:11:05.100
and confidence that actions will be taken.

02:11:05.100 --> 02:11:07.560
So, again, it's working with
leaders and organisations

02:11:07.560 --> 02:11:09.480
to make sure that they're
listening properly

02:11:09.480 --> 02:11:11.490
and they are taking the
appropriate actions and follow up

02:11:11.490 --> 02:11:13.623
to close that loop and that cycle.

02:11:15.780 --> 02:11:18.870
In May we published our Speak Up review

02:11:18.870 --> 02:11:20.940
into the experience of
overseas-trained workers.

02:11:20.940 --> 02:11:22.890
That had a number of recommendations,

02:11:22.890 --> 02:11:24.450
and a steering group has been formed

02:11:24.450 --> 02:11:28.383
to see through those recommendations
and keep them on track.

02:11:29.580 --> 02:11:31.350
We continue to work with stakeholders.

02:11:31.350 --> 02:11:35.040
A particular area has been
with NHS England and CQC,

02:11:35.040 --> 02:11:36.720
looking how to further embed guardians

02:11:36.720 --> 02:11:39.000
into ICBs, more standardised,

02:11:39.000 --> 02:11:43.050
and also into primary
care services as well.

02:11:43.050 --> 02:11:44.820
With regards to our guardian support,

02:11:44.820 --> 02:11:46.800
we continue to support our guardians,

02:11:46.800 --> 02:11:47.633
especially over the coming months

02:11:47.633 --> 02:11:49.820
through the transition period.

02:11:49.820 --> 02:11:53.883
So we continue with their
annual refresher training, CPD,

02:11:54.749 --> 02:11:56.340
and look at their
compliance against training

02:11:56.340 --> 02:11:57.603
et cetera as well.

02:11:59.700 --> 02:12:00.660
You can read in the report

02:12:00.660 --> 02:12:03.030
the engagements and
communications that have gone on

02:12:03.030 --> 02:12:04.950
over the last year.

02:12:04.950 --> 02:12:06.870
And I think, just moving on to next steps,

02:12:06.870 --> 02:12:09.300
although we are moving
into the transition period,

02:12:09.300 --> 02:12:10.590
we have got a number of work streams

02:12:10.590 --> 02:12:12.030
that we will be continuing with

02:12:12.030 --> 02:12:13.650
'cause they're really
important to be able to support

02:12:13.650 --> 02:12:16.473
the guardians, leaders, and organisations.

02:12:18.450 --> 02:12:21.570
And we are hoping to do
a short Speak Up review

02:12:21.570 --> 02:12:23.340
into the experience of temporary workers,

02:12:23.340 --> 02:12:25.950
which should hopefully
start in the summer.

02:12:25.950 --> 02:12:28.293
- Okay, thanks Bethany. Any questions?

02:12:30.180 --> 02:12:32.520
Okay, so one thing I would say,

02:12:32.520 --> 02:12:35.970
which, also talking
about this with Duncan,

02:12:35.970 --> 02:12:38.730
at the chief nurse conference
earlier in the week.

02:12:38.730 --> 02:12:41.160
So quite a few things in the report I did,

02:12:41.160 --> 02:12:42.330
and the recommendations

02:12:42.330 --> 02:12:45.150
about bringing functions
back in house as it were,

02:12:45.150 --> 02:12:46.230
of which this is one,

02:12:46.230 --> 02:12:49.140
recognising the work that NGO's
done over the last 10 years

02:12:49.140 --> 02:12:51.600
to really create what is now

02:12:51.600 --> 02:12:54.780
a significant establishment
of freedom to speak up,

02:12:54.780 --> 02:12:56.717
guardians across the whole of the NHS

02:12:56.717 --> 02:12:58.770
and in NHS England.

02:12:58.770 --> 02:13:00.810
But, as we were talking about,

02:13:00.810 --> 02:13:03.120
crucial thing is if we bring the functions

02:13:03.120 --> 02:13:05.375
back in house more, as
we're doing with this,

02:13:05.375 --> 02:13:07.080
we've then got to deliver against them.

02:13:07.080 --> 02:13:09.540
So, it's a bit of, you know,

02:13:09.540 --> 02:13:11.550
we have to say thank you very
much for all of your work,

02:13:11.550 --> 02:13:13.620
but it's a big responsibility,

02:13:13.620 --> 02:13:15.630
which, to link into the
previous agenda item,

02:13:15.630 --> 02:13:17.453
means we've really got to step up

02:13:17.453 --> 02:13:19.260
our own internal processes

02:13:19.260 --> 02:13:21.420
whether it's in people management

02:13:21.420 --> 02:13:23.493
or it's in wider quality management.

02:13:24.600 --> 02:13:29.340
- I think being clear about the role

02:13:29.340 --> 02:13:32.700
of Freedom to Speak Up within
NHS England is important

02:13:32.700 --> 02:13:36.903
because whilst there's an
internal piece of work to do,

02:13:38.340 --> 02:13:41.430
we did embark on a bit of work last year

02:13:41.430 --> 02:13:43.557
around what happens in trusts

02:13:43.557 --> 02:13:47.040
and where do they go
when there's an issue.

02:13:47.040 --> 02:13:52.040
And we were doing some work
with CQC and with the NGO,

02:13:52.977 --> 02:13:56.033
and I think we need to kind of
revisit that and clarify it,

02:13:56.033 --> 02:13:56.866
- [Penny] Yep.

02:13:56.866 --> 02:13:59.430
- because there is, out in the field,

02:13:59.430 --> 02:14:03.420
where we've had instances
where people have raised issues

02:14:03.420 --> 02:14:05.700
and it may concern, I
don't know, a board member

02:14:05.700 --> 02:14:08.610
or someone senior in the organisation,

02:14:08.610 --> 02:14:10.680
where issues haven't been addressed,

02:14:10.680 --> 02:14:14.010
people feel that they've
got nowhere else to go,

02:14:14.010 --> 02:14:16.500
and we just need to kind of be clear

02:14:16.500 --> 02:14:18.120
about those routes

02:14:18.120 --> 02:14:18.953
- [Penny] Exactly,

02:14:18.953 --> 02:14:21.450
- where people can, you
know, speak up safely.

02:14:21.450 --> 02:14:23.640
And that's a bit of unfinished work

02:14:23.640 --> 02:14:26.340
that we need to kind of cap off.

02:14:26.340 --> 02:14:27.173
- [Bethany] Yeah, absolutely.

02:14:27.173 --> 02:14:29.310
And just to say that that has come up

02:14:29.310 --> 02:14:30.480
with some guardians recently

02:14:30.480 --> 02:14:33.030
since the announcements
and the publications

02:14:33.030 --> 02:14:35.130
to have that clarity on
what the escalation routes

02:14:35.130 --> 02:14:36.810
would be moving forward, yes.

02:14:36.810 --> 02:14:37.643
- [Penny] Yeah.

02:14:37.643 --> 02:14:39.600
We did address some of
that anyway, but yeah.

02:14:39.600 --> 02:14:42.000
we'll pick all of that up. Okay.

02:14:42.000 --> 02:14:45.730
- Just my experience of when
I was a regional director

02:14:46.659 --> 02:14:48.930
and just thinking about with Tom actually,

02:14:48.930 --> 02:14:49.920
but we've got,

02:14:49.920 --> 02:14:54.180
as well as having guardians
embedded in our directorate,

02:14:54.180 --> 02:14:55.470
we always also had a lead.

02:14:55.470 --> 02:15:00.000
So we did actually manage
quite a lot of conversations,

02:15:00.000 --> 02:15:02.490
particularly the more senior ones,

02:15:02.490 --> 02:15:03.473
where there would be issues.

02:15:03.473 --> 02:15:06.840
And the key thing that
Sarah-Jane and Tom was describing

02:15:06.840 --> 02:15:08.340
was about active listening

02:15:08.340 --> 02:15:09.543
and actually getting to a place of,

02:15:09.543 --> 02:15:11.697
well what does a good outcome look like?

02:15:11.697 --> 02:15:14.760
And I think we do mitigate
quite a lot and manage that

02:15:14.760 --> 02:15:17.040
and really help people reach a resolution

02:15:17.040 --> 02:15:18.810
before it escalates further.

02:15:18.810 --> 02:15:20.220
It's not formalised.

02:15:20.220 --> 02:15:21.053
- [Andrew] Yeah.

02:15:21.053 --> 02:15:21.886
- So I agree.

02:15:21.886 --> 02:15:23.760
In the reorganisation,

02:15:23.760 --> 02:15:26.220
I think we need to make
sure that that's recognised.

02:15:26.220 --> 02:15:29.460
There's a lot happens in
regions that are not formalised.

02:15:29.460 --> 02:15:30.780
So I think we need to make sure

02:15:30.780 --> 02:15:33.000
through the operating
model, we pick that up.

02:15:33.000 --> 02:15:34.170
- Yeah. We'll definitely pick that up.

02:15:34.170 --> 02:15:36.300
So thank you. Thanks for bringing that.

02:15:36.300 --> 02:15:39.480
Okay, we've got one item
just for information

02:15:39.480 --> 02:15:41.580
which is the feedback
from the consultation

02:15:41.580 --> 02:15:44.310
around the Oversight Framework.

02:15:44.310 --> 02:15:46.710
So that's just to be read.

02:15:46.710 --> 02:15:49.160
We don't need to discuss
that, do we, Glen, or...

02:15:50.340 --> 02:15:51.749
- Just a moment on it, if...

02:15:52.600 --> 02:15:53.709
- Yes, oh, okay. Alright, good.

02:15:54.749 --> 02:15:59.749
So this report's back on the
feedback from the consultation

02:16:00.300 --> 02:16:04.410
including moving to a more
focused set of indicators.

02:16:04.410 --> 02:16:08.430
So we've we've referenced
the Oversight Framework

02:16:08.430 --> 02:16:09.540
a few times today,

02:16:09.540 --> 02:16:11.070
but one thing I just wanted to emphasise

02:16:11.070 --> 02:16:13.500
that as we move to a more focused set,

02:16:13.500 --> 02:16:16.020
we are still expecting
boards, including this board,

02:16:16.020 --> 02:16:18.870
to look at a wider range of indicators.

02:16:18.870 --> 02:16:20.190
But I believe in the adage

02:16:20.190 --> 02:16:22.620
that if you prioritise everything,
you prioritise nothing.

02:16:22.620 --> 02:16:26.400
So what we've done here is
identified these measures,

02:16:26.400 --> 02:16:29.520
and each year we'll come back to look

02:16:29.520 --> 02:16:32.520
to see whether we change
those based on priorities

02:16:32.520 --> 02:16:35.580
but also based on the
10 Year Plan priorities

02:16:35.580 --> 02:16:38.700
because it's clear that there
are a lot of areas in the NHS,

02:16:38.700 --> 02:16:41.250
particularly those that we prioritise,

02:16:41.250 --> 02:16:43.620
that haven't got sufficiently
robust indicators.

02:16:43.620 --> 02:16:45.270
So we're working on those.

02:16:45.270 --> 02:16:49.260
But this Oversight
Framework will be dynamic.

02:16:49.260 --> 02:16:51.240
It's rules based,

02:16:51.240 --> 02:16:55.770
and we will link freedoms
and incentives to it

02:16:55.770 --> 02:16:59.430
but also link it to the improvement offer

02:16:59.430 --> 02:17:00.630
that will be across the board

02:17:00.630 --> 02:17:04.170
including into intervention
for individual organisations.

02:17:04.170 --> 02:17:09.030
We've already shared provisional Q1 data

02:17:09.030 --> 02:17:13.140
with colleagues in the system.

02:17:13.140 --> 02:17:17.670
We will move to the formal
publication of that in August,

02:17:17.670 --> 02:17:21.720
link to public facing league tables.

02:17:21.720 --> 02:17:23.670
And I think the response
has been really positive

02:17:23.670 --> 02:17:25.440
as Jim referenced earlier.

02:17:25.440 --> 02:17:28.230
- Okay, good, but I think we
do want to keep revisiting,

02:17:28.230 --> 02:17:30.510
have we got the right data in there,

02:17:30.510 --> 02:17:33.930
which fits in with the
earlier conversation.

02:17:33.930 --> 02:17:38.370
Okay. Does anyone have any other business?

02:17:38.370 --> 02:17:40.293
Okay, just one from me,

02:17:41.220 --> 02:17:45.147
which is that we are going to
be changing some of our NEDs

02:17:45.147 --> 02:17:48.780
and some of our board committees
as we move into the autumn.

02:17:48.780 --> 02:17:50.873
So we will share more details on that

02:17:50.873 --> 02:17:52.860
when we get to that stage.

02:17:52.860 --> 02:17:55.460
But I would say a very big thank you

02:17:55.460 --> 02:17:58.440
to all of my NED colleagues
for sticking with us

02:17:58.440 --> 02:18:00.660
as we've been through
an awful lot of change

02:18:00.660 --> 02:18:02.250
over the last few months,

02:18:02.250 --> 02:18:06.270
and it's much appreciated that
people have largely remained

02:18:06.270 --> 02:18:07.560
very committed to the role,

02:18:07.560 --> 02:18:10.248
well not largely, 100%
very committed to the role,

02:18:11.487 --> 02:18:14.700
and have provided some
fantastic input, support,

02:18:14.700 --> 02:18:18.060
and good challenge to
our executive colleagues.

02:18:18.060 --> 02:18:19.860
So thank you to our non-execs

02:18:19.860 --> 02:18:22.800
and very many thanks to
our executive colleagues

02:18:22.800 --> 02:18:24.120
for all of the work here

02:18:24.120 --> 02:18:25.920
and, most importantly, to all of the staff

02:18:25.920 --> 02:18:27.453
working out in the service.

02:18:28.980 --> 02:18:30.600
Next meeting is in September.

02:18:30.600 --> 02:18:31.860
Can't remember the exact date,

02:18:31.860 --> 02:18:34.170
but look forward to seeing everybody then

02:18:34.170 --> 02:18:38.670
and hopefully some people
manage some of a break

02:18:38.670 --> 02:18:40.245
between now and then.

02:18:40.245 --> 02:18:41.078
So thank you,

